      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>Seasonable headgear is even more important than the rest of the toilette, and the fashions in this are soonest to declare themselves. A Tam o' Shanter has a soft glacier•blue zibeline crown poised on a bandeau of old lace, which widens out at the side into a bow, with which is mingled a sable head and tail. A flat plateau is made of the small breast feathers of the pheasant, with a wide filling of brown and white spotted foulard resting on the hair in front, a simple yet most effective toque. Marquise hat of white felt was trimmed with a deep band round the crown, and choux at the side of rich purple velvet, whilst a shaded • aigrette rises from a mauve pompon above. A :person was wearing one of the delightful new , fllerirres of mouse-brown velvet, with a strip of sable down the front, and round the back, and an inner ruffle of the velvet on the inside. The hat, of white felt, is ringed with circles of brown velvet, with a puffing of velvet underneath the brim, and a long pheasant's tail sweeping round to the back.</p>
        <p>The reign of the blouse is by no means over; in fact, this long-suffering and oft-misunderstood garment seems to have taken on a new lease of 4ife for the winter. For wearing underneath heavy coats the blouse has not its equal for com- fort, and if the coat is removed indoors, it is quite as nice-looking as anything can be. Some dress- makers are sending home with avow complete costume a soft silk or velvet blouse which tones with the material, so that it can be word with the skirt on occasions when the bodice proper is not wanted, and yet look right with it. The days when blouse and skirt presented a violent contrast to each other are past, except with those who are determinedly wedded to this form of toilette. Several of the latest velvet blouses have narrow little fluted ba.sques, which give them quite a jaunty look, and present the blouse under a new and more dignified aspect. Very charming are deep cream-tinted satin or panne blouses, trimmed with bands of sable, and encrusted with ivory lace. These are in great vogue for smart lun- cheons, and one such was further embellished with antique silver buttons, these being attached to a wide centre box pleat down the front, bordered with sal)te tails on either side, and two cravat ends of point lace at the throat were linked together with silver chains end heavy-liesdal pins.</p>
        <p>4'.,111 kinds are to be irrnienßely fashion-</p>
        <p>p, but what is not so pleasing is that passemen- terie also is to he used on cloth dressy—there -seems to be something out of place and tawdry in the conjunction. A jour inseitions will also be almost as much used as on summer gowns for more elaborate dresses, and their construction is of courzs, on a more solid scale. Gold and oxydised galons, in narrow widths, are well worn, and look at their best on velvet gowns, backed or empha- sised by bkok braiding; minute gold buckles, on skirt and bodice alike, as connecting links be- tween rows of braid, are a great feature in the season's trimmings, and any number are employed on one gown. Velvet covered buttons are an old fashion revived, and are to be used on cloth gowns and coats; they sre made in large sizes. a</p>
        <p>High collars are not in much favour. Perhaps it is at last beginning to dawn upon the mind of the sniartly-dressed that our winters are not so severe as to need this rampart of protection; that fur round the neck tends to the impoverishment of the tissues, and impairs the roundness and beauty of the shape; that a high collar has occasionally to be !oft off in favour of a turned-down, and that the change induces an attack of cold; and that °Vett the most sumptuous collar or ruffle cannot with impunity he permitted to blur the outline of shoulder. Few things in women's dress, except crinoline and the chignon, have been so inartistic as the indiscriminate muffling of the neck that has prevailed for so long. Evening cloaks have hie collars, and very justifiably, for the cold air encountered after leaving overheated rooms must be guarded against as much as possible. But with outdoor costumes for walking and driving it is a very different matter. Large muffs are to be the order of the day, and collarettes of fur or velvet or satin are made in sets with muffs to match. Your loving Jzirrtis.</p>
        <p>The Local Gavernromt Board has intimated to the Liverpool Corporation that they consent to Garston being incorporated with Liverpool. Flaculd a privat Bill to incorporate Garstcn pass next maim the popula- tion of Liverpool will be over 7CO3CCO. Emily Ann Asquith, aged 23; daughter of John Aequith, market gardener. VVrenthorpe, Wakefield. met with a frightful cleath on Tuesday. She basl been suffer- ing from religicus mania, and deliberately ee t fire to her clothing. Efer screams attracted the attention of other members of the family, and the flames were ex- tinguished, but she died shortly afterwards, after euffering intense agony.</p>
