      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAI.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAI.</p>
        <p>Hallowe'en, which falls on the last day of October,and is more particularly an old Scottish festival, used to be regularly observe at Balmoral Zastle, with bonfires and torchlight processions. The sentiments andcusteens are enshrined in Burn's poem, but like so many of our anzient customs, the observance of Hallowe'en,bas been gradually falling into neglect. With fireside games, and the cracking of nuts. it provided opportunity for a good deal of universal mirth, but in these prosaic times, witches sod fairies have gone out of date, and with them much of the respect for old legends and ancient institutions. There will be a notable gathering of the Royal Family during the stay of the King and Queen at Sanaringham. The Duke and Duchess of Cornwall will have a great deal to talk about after so much travel and sightseeing, and Prince and Princess Charles of Denmark, who have been away for six or seven months, are returning to take up their residence at Appleton Houae until next spring. The Duke and Duchess of Fife are also to pay a visit to the King and Queen, and there will thus be a united family gathering at Sandringham, where His Majesty will celebrate his birthday on the 9th of , November. Holkham Hall, 'et Norfolk, where the Duke and Duchess of Cornwall are to be entertained by the Earl I of Leicester next December, is one of the most beautiful and stately mansions in England. The Earl of Leicester is close upon eighty years ot age, and through his many children, of whom there have been eighteen, he is closely connected with a large number of families in the British peerage. His eldest sou, Viscount Coke, who was born in 1848, was one of tbe friends of the King's youtb, and on more than one occasion His Majesty, as Prince of Wales, was a visitor at Holkham Hall. All the world loves a love-match, whether in th palace or the cottage, and Lady Frances Cecil's inter- ference with her 1301:1's marriage with Miss Jessie Bain, of Belfast, has caused much sympathy to be felt with Ike young people. Lieutenant Cecil, who is a grandson of the third Marquis of Exeter inherits a large fortune, under the will of the late Sir W Cunhffe Brooks, but, being under age, the Court of Chancery was appealed to, and issued an injunction warning all and everyone against aiding his matrimonial projects. But our Marriage Laws are more elastic than is the case in France and some other countries, and even if Mr Cecil had found any difficulty in proceeding with his marri- age in Scotland, there are many Registrars in England, who it is believed, would not have felt compelled to observe the Lord Chancellor's solemn injunction. The Cecile of Exeter, and the Cecile of Salisbury are alike descended from the celebrated Lord High Treasurer whom Queen Elizabeth created Lord Burghley. Lord Burghley was a self-made man, but he was very suc- cessful at Court, and in 1605 James I. made earls of his , two sons, the elder being created Lord Exeter, and Robert the your ger Lord Salisbury. The Cecile of Exe- ter are the senior branch of the family, but the Cecile of Salisbury take precedence, as the Premier's marquisate dates from the sear 1789, while Lord Exeter's was only conferred in 1801. The Archduchess Elizabeth, the only granddaughter of the Empercr of Austria, could easily have made a brilliant alliance, with one of the reigning houses of Europe, but like her mother the Countess Longay, the Crown Princess Stephanie, she will marry the man of her choice, namely Prince Otto of Windischgraetz. The Prince has neitaer fortune or expectations, but the mar- riage which will be celebrated in January, will be re- garded as an equal union, and it has the full consent and approval ot the aged Emperor. The Archduchess, who is in her nineteenth year, is tall and fair, and is one of the most charming, as she will be also, one af the richest Princesses in Europe. In these busy days, a collection of good short stories, such as we have iu Mr. S R Crockett's prettily bound "Love Idylls " (John Murray), is very acceptable. Despite all that has been and is said against " scrappy " reading those among us—and there are many—whose days from start to finish are crowded with physical or mental labour, know well the value of a brief half hour devoted to the perusal of a bright intei esting story, which we can " finish right off." In the collection referred to, there are touches of a pretty sentiment everywhere, and, more especially with "Love Among the Beach Leaves," and " Billiaim," none can fail to be charmed. Flannel is being extensively utilised just now, not only for shirts and blouses, but for skirts also. And certainly the latest samples of this fabric amply justify its elevation in the' modiste world. In a very fine make cream flannel is being employed fcr tea gowns, also dressy afternoon toilettes, and the effect leaves nothing to be desired. There is but one drawback attached to this delicate material—it quickly soils— thereby placing iteelf beyond the means ot the merely , moderately endowed. In shirts and Wages built of flannel silk strappings are the most approved form of trimmine. To prove successful however, they need to be very carefully applied. Brocade promises to be a vary favourite material for , evening wear this coming winter, among quite young ' matrons as well as dowagers. The patterns, which in beauty surpass anything' we have seen before, strike one as rather too large, but in these days, tall fine women abound, and such will doubtless carry the designs well. The colours in these brocades are beauti- ful beyond compare, more especially the pastel tones, which happily show no sign of disappearing from our midst. Tucking has become a somewhat too popular form of trimming, for exclusive dressers, eo shirring—a species of fine stitchery, that has a quilted effect—is taking its place among reslly first class modistes. Con- trary to expectation beads and seemins are not to be excluded from the list ot trimmings available this winter, but unquestionably their day is over. Lace promises to again enjoy a wide vogue, especially Irish lace, which is being employed to trim all the smartest frocks, rose point. and Irish crochet, in black, white, or unbleached colours, are flat favourites. All the old-world fur tippete,so much in evidence, show touches of lace at the throat, and down the front, some indeed boasting long ends of the same. The union of fur and lace is charming, as we, by now, well know. Usually with the departure of the warm fine weather comes a re-appearance of veils, but at present this item of feminine attire is conspicuous by its absence. The</p>
        <p>one-time excuse, that a veil was necessary to kem the hair tidy, can no longer he employed. To such perfec- tion have hair nets been brought, that it is almost im- possible to deteat them, and they keep the hair much more neat and orderly than a veil could possibly do. With very few exceptions, we should do well to eschew veils altogether, and on no account should the spotted variety ever bc indulged in. As a rule housewives are not very ready to admit that they have anything of importance to learn.-'in the management of a home, but at the school recently started, by the Chelsea Polytechnic, for this purpose, there is no lack of pupils. Mostly they are young women who having followed a business calling, know very little about domesticity but many it seems, have homes of their own, and are anxious to obtai a a better know- ledge of their duties. A thorough course of training is provided, from the washing of a handkerchief to the cooking of a dinner, or the nursing of a child, and judging by the success of this experiment, there is ample E cope for many more schools of the kind.</p>
        <p>ALIDGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MONDAY.— Before Muer' J. Ockleston (In the chair) and J</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions. MONDAY.— Before Muer' J. Ockleston (In the chair) and J</p>
        <p>Pritchard.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED MISCONDUCT DT A PUBLICAN.—Thomas White Clarendon Hotel, Church-street. Runcorn, was summoned for permitting drunkenness to take pliwe en licensed prem- ises on the 2nd October.—Mr A F Fletcher, Horthwich ap- peared to prosecute on behalf of the pollee and Mr F Ii Smith, barrister, Liverpool,lnstructed by Mr F A Lake repre- sented the defendant.—Previous to the opening of the case Mr. Fletcher said they were short of one witness who had been duly summoned. The police considered his evidence im- portant in order to silt the matter thoroughly so he applied for an adjournment.—The witness Ratcliffe was the man alleged to have been drunk when be was supplied by the defendant with beer. He was a seafaring man and he would not be back for a fortnight. He aeked that the case be adjourned. —Mr Smith said he Was opposed to the adjournment simply because the man had been tried on the charge of being drunk and had pleaded guilty—Under those circum- stances whatever was the value of the witness's evidence was shown by the fact of his conviction. If the police would pay the rests of the adjournment he would not oppose the appli- oation.—Mr Fletcher said the police were not willing to pay the costs.—The bench decided to adjourn the case on the •:nderstanding that the police paid the costs but as this was refused the case went on Mr Fletcher said that on the date in question the man corn- menoed drinking at 10 30 in the morning and after drinking at several houses was supplied by the defendant when in a ' state of drunkenness.—P C Wilding said he was in Church- Street at 15 in the afternoon when a girl named Smythm brought him a message in consequence of which he went to Mrs Smytiam's and found the man Ratcliffe in Regent-street so drunk that he bad to take him into eustody•—lthei Smythm, aged ten, maid she lived with her mother who kept the "Cornwall and Devonshire?' On the 2nd October she came from school and saw Ratcliffe and another man In the bar. From what her mother told her she went for a police- man.—Martha Smythm, licensee of the "Cornwall and Devonstire " said it was something to one o'cle ck when her girl came home. Retell Ne and Dunbavand were in the house. They asked for a drink but she replied she would not serve them drunk or sober.—By the Bench : She thought they were too drunk to be served.—Cross-examined : The reason she did not serve him was because he was a nasty tampered man.— Richard Duni:l'mnd said that heaud Ratcliffe went to several houses and had drinks. They went to Whites about 12.6 and had some beer in the house, He was sober then.—Peter Leathwood spoke to the man being drunk at 1.30 in Regent- street.—Sergeant Gill said the man was drunk when brought to the Station.—Superintendent Bunion said be went to Mr. White's in consequence of what be heard, and asked him what he had served Ratcliffe with. The defendant replied that his wife had served him about 12 o'clock with a glass of beer. He was very sorry if there had been any thing wrong for it had not teen done with any bad Inten- tion. The defendant had been in the trade 15 years and there was nothing hitherto against him.---Mr Smith, for the defence contended that though the man was druuk at 1.45 there was no evidence to show that responsiblity attached to the defendant. Ratcliffe was there at noon and he might have had some beer elsewhere.—Thomas White, licensee, said he was standing outeide his house when be saw Rat- cliffe come in. He was quite sober and tit to be served.— Annie White, wife of,last witness said she served Ratcliffe , who was quite fit to be served. She had had 15 years' ex- perience. She had rational conversation with him.—Fdward Green swore he saw no signs of drink on Ratcliffe. Ile saw him going out of the house at 12.20. He could walk all right.—The Bench, after consultation, thought there was in- sufficient evidence and dismissed the case. ASSAULTING A FEMALE.—Thomaa Norman, was summoned by Mary Furnival for having assaulted her on the 12th October.—Complainant stated that she was standing by her door in Cambridge-row when defendant, who was drunk,ran at her without provocation and hit her in the stomach with his head. He also struck her in the face. He had apolcgised to her on Sunday but she wished to have Urn bound over —Defendant y leaded guilty and was accordingly bound over to keep the peace for six months and to pay the costs 9s 6d. ALLEGED DESERTION.—Joseph Brereton, Birkenhead, win summoned by his wife, Kate Brereton. for having deserted her.—Complainant stated that she and her husband lived in Birkenhead but he ill used her and would not give her any money. He peeked her oft to Runcorn and she bad been living with her aunt, Mrs. Lawson. Regent-street, sine° the 7th August. During that time he had only sent her 18s. She went to see him some time ago at Birkenhead and met him in a cart three miles from where he worked. As soon as she told him she wanted some money he jumped back into the cart and drove away. It was raining at the time and she had a baby ira her arms. She had to walk three miles before she could get any shelter.—Defendant denied deser- tion and said he would make a home for her if she would live with him again.—Complainant refused the offer and the bench decided to adjourn the case for a month to see if the parties could not come to an amicable arrangement.</p>
        <p>HOCKEY. --- Stockton Heath v. Chester.</p>
        <p>This match was played at Stockton Heath on Satur- day, and resulted in a win for Chester by three goals to nil. A match between the second teams resulted in Chester winning by five goals to four. . . . .</p>
        <p>, Latchforil v. Crossley's. These teams met at Latchford on m.turday. The weather WitS all that could be desired, and the ground was in good condition, considering the rain of the day previous. The borne team had the best of matters running out winners by Fix goals ta three. For Latch- ford, Banks and Hallows on the right wing and ileale. at centre half wore very conspicuous, whilst for the visitors the two full backs, and right outside were in good form. The g,oals scored for the home team were as follows : Albinson three, Grounds two, ffail)ws one.</p>
      </div>
