      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 25 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 25 1901</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>A QUEEN'S SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>• BY WALTER WOOD, Author of "Barrack and Battlefield," "Famous British Warships," "Through Battle to Pro- motion," "A Captain in the Fusiliers," "A Soldier of the Legion," dtc.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING .CHAPTERS: Mark Been, the son of a substantial farmer in Yorkshire, volunteers for service in the Yeomanry, about to be despatched to South Africa. After the farewells due to his father and his sweetheart, Lizzie Jackson, he duly embarks, the troop also including his friend Bngg. To his surprise he finds Lizzie Jackson at Capetown as an army nurse, she having left England on a mail steamer, which had pulsed the slow-going transport.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>CHAPTER.y.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER.y.</p>
        <p>TO TIM FRONT.</p>
        <p>_ - "it's all right," said Brigg, in a low voice.' "The Captain doesn't mean anything. It's dis- cipline, you know. He's coming this way—just ask him if you can slip up there for two minutes— to see your sister. That'll fetch him better than the other yarn." - -</p>
        <p>"Not I,'" answered Mark, sullenly; "I'll ask no favours."</p>
        <p>"Favours be hanged !" said Brigg. "It's your right. Now then, ask him; don't be a jackass. You know this isn't a country for ceremony and dignity just now." • •</p>
        <p>The Captain had come up. He was a pleasant enough officer, brisk and sharp, but always willing to stretch a point in favour of a man who did his work well. He had already formed a specially good opinion of Brigg and Benn, and was pleased with the promptitude with which Mark had returned to his place. It had not been so always with the Yeomanry. He had even known troopers reason about orders they had received. "What is it, my lad ?." he asked.</p>
        <p>Mark's determination to say nothing and ask nothing vanished in the presence of his genial superior. "I only wanted to see a girl/ I know, sir," he said. "She's on that steamer there—just come out from England." "Why, certainly," said the Captain. "Slip up. I thought as much—only, you know, you can't do these things without leave." There was still a suspicion of tolerance in the Captain's words—a tone which might have been adopted in addressing a very young soldier who had yet to learn the rudiments of his work; but of this, which would have stung Mark afresh at any other time, he took no notice. All he wished for was to join Lizzie, to ask her how and why she had come there, and generally to try to compre- hend the astonishing behaviour of old Jackson and his daughter:</p>
        <p>He stepped from his place for the second time, and walked towards the mail-boat. Lizzie, seeing him coming, managed to push to the gangway and get on to the wharf. The eyes of his company were upon the trooper, and those of the steamer's passengers were steadfastly regarding Lizzie. It was felt that here was some romance, and while the Yeomen made friendly grimaces, as of men kissing and embracing girls, the lady passengers whispered hurriedly amongst themselves, and opined that the soldier and the nurse were either newly married or about to be married. Instinc- tivly they knew that here were no brother and sister. Generally speaking, such relatives were not quite so anxious to see each other as these two were. "Now for it! Oh Christmas!" murmured a Yeoman who was very near the pair. "Go it, Yorky "—this was a nickname they had given to Mark on board the transport. "Weigh in with it. Don't I wish I was in your shoes I" The watching ladies also wondered to what extent the nurse's affection would carryler, while Mark, flushing because of the attention he had aroused, hoped strongly that Lizzie would not forget herself and make the pair of them look foolish.</p>
        <p>----- She met hit iin a helpful spirit. As she seized his hand she said: "Well, Mark, I've come." , "So I see, Liz," whispered the trooper, quietly. "I'm going to ask you about it in a jiffey., For a moment or two the Yeomen and the passengers continued to watch; then, yith such remarks as They are brother and sister, lifter all," and "What a dull pair!" they gave attention to other matters.</p>
        <p>Mark and Lizzie saw, the falling-off in interest concerning them, and were grateful for it. They courted neither fame nor notoriety, and, being merely people from the country, shrank from publicity of any sort. "Now then, Liz," said the trooper, briskly, "look sharp and explain what you're doing here. I'll confess I was a bit surprised to see you. I shouldn't have thought this was the sort of journey for a woman to make. Who did you come with?" "The rest of us," answered Lizzie. "There are lots of girls on the ship that I know, only they've landed. I kept back because the sailors told me that your ship was due in, and would be here nearly as soon as us. We passed you, you know; but you didn't fancy I was so near, did you ?" "How should I?" asked Mark. "YOU see, you had the pull of me—l expect you knew: I was on board; but how was I to know that you were so near? Whose notion was it that you should come out like this ?" a-" Mine, of course," answered Lizzie. "Whose should it be?, Do you suppose it Would ever have entered father's head?" "Well, no; I don't think it would," answered Mark. "There was one thing he did, though—he kept his mouth shut." "That was because I told him to," said Lizzie. "He's very obedient. So's aunt." "They've spoilt you; they've let you run wild," said Mark, admiringly. "It's a bad thing to have only one in a family. You get your own way, and—" "If I hadn't had my own way, I shouldn't have been here," interrupted Lizzie. "How would you have liked that ?" "Nell," said Mark, cautiously, "there's a lot to be said on boat sides. Of course, I'm glad to see you, but I shall feel a bit bothered about you. I shall be always wondering if you're all right, and getting on well. What made you really come?" whenou ng Ia n d explainedstrong, and d i things. us t "Why, to look after you and as many more as I can," she replied. "Father was dead against it at first; but he gave way You see, I thought it would be such a pity not to use my trainin,". I'm y the thing for work out here. I had no difficulty about getting them to accept me." "But you should have stayed at home," protested Mark, rather feebly. "It was your place." , "My place is out here," said Lizzie, stoutly. "I'd been trained as a nurse in the infirmary, hadn't I? And even if I'd gone back home to look after the farm and things, it wasn't likely I should sit down quietly when I heard that you were coming, was it?" "I suppose they want women nurses," said " Mark, evading a direct answer. "Though I have beard it said since we started that women in a place like this, at such a time, are a pest and a nuisance. It was an old soldier who said it." "I daresay some of them will be—we brought out some fair speclinew," answwed Lizzie, by no means disturbed at this sweepiffg criticism of , her sex. "We've got both bees and drones. There was a small tribe of us nurses coming out second- class; we've all been through the mill, and know our work, and are going to do it. Ia the first- class were soine of the grand ladies who are going to do such things as were never heard 9f. Two or three of 'em had ladies'-maids with them, and have an idea that they can go sailing round in fancy caps and aprons, bewitching everybody, wad comforting and soothing poor Tommy." "I hope 'they won't come messing about me," isnagidsMortarokf, non, gruffly. want none of that patronis- "As for us," added Lizzie, speaking cheerfully, but with an under-current of bitterness which Mark noticed and understood, "they didn't seem to think we were worth bothering with. One of then; one day came up and began to talk with me. Lady Mildred Maud they call her—what else I don't( know. She said: 'My good woman.'" • "I hope you cut her short," said Mark, angrily, "I told her I thought she'd made a mistake," added Lizzie. quietly, "and that if she wanted her maid, she'd perhaps find her downstairs." "I'm jolly glad," said Mark, with a smile. ''Confound her impudence I" lie exclaimed, wrathfully, as the full enormity of the .turtnown lady's offence recurred to him. "I wish I'd been there—and I wish she'd roma up to me and called me: 'My good man !' that's all!" "She isn't worth bothering about," said Lizzie: "so what's the good of wasting words on her? Well, your people ate pulling themselves together, aad seem to be wantinityou."</p>
        <p>t Mark turned hie head, and saw that the Yeomen were preparing to march off the wharf into a neigh- bouring shed. He was also reminded that it was .tintela leave by cries of: "Cut it short, Yorky !" and that be must fall in.</p>
        <p>"I'll have to go back," he said, hurriedly. a Well, • I'm glad I've seen you; but I wish you hadn't come. It'll upset me to think of you running risks out here. I'd rather you'd stayed at home."</p>
        <p>"If it was your duty to come, it was mine too," said Lizzie, courageously. "I'm not going to get in anybody's way—don't you fear that. I'm just going to make myself handy and useful. Women have done it before in war time, and I don't see why they shouldn't do it again. Don't you bather about me. I can look after myself—you do the same."</p>
        <p>Mark with this rejoined his comrades, and with a somewhat heavy heart marched off the wharf. Glad though he was for many things that Lizzie had come to the Cape, and was so near him, her presence had remiaded him deeply of the farm and his people—all of whom he had tried resolutely and successfully to forget. In this he followed 11; example of more than one member of the company. men who had vowed that they must eitheP obliterate their memories of home, or be at OP times wretched.</p>
        <p>"Throw yourself neck and crop into your work Mark, lnd you'll soon get out of the dumps," said Brigg,wlho noticed his friend's depression. "That's what I'm doing. If I stop to think of the governor and the old place, I feel a lump in my throat that's big enough to choke me. It won't do, you know, and 'that's the fact. We've got to see this game through now, and, for my part, I want to see it through as cheerfully as I can. Besides, I'm going to weigh in for a commission. There'll be lots knocking round for smart chaps, and I want to be one of 'em. If I can go back as Lieutenant john Brigg, it'll reconcile the old man to selling the brewery and letting me stick to the army. I tell you, Mark, this sort of life snits me down to the ground; it's just what I was built for. Now then, buck up—that's my tip. There, that's better. You look a different man. You were moping a bit, weren't you ? " "Just a trifle," Mark admitted. "But it's over now. What's the programme ? " "Get the transport cleared, and leaie to-morrow for the headquarters of the Imperials—that's a place called Newport, or something like it; we're to stick there till they get the bridge at Norval's Pont repaired, and then go straight on with half- a-dozen battalions of the Yeomanry to Bloemfon- tein. That's what I've managed to pick up. Yon see, I'm very keen on the business, and want tif know all I can get to know. I'm like the news- paper chaps you've seen knocking about—and thee isn't much that they miss." "Then we're not to be kept mugging round at the tail-end of things P" said Mark. "No," answered Brigg. "Good old noodles What price the scoffers when we all go marching home again?" They, like the rest of the company, threw themselves heart and soul into their work; indeed, so full of his task did Mark become that it was not until they were actually on their way to Naaupoort that he found time to dwell on the meeting with Lizzie and the possibilities of coming across her again. He knew that in such a vast field of opera- tions she might be•sent to a hospital hundreds of miles away from any place he was likely to touch; but, on the other hand, it was 'just- as possible that she might follow, if not accompany, the Imperial Yeomanry. He hoped that fortune would favour him enough to let it be so. _ .</p>
        <p>The Yeomanry were keen to learn and willing to work, and with the ambition that filled many of them to rank amongst the best troops in South Africa, they rapidly developed into a first-rate fighting body. As to their kit, they compared unfavourably with Some of their fellows—they had not, for example, the field-glasses, macintoshes, compasses and pillows with which a grateful county had provided a neighbouring battalion ;but, on the other hand, there was ample provision -in the shape of necessaries, and extras like flannel cholera belts, worsted caps, clasp-knife, and lanyard, with marlin-spike, puggaree muslin, and nippers for cutting barbed-wire fences. As for Mark, he did not notice or feel concerned about the disadvantage; he was rapidly becoming used to sleeping without a pillow, and as for water- proofs, he had lived too long iii the open air, and had been too often drenched to feel the loss of one. Nor, with his keen sight, did he think that the glasses would be much advantage. As for the compasses, he considered that the probability of having to fall back on one was too remote to cause him trouble. _</p>
        <p>Brigg was less phlegmatic. He coveted the extra equipment, since it would have added to his value as an isolated lighter; he was envious, too, of thn luck which caused the compassed horsemen to be ordered on to Kimberley, whence they were to join an expedition for the relief of Mafeking. , "Never mind," Mark said to Brigg, "our turn will come soon. May it come before we've time to rust and rot in camp."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>CHAPTER VI.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VI.</p>
        <p>TTIE FIRST SKIRMISH.</p>
        <p>Every officer and man of the Ninetieth Company of Imperial Yeomanry, to which Benn and Brigg belonged, became acclimatised with marvellous celerity, and, as the Major commanding proud!; told them, made one united and happy family, because they came from one united and happy county. It was fitting, he opined, that the largest county in England should send to the war the best equipped company, and that when so many of the other horses were sick and out of condition, they should be proud that a horse-breeding county had furnished them with mounts that were almost eaual to the salted quadrupeds of the land in which they were to fight—and conquer. The Major was an e.xcellent old cavalry .officer from the Wolds district, and liecause he was a shrewd ridge of horseflesh, he coveted the animal which }antler Bonn had given to Mark. But he had pledged his word to the old man, who had seen him, that Prince should be the property of Mark, and Mark alone, and he could not, with an easy conscience, try to wheedle the trooper out of it. There was- his authority, certainly, but, as he remarked to the senior subaltern, one had to be careful in being unduly authoritative with the Imperial Yeomanry "It isn't, you see, as if they'd been 'trained as real soldiers," he said, unconsciously speaking in the spirit which had caused Mark Benn such bitterness. "If I pressed the point of right to possess the horse, the young rpan would be quite cipable of saying that a bargain' S a bargain, and, superior or no superior, he'bl' see me—well.' bothered first. It's a way they have in the part of Yorkshire where he comes from. I admire 'em fca it."</p>
        <p>But the Major did not utter the last senterme until he wa3 well assured that no one except the subaltern would hear it. "All thl same," ha added, in a low voice, "if anything should happen —which I should be genuinely sorry for—,that horse is mine." -</p>
        <p>The commanding officer's scruples did not prevent hint from commissioning- the senior subal- tern to try and induce Mark to effect an exchange —Prince for a charger, with, say. fifty pounds thrown in ; but Benn was_so emphatic in declaring that not for all the gold and jewels in South Africa would he part with Isis horse, that no further effort was made to suborn him. It was not km; before Benn and Brigg were in their first skirmish. A Yeomanry patrol had reported a body of the enemy ten miles west of the company's headquarters. When the alarm was given, the horses of the mounted troopers were out grazing, but.in 'just over half-an-hour after the order to turn out had been given Benn's company marched off. It was found that the enemy was occupying :'a kopje, and to dislodge him the company was dismounted, and went on foot to the attack.</p>
        <p>Very steady and methodical did the business seem to the ardent Brigg. For more than three hours the advance was conducted over the bare veld, then the kopje was stormed and carried with the bayonet. The defendcri had waited almost ti.tt the last, then a white flag had fluttered, and after thatiwas quick ,surrender. Wild, with the excitement of the fray, Brigg had pushed on to the further crest of the kopje, a pont from which there was a long slope to another stretch of, veld, Bonn keeping with him. "We can easily skip back again and join the others," explained Brigg. "I wanted a peep into this new part. Come on; we shan't be wiseed for ten minutes or so._ _Hullo! Down, Mai`k, down!" ,</p>
        <p>Its crouched behind a rock as be uttered the warning, and pointed to some distant specks. Apparently more of the enemy, unconsorous of their comrades' fete, were corning on towards the kopja to join the commando. "There are only three—we'll bag them ourselves, and get all the glory. They'll just walk into our clutches as softly as saying 'Good-morning' to the parson," Nvhi spered Brigg. "Sit 1 Keep quiet. Our people are jostling down the hill again, taking their prisoners with 'em. Ten minutes later, and we'll make the Major a present of three more.</p>
        <p>Ile raised himself a little, and pstoilli,"over the edge of the rock. By this time the figures were pretty clearly seen—three of Atem, jogging along on wiry ponies, with a good spate between each. Bat when 'they came within range dt the two Yeomen Brigg exclaimed: ,"They're not coming up the hill, after all; they're going to sheer off to the right there. But we can't be done in this way; we shall have to pot 'era, Mark." Benn made no reply'. He crouched and watched the advancing horsemen, making such a fair target in the clear air and on that Tait plain. For some minutes there was silence, broken only by the sounds of the departing Yeomen; but the hvcopers were not seen or heard by the advancing It was plain that they were ignorant of the p:sence of a foe.</p>
        <p>"But, I say," exclaimed Brigg, in a low, intense voice, "this is a bit of bully sport, isn't it? Better than the old stick-in-the-mud life at home, eh, Mark ? I wouldn't &amp;range places- now with the Prince of Wales, would you?"</p>
        <p>Benn made no answer. He was to the full as excited as his friend, but he had morb control over himself. He felt, too, an undefinable unwillingness to take the life of fellow-men in, as it seemed to him, cold blood. There were no fair odds here; everything was on the side of himself and Brigg, and every-thing against the three unconscious figures on the veld. "Now we've got 'em V whispered Brigg, hoarsely. "I'll pot the chap in front, you tackle the one in the middle. If we're lucky we'll both blaze away at the third, if not, just weight ia on spec.." _</p>
        <p>Brigg's rifle was steadily levelled for an instant on the ledge of rock; there was a crack as he pulled the trigger, and almost unconsciously Benn also discharged his own weapon. The first of the horsemen threw up his arms with wild cry that rang on the still air and came _towards the Yeomen. He reeled backward and tumbled to the ground, where he lay perfectly still. The pony in terror dashed onward, side by side with the other two, who were galloping back across the veld.</p>
        <p>Brigg gave a loud cheer. "Again Get Fsther !" he shouted. "Steady, Bonn, steady 1" own hands trembled as he fired again. Mark also fired mechanically, as it seemed, but with a hard, set face and unfaltering nerve. . .</p>
        <p>"Done 'em-both, by--" cried Irrigg, springing to his feet and gazing intently over the brow of the hill. "The ponies are off, but all the men are down. See, they don't move I Let's slip down and see what their luck is. If they're dead it's a lucky bit of work; if they're only hurt we must look after 'em; besides, they'll be our prisoners. Come on." _</p>
        <p>He sprang over the rocks and rushed wildly down the sloping face of the kopje until he was on the veld. Bann, as good a runner, and as excited, leaped and hurried too. Brigg had ' dropped hie rifle on starting to run, and Mirk left his behind as well. Almost without thinting, he was regarding Brigg as a otuerior officer Whose actions were to be obeyed as readily as if they were spoken orders. The first of the enemy had been brought down three hundred yards from the foot Of the little hill. He was lying on his face motionless, when Briggs and Mark bent over him. "He's done for," said Brigg, after a very brief examination. "By George I . Look, the bullet • went slap through his back. Put bun down. We'll look at him when we've seen the other two." They hurried on for a hundred yards more, then stopped beside the two men who had been thrown from their saddles, and whose ponies had become mere moving specs. I "They're alive and kicking!" exclaimed Brigg. '"There isn't much the matter with 'em. A bit knocked by the fall, I daresay. Isn't that so, 'sonnies? Any bones broken or damage done? Or is it only a bit of a thump on this confounded hard nobby earth of yours? Come, jump up on to your pins." I The two men rose slowly to a sitting posture. "You seem to be all right," said Brigg, critically. 1"I don't see anything wrong with you. Then you ,weren't hit, after all's'? You were only jerked out of your saddles ? " I He spoke in deep, but, only temporary, dis- appointment, as if this state of things reflected on his markmanship. Benn, standing impassively .near him, was secretly delighted at the men's escape. It seemed to him that penalty enough had been paid by that silent figure in the roar which they had looked upon just now. "Come, you're prisoners," said Brigg. "Get up on your pins, and march. Stop that. No signalling. What are you up to ? " I He turned swiftly round to see to whom the men ,were making signs, then he sprang into the air with a deep groan. At the same instant there was the sharp crack of a rifle. , I Mark 1 ent down and raised his comrade's bead. "It's (ome sooner than I expected," said Brigg; • "but tell the old dad I died game." Without another word he lay stretched upon the veld; and before Berm could collect his senses the men had sprung to their feet and were upon him. While one pinned him for a moment the other • snatched a rifle from the ground and covered him. Immediately the grip was loosened, and 'Mark stood upon the veld covered by the rifle. "Move an inch and you're a dead man," cried _ the Boer who covered him.' .-1 Bann looked from him to the other, who by this time had also got his rifle, and before the two muzzles felt his own absolute helplessness. The truth flashed through his brain as he stood there watching. The man they had left. for dead was shamming, though wounded, and it Vas he who, crhile the Yeomen's backs were turned, had raised himself and fired. Even while Benn was covered the injured man came up. His thickly-bearded face was darkened with the dust of the veld, his clothes, tap, in front, were brown with dry earth, and on this background was a tiny red line, made by the blood from the wound caused by Brigg's bullet. The wounded man came up and turned over the form of Brigg pretty much as, a few moments earlier' the Yeoman had turned him. As he dropped the lifeless figure twain he gave a short harsh laugh, and turned his bloodshot eyes on Benn. "He's done for, too," he said. "A bullet through the chest is neither here nor there; but if. you get it through the brain from the back you ist no more. And, look you, I've a mind to serve you in the same way.' , "Skunk !" said Benn. "That would be murder, not fighting!" He spoke with all the passionate loathing Which he felt at such an act, reckless of the dangerous look on his opponent's face. "What was your's then," demanded the burgher. ."Hiding on the kopje and shooting us down?" "You took the risk of it," answered Benn. "You knew what to expect." "Fine courage Nice game, hiding in a hole like a toad and spitting out," sneered the burgher. "It's a game that your people have taught us to play," replied Bonn, undaunted. "I suppose, as you speak English so well, you'll be getting pay as an English servant of scme sort, as well as the blood money from these people?" He nodded his head contemptuously at the other two men, who were staring somewhat vacantly at the burgher and the Yeoman, not fully understanding what was • passing. Stung by the taunt the burgher, who was in reality a man receiving English pay as a railway employe, and also pay from the Transvaal Govern- ment, half-raised his rifle.</p>
        <p>Without a word Benn flung himself forward, striking the rifle aside as he closed with the burgher. With one strong wrench he had the weapon in his hand, with another he gripped his opponent and swung him round, so that he Iced "—• rifles of the other two startled men. _ _ _</p>
        <p>he Btood thus, with the burgher a shield, Ptis left arm pinning the weakened limbs of the •--ounded man, he raised the Mauser he had captured, and in turn covered the two Boers. It was a skilful feat, one possible only to a man like Mark, who had handled a gun and run and wrestled ever F ince his boyhood. . _ _</p>
        <p>"Down with 4 'em! Ground 'em! DOW 2, 1 say!" he exclaimed. For an instant only there was hesitation. ' They looked at the blanched face of their companion, helpless and almost fainting because of the hmmmorrhage which had come upon him, and looked again. To kill or capture him it would be needful to destroY their comrade also, for ;t was impossible for shaking hands to hold the rifle steady enough to cover tho portion of brown, bard facNwhich peered over the burgher's shoulder. "Down! Do as he tells you I" ordered the burgher, in terror; and the men sullenly put their Mausers on the earth. .</p>
        <p>"Stop out, one on each side, further; well out, and,then back," pried Benn. He watched them as they obeyed him, watched until they were twenty yards from each other aml twenty. from himself, then commanded them to halt. They obeyed, casting longing looks at their irreapons. "Now, you," said Berm suddenly letting his prisoner go, and stepping swiftly • beyond reach of him. "Fall in with them." "But.—but---" began the burgher.</p>
        <p>"Fall in with them. If you're going to say yono can't walk, remember how you got over the ground- a minute since."</p>
        <p>Benn spoke mercilessly. He remembered the burgher's hurt; but he remembered also that Brig: was lying near him. For the moment all. his humanity had been dried up within him. The burgher, more feebly now that his wounds had become aggravated, tottered towards his com- panions, who received him churlishly, as the cause of their present trouble. When the three were facing him, Bonn stepped- forward and quickly took up the two rifles Which, were lying on the ground, never letting his gaze leave the prisoners. With two Maniere tucked under his left arm, and carrying the third in his right, he ordered the unwounded men to sup- port their comrade and to march to the camp which he had left so recently with Brigg. Slowly, and with muttered grumbling; the two Boers each took an arm of the wounded man and began to journey doggedly towards the camp. They were in front of Benn, and their long. shadows, from the declining sun behind them, travelled fantastically over the dry veld. Benn passed very near his comrade and marvelled that he was so much like a sleeping man. They were a hundred yards from the foot of the kopje from which the shots had been fired, when suddenly, on the summit, standing boldly out against the sky-line and with the sun shining full upon them, appeared some men with rifles. In authoritative voice cried "Halt ! " and Benn, as well as his prisoners, beame motionless,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>(To be continued.)</head>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
        <p>AN AUSTRALIAN_ GIRL IN LONDON- Extraordinary Statements to a Sunday Paper.</p>
        <p>The Weekly Dispatch is responsible for the authen— ticity of stioP tollowing remarkable interview with a young Australian lady now residing in London, at an address publisned and vouched for by our enterprising Sunday contemporary, whose actual photograph of Ve girl is here reproduced. _</p>
        <p>"I' was born in Melbourne," said Miss Olive Clarke; of 8, Harlock•road, Wimbledon, "and I am now just upon the age of sixteen. I have always been a very healthy girl ; in fact, I did not, whilst I was In Auetralia, know what even a headache was. InEngland people do net seem to live so much in the, open air as is the case in Australia, and I think this accounts for the number of pale faces that are to be seen when one walks abroad. But I want to iell lOU about myself. I was well and strong when I came to England a little over three years ago. As you know the. winter in Australia is merely a wet season, but I don't think it hurts anyone. I had never seen snow until I came to England, and I could not make out what had come over London when one day I found it suddenly grow dark tbrough a November fog. Snow followed, and one day I got my feet wet, of which I took little notice. About a fortnight after, however, I began to. feel HI. I had dreadful headaches and pains in my back. My mother gave me some simple homely remedies, and I think I improved, but I had a renewal of the pains in my back, and they remained long with me:</p>
        <p>"For more than a couple of months I had the pain, night and day, until I began to think I should never get rid of it. I had grown deathly pale. My lips had no • cf lour at all, and my hands were lust like a white sheet of paper. My eyes were sunken in my head, and often for days I would have terrible noises in my ears. 1. was always tired, and if I walked quickly I get BO out. of breath that I had to stand still for a few minutes before I could proceed again. I tried lots of remedies, and had many nottles of medicine from doctors, but nothing seemed to do me any good. Everybody said I was in a decline, and if you had seen the lines about my face you would have thought that I was an old woman, instead of a young girl. At lc* my mother read in the Weekly Dispatch of a lady who had suffered in the iame way as I was suffering, and who had been cured by Dr Williams' pink pia for pale people. That is about tour months ago, and I wish she bad seen the story before. I went to a chemist in Wimbledon and bought a hex. Before I had taken the pills long the noises in my ears stopped. Betore I had finished a second box the pains in my head and back left me suddenly, and I began to stand upright. I still went on with Dr Williams' pink pills, and when I got to the end of my fourth box I was completely cured. I could run and jump about the same as I did in Australia, aad I got quite stout. If you had seen me before I took them you would hardly have known ma n0w..." By the side of Miss Olive Clarke stood her mother, a handsome dark-eyed lady, who verified every word her att hhu ear letrdhr aebvpyeu Dtbaertei onne ffi secat el Williams'rde abpdyiynDkwr Williams' r daughter uttered, and, indeed, so many are the cures The disordetS ment of the blood. :motels, rickets. - lYi d .pink pills that tn. oluonic erysipelag. consumption, so temla, pale and sallow complexion, 100 pills include impovertsh- of appetite, palpitations, pains in the back, sciatica, neuralgia, early decay, rheumatism paralysis, and loce- motor ataxy; also the ailments from which women alone suffer. The pills are sold in wooden tubes, with pink paper wrapper,bearing the full name, Dr Williams' pink pills for pale people, in red. If not readily obtainable, send two shillings and ninepence for one box, or thirteen and nine for six boxes, to the manu- facturers, Dr Williams' medicine company. Holborn- viaduct, London, but when purchasing at a shop see that you obtain the genuine pills, which have cured, thousands, by asking for Dr Williams' pink pills, and refuse to be put off with worthless imitations that never Cure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>BRUTAL WARRINGTON FATHER-</head>
        <p>A BRUTAL WARRINGTON FATHER-</p>
        <p>Baby Severely Injured,</p>
        <p>Twelve Months' Imprisonment.</p>
        <p>The magistrates at the Warrington Police Court On Tuesday investigated prosecutions instituted by the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Child- ren against Thomas Mitchell, Bakehouse-yard, and the facts revealed were of an extraordinary:nature. Prisoner was charged with assaulting his fourteen months old child, Lawrence, on October 14th, and also with neglec- ting his four other children in a manner likely to cause them vinnecessary suffering and injury to health. Mr A Browne prosecuted on behalf of the Society and informed the Bench that prisoner had previously under- gone terms of imprisonment for neglecting his children ; and bad been under the supervision of the society for some years. Since February last, Inspector Hackett had visited the house no less than ten times but had found no improvement and would have prosecuved 6 &amp;On, but for the interventionl tif the man's wife. On the 14th inot the prisoner had not been working all day and at 10 o'clock at night he came home drunk. His wife bad gone to bed with the child, which was aged 14 mouth,. Prisoner went upstairs to her and _asked • her for his supper. She told him that as . he had not worked she had rot got any supper for him sod prisoner thereupon made use of a threat towards her. The woman jumped up in order to try and get out of the bed, when prisoner struck at her with a belt. Instead of hitting her be caught the baby a blow in the eye with the buckle. The woman took the baby to a doctor ths next morning, tolling him that the child had sustained the injury by falling out of bad. The doctor did not believe that statement and concluded that the injury had been caused by very violent usage. no advised the woman to take the baby to the Manchester eye hospital and she did so on Monday. Inspector Hackett got to know of this and followed her to the hospital. Elei there ascertained from the doctors dist the sight of the eye had been entirely destroyed. Prisoner bad neglected his family from time to time and would not work. Prisoner said his wife was lying in bed drunk Said he intended to hit hers The Chief Constable said the man had been before the court 13 times. He bad served three terms of t:lree- months' Imprisonment for neglectieg his children, and had also been convicted three times of theft. He was an idle, worthless, loafing thief. The Chairman (Mr Birehall) said the magistrates would mark their sense of the enormity of the offence by sending prisoner to gaol for six months on each charge, the sentences to run consecutively.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAlLUREL—According to " KenlP'6' Mercantile Grazdte," the number of New Bankruptcy proceedings In Bngland and 11,41.. a gazetted during the week ending October 19th, was 90. Tbn number in tile eorresponding week of hat year was 73, showing an, 1-crease of 17, being a net decrease in 1901, to date, of 149.</p>
        <p>1M...-_</p>
      </div>
