      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Warrington Thrash Oldham. On Saturday last Oldham came to Warrington with a record of six suecessive victories, hut that record spoiled by the " Wirepullers," who trounced their formidable foes by 15 points to 5, the largest seers which has been obtained by any club against Oldham ~,,for some years. Neither side was at full strength, Warrington having to substitute Ratcliffe for Dickenson at centre, while Oldham missed Thomas (full back), and Lees (centre). their places being filled by Dickson and Ike Tayisis A special train brought several hundred enthusiasts from Oldham, and there would be a crowd of about 7,1100 present when the game commenced. The Weather was again all that CDuld be desired. Mr Kidd, Df Millom, refereed, and the teams were as follow . Warrington Hallam. full back ; Fish, Isherwood, Ratcliffe and Harris, three-quarters ; Allen and Hockenhall, half backs; Morrison, Fell, Edmondson, F Swift, J Swift, Jolley,Cunninghain and Dakin,forwards. Oldnatn Dickson. full back ; fluzzey, Civil, Taylor and Williams, three-quarters ; Lawton and A Lees, half- backs ; Frater, Fosgus.ces, Ella, Topham, Vowles, Wormald, Wilkinson and Waterhouse, forwards, Frater started the game. and Warrington scored sensatonally in the first minute. Morrison had replied to the kick and A Lees misfielding the home front rank • svas down on him in a trice, the ball being dribbled past Dickson and over the line where Jolley completed the effort to the dismay of the visitors and the frantic delight of the home crowd. Fish missed an easy shot at goal. This early wiccess in- spired the locals with confidence,for following the drop- kick arcentre,the backs got going and Ratcliffe, passing to Flarris, Williams just grassed the outdde man a few yards from the line. Allen took advantage of some • clever work by the home forwards, and the ball, travel- ling with rapidity through the hands of Isherwood and Hockethall, an opening was made for Fish, and this player eluding Huzzey, much to that player's astonish- - ment, the Warrington flyer soon defeated the Oldham custodian, and raced in behind the posts, for Isherwood to add the goal. Warrington still attacked strongly, but after Hands had made a futile attempt to get through,Wiiliams dribbled to the quarter flag. Hallam, however, piloted back, and for an infringement the - visitors were penalized, and Fish placed a goal from an easy position. The visitors could not make headway., and another round of passim; by Allen, Ratcliffe, Isherwood. and Fish almost cost Oldhan another score. fluzzey this time made ro mistake with Fish, grassing him at the corner. Taylor took a pass from Lawton, and for the first time in the match Oldham showed something like their proper form. An opening, was made for Williams, and he appeared likely to get clear, until Harris grasSed him at half way. Headway was made by Frater and Ellis, and a forward movement was checked by Hallam. A round of passing by the visiting three-quarter lini saw ' Civil throw forward to Huzzey, but from the succeeding pick tie ball travelled beck across the field, and this time Williams put on the finishing touch, Huzzey failing: at goal. The game costinued very fast, emu Warring- ton forcing the game at the railway end, Allen and Hockenhall, in quick succession were both within an ace of scoring. A fine effort by Fe'l was followed by nombined play by Edmondson, Dakin, and F Swift, Dixon being hanky to save near his line. A penalty against Allen did not afford the visitors much relief, for Warringtoa coon harassed Dixon's charge. Civil punted out of danger and Hallam was called upon. The War- rington cuOodian in his attempt to follow up was Palpably obstructed, and the place being an easy one Fish had. little difficulty in adding the goal, this item 'bringing about the interval with the score Warrington, three goals two tries (12 points) to Oldham one try (three points). On resuming,tbe Warringtonians were soon attacking, and after Isherwood bad been pulled up near the Oldham line. Fish almost placed a goal from a fair mark by Allen. Immediately after the last-named got away and looked likely to score until Williams floored _him a yard from the line. The fast pace was maintained, and Ea- CCFSiVe runs by Huzzey and Williams took the ball beyond the centre line. Hovey tried to place a goal from here, but the ball rebounded off an opposing for- ward. Oldham, however, further improved their Pie- Lion by forward stages, and the ball travelled through several pairs of hands to Huzzsn,who tried a long drop, the ball travelling outside. Oldham still attacksd and a Movement by the backs saw Civil essay to pass to Huzzey, but Fish, interception in his own quarter. the Warrirgtonian scored one of the most sensational tries over witnessed on the Wildelspool enclosure. With only Dixon to teat, Fish shot past the Oldham back with wonderful rapidity, and although pursued by limey he held his advantage to the end. The reception accorded the scorer on returning to midfield was kept up for several alinutes, and rarely has such a scene Of enthusissm been Witnessed. The succeeding play still went in Warring- trin's favour, and the forwards took matters in close Proximity to the pavilion goal. Huzzey and A. Lees kist their side out al a difficult position, and further pro- gress being made by Lawton and Williams, the visitors got near the Warrington quarter fig.. After Huzzey had lipe failed at a peoalty kick, the Warringtonianr were nalized near the posts' and Dixon easily landed the g°lo. A clever piece of rlay by Hockenhall saw Huzzey</p>
        <p>ttckled near his line, and in the last stages of the match the Warringtonians at:ackel with such energy as to astonish evert the hoar, supporters. The pace continued a cracker, and tter Allen had been pulled up Oldham were penaliz-d, but Fish just missed the goal. In the last minute of the match fluzzly made a floe effort to get through, but Allen and Hallam proved too much for him, and the whistle sounded a cessation of hostilities wita the score as follows :—Warrington, three goals, three tries (15 points) ; Oldham, one goal, one try (five points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Broughton Rangers v. Salford.</head>
        <p>Broughton Rangers v. Salford.</p>
        <p>These local teams met en the Rangers' ground, before about 16,000 spectators. Both clob3 placed their strong- est team in the field, and a very exciting game was played. From start to finish the S Word forwards more than held their own, and this enablrd Griffiths and Lomas to get the ball almost continually from the scrummages. They fed their three-quarters unselflqh- ly, but that line was the weakest spot in the Salford team, and many capital chances wen allowed to :1 p away. On the other hand, the Rangers played tar tr2low their usual form, and they ware decidedly lucky to win, even by such a narrow margin. Final score: Broughton Rangers, one try (Widdesm) ; Salford, one goal (L'unas).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>Leigh v. Hunslet.</head>
        <p>Leigh v. Hunslet.</p>
        <p>At Leigh, b fore 4..000 spectators. The Leigh nice- quarter line was weak-n dhy the absence of Ganley, who was injlred. Hunslst started, and after two forward rushes by Leigh had been repell,d the Hunslet passing machinery got going, and W G3ldthorpe made a fine openina for Hannah. who scored at the corner,the place-`Tick fading. Shortly afterwards fine passing by the Leigh backs ended in Johnson clodang Horne and Wright and scoring.. but Milyneux fail3.l at goal. At the interval each side had scored a try. On resuming, Hunslet went off with a rush and reached the Le gh line, where GilLngs passed wide to W Goldthorpe, who threw to Hanna 'a for the latt,,r to score essily. and A Goldthorpl kicked a goal Leigh then attacked hotly, and Du,laavin placed a penalty goal. For a long time Leigh swarmed round the Hunslet line, and Davies, fr-mi a croarick by M,l7nrux, got over, but was ruled off-eide. Hunslet eventually cleared their lines. and after a series of attempts A Goldthorpe eventually kicked a goal f,om a mark. Two 4lorious chances of scoring were misse I by Leigh in the last few minutes, rod flunslet won by two goals and two tries (ram points) to one goal and one try (five points). Brighouse Rangers i.e. Batley. - -</p>
        <p>At Brighouse, before 3,000 swet3.torr. Itssult : Witlcy, on, goal and one tcy (five points) ; Brighouse, two goals (four points). Bradford v. Hull.</p>
        <p>At Bradford, lolfore about 9 000 spectator,. Rout . Hull, one coal and two tries (eight ry)ints) ; Bradford, two tries (six points). Halifax V. Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>S'x thousand spectators gathrred at Halifax. R.sult : Halifax, two 'goals and five tries (19 puiots) ; field, one one goal (two points).</p>
        <p>THE SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The tablesun-to-date is as follows : Points. P. W. L. D. For Agst. P. Widnes</p>
        <p>Warrington 1 ... 1 ... 0 ... 0 .. 10 ... 3 ... 2 Wigan 2 1 1 0 11 10 2 Runcorn. ...... St. Helens 3 0 3 0 4 .15 ... o Runcorn v. St Helens.</p>
        <p>A capital attendance welcomed Runcorn at the St Helens enclosure on Saturday. Both ides played strong fifteens, end the greatest interest was taken in the match. In the early stages of the match Runcorn were penalimcl, but Barnes failed to place the goal, and the subsequent clearance by Wilkinson and Butterworth saw Runcorn hotly assail the home goal. Foulkes relieved slightly, but another return to the attack saw Jolley speeri down the open, beingwill collared by Pearson. Play was very fast, and excellent work by the Runcorn forwards Cave Jolley and Richardson every opportunity to shine. Both Butterworth and Warder had chances, but I hey failed to escipe the vigilance of Pearson, Foulkes, and Barnes.- A rapid passing movement by the whole of oho Runcorn backs saw Jones miss a somewhat easy °hence, anal his failure was taken advantage tf by the home forwards, who rushed the game to the Runclrn quarters. A shot at goat by Nunter travelled outside, hut from the drop cut Richardson ran strongly after taking a pass from Jolley, reaching Foulkes before he was floored, The visitors , pressed hard and excellent defensive work was shown by Hunter and Flibberr. Following a spell of desultory play, ttfe ball was handed to Butterworth, who coolly dropped a goal. The succeeding play generally went in Rcncorn's favour, and St Helens were lucky with only two points against them at' the interval. St. Helens re-itarted, and after play had settled down Runcorn attacked, Good paving dent the bail to Moran, who missed atogether when near the lino. After a series of wild kicks on the S. Helens line Runcorn had a free kick, but Farmer failed to land the goal. Barnes was applauded for a grand run, and Green started a dribble, and took the ball beyond half way. The game continued at a fast rate, and after Briers had dribbled to the Runcarn quarters the Ceerians asserted , themselves, and a fine running passing movement was checked by Foulkes. Free kicks to the visitors further improved their position and Warder tri:d a drop at, goal, the ball striking the upright. Soon after disaster befel the visitors, for Jolley was badly damaged, and had to be assisted off the field. Desp.to the fact of being short banded, the visitors kept the locals busily defend- ing, and another shot by Farmer, from a penalty, wer t outside. A momentary inva,ion by Doherty and Foulkes into the Runcorn quarters was dealt with by Warder, and Richards,m tram a short range tried a shot at goal, the ball travelling outside. Near the close St. 11-lens made an effort and Briers almost succeeded, after a brilliant run, but Runcorn's defence proved sound to the ciao and the final came with the score Runcorn, one goal (two points) ; St. Helens, nil.</p>
      </div>
