      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>I'HE EXAMINER, FRP) A le, OCTOBER 25 1901.</head>
        <p>I'HE EXAMINER, FRP) A le, OCTOBER 25 1901.</p>
        <p>Sankey Aibions v Warrington Central.</p>
        <p>D,is match was Wayed on the Ailbions' ground and re- sulted in a win for the homssters by five goals to one.</p>
        <p>Sankey Green A.F.C. v Sankey Villa.</p>
        <p>Thee team; met on the ground ot the first-named and the match r e ulted in an easy win for the homeaters by six goals to one. T Spooner, P ['mulct, %V Chippendale each scored goals for the winners.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ASSOCIATION FOO rBALL LEAGUE</p>
        <p>Position of the clubs up to o;tober 19h, 1901. Goals</p>
        <p>_ Plava3 Won. Lost Dwn, For Agst P. Bewsey All Saints Burtonwood 6 . 4- . 1 . 1 . 10 . 5 . 9 Latchford Warrington Trinity .... St. Austins . Runsorn Parish Church St. Mary's 5 . 2 . 2.. 1 . 12 5 5 Earlestown Amateurs 6 ... 2 ... 3 ... 1 ... 9 ...12 .. 5 Frodsham Parish Church 6 ... 2 ... 3 ~. 1 ... 11 ...11 ... 5 Sankey Albilns Latchford Britannia 4 . 2 . 2 . 0 6 . 7 4 Dare sbury 5 . 2 . 3 .. 0 9 ...10 ... 4 Stockton kleath nichrnonds A.1P.0 6 .. 1 . 4 .. 1 . 11 ...17 ... 3 Guardian A.F.C. Runcorn Parish v. Daresbury.</p>
        <p>This encounter took place at Runcorn in fine weather The game proved to be of a rough aed tumble descrip- tion, no ac• 1141 fotball taking placP., especially in the latter portion of the gam'. :S'vera.l ph&gt;es were hurt, and stoopvos in the g,..ne were very f.equent. The visitors ev-.ntually aOL th better of a miser..ole cxibi- Lion by four goals to one.</p>
        <p>_ Liverpool and District Combination. Newton-le-Willows V. Rhyl.</p>
        <p>The return match between these teams was played at Newton on Saturda!'. In the previous encounter, a fortnight ago, Newton were defeated by eight goals to one. The visitors, who turned up with only ten men, had the advantage of the ground in the first half. The g2.me ultimately ended in a draw of one goal eateh, although trom the chances afforded the home team the score should have been al least foul, to one.</p>
        <p>Lancashire League.</p>
        <p>RESULTS UP TO SATURDAY. -00118-</p>
        <p>• Pld Won Lost Urn For—Agst Pts Darwen . S out liporS Central Workington Chorley Haydock Stalybrldgo Rovers . 6.. 1 . 4 . 1 3 ...12 ... 3 Roehdkle Town 4 . 1 . 2 . 1 6 . 8 . 3 Preeoot Baena town Barrow St Helens Town 2.. 1 . 1. 0 4 . 5 . 2 Wigan United Warrington Rscup St. Helens Recreation v. Haydock.</p>
        <p>This match was played at S. Helens on Srtorday, before 1,000 spectators. The home attack prevailed aLd Haydock were defeated by thrse goals to none. Warrington Draw with Workington.</p>
        <p>- ; The Workington team were the visitors to the Arpley enclosure on liaturday-. Lynes started for the home team and play after opening stages was soon hovering round the Workington goal. Harrison and Sharpies were promimnt with several fine rues down right wing, but the final effort was in each case weak, and the visiting backs, Brown and Cacopbell,bad no difficulty in dealing with the attack, the former being in rare trim. Pretty work by Howarth and Dilworth saw Lynes making a good effort, and Pratt was lucky to clear. Profiting by this, the visitors took heart, and raced away to the other end, Riley being especially noticeable in some smart work hear the home goal. Aspinail cleared the danger, but only for the moment, as Et Smith was almost scoring immediately after. It was left to Cook to clear the visitors out, and from now to half-time play was fairly even. The resumption was eventful,for Warington. From a corner kick Aapinall only partially cleared, and R Smith scored. Tne home team roused themselves after this, and after a prolonged attack, Lewis gave Lynes, possession, and by passing to Dil- worth, the latterr scored a fine-goal. Nething further took plae , alth.oegh both sides tried hard to gain the lead, the result being one goal each. Earlestown v. Stalybridge Rovers.</p>
        <p>Earlestown commenced their list of home engagements on Saturday by receivirin a visit from last year's cham- pions. On the opening 'sday of this season, Earlestown made a very creditable draw at Stalybridge and the return match wis thnef6,re looked f n'ward to by the local supporters with very little misgiving. Since Settle left them, Earlestowa have been badly in want of a left outside and the player to take this position on Saturday was not chosen until the last minute, Reyes then beim: deputed to fill the vacancy. The teams were as f ol- lows :—Earlestown, Chew, goal; Leigh and Cottarn. backs ; Pennington, Whitehouse and Clibborn, half- backs ; Bartley, Barlow, Leach and Fleyes, forwards. Stalybridge Rovers, Armstrong, goal; Collinson and Brooks, backs; Green' Thompson and Madden. half- backs ; Holland, Cook, Lawson, Reads and Bamber, forwards. . .</p>
        <p>Eariestown won the toss, Lawson setting the ball in motion in the presence of about 1,500 spectators. The home halves were prominent by good tackling, all the eff las of the Rovers forwards being nullified in their infancy. Barlow got away with the leather.and cleverly eluding the backs made no mistake with his final shot, Armstrong having no chance of scoring. Encouraged by this success which came after the game had been in progress twenty minutes, Earlestown played up strongly, Reves swinging across a brautiful centre which Armstrong got rid of with difficulty. After a brief visit had:been paid to the borne s,,oal4artley and 'Barlow got possession from a pass bylDawsoh,the former shooting hard and straight, Armstrong falling full length and iHist tipping the leather round the post. The ensuing corner was correctly placed by Bartley, the ball dropping on top of the cross-bar and falling at the feet of Leach, this player tipping the sphere to Dawson • who in turn passal it on to Barlow who banged in a beauty, one of the Rovers backs, Brooks, knocking the flying leathor out with his hands. Dawson was deputed to take the teSultant pen,lty and although Armstrong got his hands to the ball, he failed to stop it from going into the net. Stung he this second reverse the Rovers pu'L forth strenuous efforts to reduce the h,mesters lead but the home half-backs played up splendidly, and the visitors' forwards were rarely allowed to become dangerouF. A foul against Leach placed the homcsters on the defensive and a neat pass from Bamber enabled Reade to have the first pop of the match at the Earles— town goal, Chew easily eff )cting a clearance. Prom now to the interval the homesters practically monopo- lised the play, but the whistle blew with Earlestown leading by two goals to nil. The second half was wel contested, though Eirlestown had tbe best of matters ' Play was in midfield when the final came with Earles• town victors by two goals be nil. STOP A COUGH IN ONE NiGHT.</p>
        <p>Take VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE. It stops an ordinary ctugh in one night, and cures ctironic,coughs, bronchitis, asthma, influenza and wnooping cough. Its vast superiority over the different emulsions and ordin- ary cough mixtures cannot te estimated. It has saved thousands of lives after they, had been turned oat of hospitals. It is a new scientifio remedy endorsed by Medical me a became it acts so speedily upon acute and c4ronic coughs, clears the bronchial tubes, gives perfect ease in breathing, and is a far superior remeui to any of the coalmen cheap evtuav mixtures now en the market. VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE contains ingredients never before used in Great Lirit sin and which are of incalculable value, that is why it is so highly re- commended. It already bas the largest sale, because it gives universal satisfaction. Ask for VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE : don't let a dealer give you a substitute, insist on 6' VErro's," as chemist can soon get it for you. Price li, lid. and 93, All Chemists and Medicine Vendors. (1).</p>
        <p>UNITED .IETITODIST FTEEE CHIJRC,TI MUTUAL IMPROVEMENT CLASS, RUNCORN A meeting of the above class was held on Friday evening under the presidency of 1r J Scrag when a paper was read by Me T Parry on "The theatre, ite influences." The writer dealt with the early</p>
        <p>introduction of the drama into this country, when it was brought over the sea by the ecclesias:ics who came over with the conqueror. , It took !la religious form, soon becoming popular with the people who were for the most part ignorant, and it was necessary to present the realities of life in some other form than by reading, Ttlll9 early England came to possess a popular form of amuse- ment, aiming at repreQenting life in forms combining the practical and the poetical The essayist went on to trace the history of the stage from those early times down to the present time, statinx that many eminent men went to the theatre in order to receive lessons which might be useful, both on platform and in pulpit. The ohs% of play produced dependedentirely on the character of the playgoer. That these had not always been of the best they knew for,— "Their tyle was witty though they had some ; Something they might save mended so may all.' A discussion took place afterwards, in whick the following members took part the Rev W Gay, Messrs W Fouldes, W Gandy, and T Curzon. A vote of thanks to the essayist, proposed by Mr Fouldes, seconded by the superintendent Minister brought the mesting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Ettters to the Orator.</head>
        <p>Ettters to the Orator.</p>
        <p>A Question of Education. 11.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lf any social good is to reach the people and beet me of universal benefit, it must be administered be the state. It cannot be left to voluntary and private efforts, otherwise it is only partial and temporary in its action—it has not the widest application and it will evei,tually e liapae. The wml I has only one sun—(re wWch it derives light and lite—Which rules all things and upon which ad things depend. Two suns would ruin the universe. A circle, also possesses only one point within ilB circumference ca lad the centre, and if other points are taken there: will be ol her circumfer- ences, each depending upon its own individual cen're, but cutting ore another and where they meet, making confusion and discord. Sa it is in the Stare. Where universal good is desired, it must spring from a com- mon centre, ntnerwise there is only partial success in anything undertaken. New, sir, looking oack upon the history of the past, I have come to the inevitable conclusion that everything lasting—everything really strong. and of general good— everything of universal benefit, has coma from the State. In feudal tiree when the barons held power, and were absolute, each upon his own laud, there was &amp;lac); and snatchy. Where every chin f administered justice within his awn domain there was no law and it was only by the Supreme Court of the monarch gain- ing power and preatige over all lesser courts-4n eter words by the State taking its proper position as law- giver to the people—that justte became really adminis- tered and law rose ir.to bin. This is the history of every feudal country in Europe. Again, when the defence of the country depended by feudal tenure upon each :baron bringing his own retinue of warriors to swell a common and often discordant area ,it will be easily underst-yed that such deft nee was always ineff,c- tual and precarious ; and the canntry which first realised the power and importance of a standing (or state) army was the country which first gained power over its contemn caries both in offence and defence. (See Robertson's " View of the Middle Ages.") In addi- tion to this let 'is come to more recent times and com- pare the Poet °dilo when first instituted and worked by private individuals—with its manifold abuFes and ineffectual etre, ts—compare this, I say, with its marvel- lous operations at the present time under State control. But this latter is too well known to need recapitulating. . .</p>
        <p>We repeat, thec, that any social gmod, to be of uri-i- -versal benefit, must be administered ti) the State. Manhind has realized it partial:y in the past—he is every day becorninv, more cognisant of the fact—and we will go fuLthsr and affirm that his social salvation in the futur.lepends mainly upon the clearness and celer- ity with which he apprehends what is rsally the first and greatest priocio!e of social science. When he does so,he will strike at the root of every sceal abose—of every impediment in the way of his progress to social perf(c'- ib 1 ty ;and he will achieve trueifreedom and independeace for the first time in history.</p>
        <p>Try to realise the situation ! It i 9 this. The body politic is a circumference and the State is its centre, from whish every eeneral good most irradiate. In stead, however, of b,ing this common centre she is the agent only of a few important services. She has the control of our defence—the Army and Navy. She is the controller of our Law. She administers the Post. But with our food supply-wits production and distribu- tion,—the railway (-hat truly important service),—with trade, internal and external, with manufactures, mines, agriculture, and land generally, the State has nothing to dr. Lastly, education, which is the subjrct of our present consideratioi, has been left to the' voluntary efforts of private individuals, or b)dies, and until recently, has tern wholly outside State Management. The State, own yet, has not the complete control of education I _ _ _</p>
        <p>--.„... Let us now recapitulate the position. England is taking a secondary commercial place 'amongst the nations. This decadance is due to her lack of education. Fier lack of education we must attribute to the fact that the State has not yet taken the sole management. BA why has she not? Like everything else of general truth it has been opposed by private and circumscribed principles—by vested interests—by stereo-typed instiru- tions—in a word, by that Conservatism, wherever found,which complacently theliev( s itself to be the sole depository of truth.heyond which there is nothing. Conservatism in England has taken many corms, but the same spirit rules each and every one of them. Them are Brewing Conse,rvatisms—Political and Social Conservatisms—Capitalist,Trade, and Clerical Conserve- tisms. E4ch of these is a petrified contentment, con- tent with its own prosperity without thought for others; estimating truth only by its own particular successes ; setting itself sternly against any scheme which is broader and mcre far-reaching than its own ; deemingy that nothing outside itself .can be of more • per- manent or more universal benefit. National Edu- cation, therefore, is opposed and hampered by vested or Conservative interests, but chiefly by the Clerical. This has always been the attitude of Clericalism in every age—in every county, and of all antagonists it is the most dangerous. Other Conserve- Clams are material—upheld by material force which force, after all, is the weakest. But Clericalism is a mental and intellectual Conservatism invading man's innermost being, blinding him to all material progress, and paralysing his action. It has always stood in toe way of civilisation as a very slight acquaintance with history will prPve. But the " thought "of man was de- termined to be free. Science conquered and so will education. To trust the latter to hundreds of little ves'od intere‘ta or even to the chief of theme—the established Church—is as absurd as to make them agents of our law—of the Army and Navy—or of the Post Office- But I shod be a little more explicit in my next letter.—Beieve me, yours faithfully, L'fIOASIE ttUl BIT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>CHILL OCTOBER OCTOBER.</head>
        <p>CHILL OCTOBER OCTOBER.</p>
        <p>This is the month when so many peeple, whose systems have been weakened by the heat of Summer, find themselves suffering from colas, coughs, difficulty in breathing, and sillily sensations generally. The heat of Summer fuquently resul:s in weakening the diges- tive system, which in turn reacts upon the livrr. As soon as the liver is deranged a certain amount of bile finds its way into the blood making it sluggish and impure. When the sYstem is in this state the food taken is only imperfettly digested ; and instead of creating rich, pure blood, blood of a very poor quality is produced. The outcome of this is that tire syst m loses its resisting power and the least draueht brims on a cold more or less severe. The circulation also becomes feeble, and the hands and feet are only kept warm with difficulty. Fits cf shivering and sneezing are common, and a mis- erable chilly sensation preyails. The nerves are also affectod, sympt -Tam of which are, restlessness, depres- elem, and a sensation of impending evil. 1,,1)w all theqi dire ailments arise primarily from die- orders of the digestive system end the bver ; indeed, liver chill may be said to account for most of them. It is of no use to take cough mixture fur the cold, sleeping draughts for reetlesiness at night, and tonics for the weakened systern.To do this is to deal only with distinct branches of the evil. What is required is a medicine which will remove all these branch ailments by eradi- cating. the rcot trouttle. Put the liver into proper order and the effects of its disorder must disappear. No other result is noasible, just as when it is disordered the all. ments referred to above must ensue. Chas. F nde's Bile Beans for Biliousness are a live" specific. For liver chill, sluggish liver, defective bile fl,w, and the various results of those disorders Bile Beans are unequalled. Digestive troubles, headache, colds, shivering, b.td breathing. chest pains, female weaknesses and ailments. rheumatism, and a host of other ailments traceable ultimately to the condition of the liver and digestive sytthm vanish before them, Its thousands nf cases they have cured when all else has failed. Bile Beans are obtainable from all chemists or post free from the Bile Bean 'Manufactutine Co's. Dead English Deno 119 &amp; 120, London Wall, London, EC.. upon receipt of price one and three halfpence or two and mine.</p>
