      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>The attendance at the meetings of this society are fuLy maintained and each Sanday witnesses an acces- -sleo to the inembuishin. The number of enrolled mem- bers is now nearly 600, which is an increase upon the previous half year. The nair at the meeting of the society on Sunday sv,is occupied by Mr G Smiley, who delivered an address full of encouragement. Mr J flort.n wne the evaker for the ocaasion and hia inter- wing address was attentively lisrened to. The ser- vice was also taken part in byithe Rev John Yonge,tilr John A.i.kinson,Mr R Webb and Mr B Farrar.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church, Latchford. Toe autumnal sermons were preached on Sunday, there deing good congregations. At the morning ser- vice the minister, the Rev. J. F. Matthews, was the preacher and spoke from the words One is your Father, and ye are brethren." A sp(cial song service was held in the schoolroom in the afternoon. In the evening the My. John Yonge occupied the pulpit and delivered an earnest discourse. The collections realized a substantial amount', and were in aid of the Church Funds. Mr. Wilkinson presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Bold-street Wesley Guild.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesley Guild.</p>
        <p>A literary meeting of the above guild was held on Monday evening, when the Rev J C Neville gave an instructive lecture on " Milton's Shorter Poems," deal- ing with the early life of Milton and the circumstances under which the various poems were written. He also pointed out the thoughts embodied in each. The meet- ing, which was well attended, was presided over by Mr J Stanley Kelly.</p>
      </div>
