        <p>and so cure itself. Of all Chemists, 2s. 9d.—Six Boxes, 13s. 9d.</p>
        <p>Mr Brioox 3 proposed th 3 adoption or the minuteg. Mr PILLING seconded. _ _</p>
        <p>_ The IlevJ RIDDETTS asked whether the Chairman of the Committee was deliberately reB3rviog any statement because if so he would sugg3st that it was hardry the course to pursue on a matter of such crave importance. If the report to be sent to the Local Government Board was to b 3 of a satiifactory character he thought they I were entitled to ask the House Committee far the grounds on which they had come to that decision. Mr EIOUGEITON : The minute seems to speak for itself. ilar BROOM said the matter was discussed very fully at the last meeting of the committee. The views of various members ware elicited and he thought Mr Ward's conduct:during the 1 ast his months was very satisfactory That was the general opinion of the committee. Toe minute was passed unanimously at the meeting of the House Committee.</p>
        <p>The Rev JR RIDDETTE : WAS it a full meeting ? Mr BROOKS : There were 13 members preset. The Rev J H RonnErre said he would remind the Board that the: charges brought against Mr Ward a few months ago, were of a very grave charae- ter—so grave indeed tbst Mr Ward after a Log periael of service was practicall7 put again on trial. When it came to the question as to the action the Board should take his (Mr. Riddette's) voice and vote were given on what he might call the side of mercy. It was no vicla- Unto of confidence to say there was an expect ttion at . the time by alma members of the Board that during the Period of probation Mr. Ward woe] I either send in his Iresignation or take another appointment. Ile was aware that he WAS speakinq in the presence of the reporters, and he did not wish to say anything severe or uncalled for, but he cool I not forget that the interests of several hundred peopl?, many of them aged and infirm. were committed to their keeping. The Board had , gone to great expense on buiiiings, and they had taken great care to eqam tho bud Bogs with an efficient staff of nurses, and he t oought they would not be doing justice to the inmates if they were to continue Mr Ward indefinitely in his present position. Mr Riddette proceeded to read some extracts which he hart made from the visitors' book showing that from Jane 29h to July 9sh the Work- house Master had been absent from the house 46 hours in the evening. He did not with to say anything which might be considered uncharitable. In his present ill- ness Mr Ward had his*(Mr Riddette'sr full sympathy,but he could not conceal from the Board the fact that they had lost a considerahle pottion of the Master's time as shown by the tiguses he had given for nine days only. In conclusion Mr Riddette proposed an amendment " that the Cork be instructed to reply that the report is deferred owing to the serious illness of the Master,' Szo., in substitution of the Home Committee's recommenda- tion.</p>
        <p>There was no seconder to the ammltnent, and it fell to the ground. In r ply to a remark of Mr ALFRED JOLLEY the Rev RIDDETTE said he was not ashacn,d to be alone. Mr. BROOKS then moved that authority be given to the House Cocarnittee to norkr stroll temporary appoint- ments as they may deem necessary during the illness of the Workhouse Master, to doing so.he said the present management of the Floase was all that it should be, brit owing to the Master's absence the books were not posted up to (fate and temporary assistance was required Mr. MATHER seconded.</p>
        <p>• Mr. ASHCROFT Who wil have to pay for this extra work ?</p>
        <p>Mr. ALFRED JOLLEY : Tho Guardian?. Mr. A_SE/CROFT : Couldn': Miss Ward dd the work?</p>
        <p>The CIIAIRMAN said the resolution was giving the committee power to obtain temporary assistance if they found it neces;ary to do 89. The resolution was then passsd.</p>
        <p>THE ASSOCIATION OF POOR LAW UNIONS.</p>
        <p>Mr. PILLING moved that pursuant to resolution wiled at h, last annual board meeting that the Chair- man and the Clerk of the Board represent the Board at meetings of the fissoclation of Poor I.Aw Unions, such representatives be authorised to attend the annual meet- ing of the Coulereoce to be held at London on the 14th and 15. h days of November, as such representatives of the Board." Tali was seconded and agreed to.</p>
        <p>Warrington Field Club. There VMS a good attendance at the second meet- ing of the winter session, held in the Museum Lec- ture roam, when Mr. John Peers read a paper en- titled "A Siesta," in which he described, with characteristic hutatour, the experiences of himself and two friends during a summer cruise along the Welsh coaet in a yacht of that nanse. His style certainly did not produce uppn his audience any soporific effect, though his scientific observations were subjected to some criticism in the discussion which followed, and one speaker unkindly com- pared him with" Rip van Winkle." Mr Madeley exhibited specimens and photographs to illustrate the manufacture of gun flints as still carried on at Brandon, in Suffolk.</p>
        <p>Important to Water Consumers. In order to admit of necessary alterations on the inains,the water will be shut off fro.n 6 p.m. to-day (Saturday) until 6 p.m. o anday. Consumers would, therefore, do well to provide themselves with an adequate supply of water beforehand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>How</head>
        <p>How</p>
        <p>Strong are</p>
        <p>YouP</p>
        <p>SIR JOHN BRUNNER AT RUNCORN</p>
        <p>Sympathy with the P.S.A. Movement.</p>
        <p>Speaking at a Luiz oar in all ol the funds of Bethesda Congregational Cherch, last Friday, Sir John T Brunner Bart, M.P., said in reference to the P.S,A. movement associated vita the bazaar, that its eatirely tandenominational and unsectarian character appealed to him very strongly. It embodied an idea of his which was entirely familiar to them. He had all his iite en- deavoured to lift up his fellow men and to eusourage,as far as he eould in bumble fashion,every man and every wornan,every body of men and every body of women that were engaged in doing good to their neigebourserhe less they had of sectarian differences the better, in his opinion, they would be, and the nearer to their Creator. (Applause.) That P.S.A. was practieally the only opportunity presented to the men who j ened it, and entered into it, of treading the pathways of music, art, and 'iterative and religion, and it was because he (Sir John) was tasting the pure joys of art, music, literature and religion—it was nere he was at his best and happiest—it was beceese of this that he was entitled to say that on the present occasion he was in the right place in asking for their sympathy towards this move- ment. He was pleased to see in that room lessons in architecture and lessons in taste. They knew he was a great traveller. He had been all round the world, and every year he visited,some foreign country or other. He was greatly impressed and greatly troubled by tbe pro- gress that other countries were making in beautifying their towns. This was tak.ne place at a rate very much faster than anything that they were dresming about here. Our towns compared with the towns of France, Switz,rhand, Holland, Belgium, Austria, were shabby, most dirty, and unkempt, Sir John mentioned improvements be had seen abroad in the building of shops, where tradesmen lived above their premises, and which were absolutely palatial structures, and bore a dignified appearance. Let them by-and.by, when they could affeal to indulge their tastes—and no hoped a good many present would attain the means to do so— let each one indulge his tastes in attempting to make his own hense beautiful. They had examples in that room' which it was pleasant for them to contem- plate. It was a great pleasure to him to come back again to Runcorn after an absence of some months, and to thank them for their forbearance. He was never greatly pressed by the ladies, but be asked them to be good enough to convey his thanks to the gentlemen. , (Laughter.) He had to thank his constituents for not putting a great preasure upon him, and not demanding Vi,r7 much of his time. He had been able to enjoy since Parliament rose an amount of family life which had been a delight to him, hour by hour and day by day. He had not for eleven years past, at least, had his young people about him to such an extent as he had had dur- the last eight week,, and he came back refreshed, not only in body but in mind, to the old world of work, and in Char cordial welcome he found an extremely pleasant beginning to his winter's work. (Applause).</p>
        <p>Warrington P.S.A. at the Y.M.C.A.</p>
        <p>62rvice was held in the lecture hall of the Y.M.C.A., on Sunday Afternoon, Mr Edward. Taylor presiding. Prayer having been offered by Mr Beckett, an address was delivered by the Rev Jlf Matthews, Latchford. A solo was creditably rendered by Mrs Harding.</p>
      </div>
