      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Local Government Board and the Workhouse Master.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Local Government Board and the Workhouse Master.</p>
        <p>I meeting of this Bard was held in the Board Room at the Worlthousa on Wednesday nalrning. Mr A J Jolley presided, and there were also present :—Airs Clay, Be, Father Wilson, Rev, .1 U Riddette, Rev T R Matthews, Dr Patterson, Dr Murray. Messrs William Houghton, A Jolley, G Pilliag, J Billinge, T H Ashcroft, J Payton, T Flalton, Mather, Robinson, A Brooks, Ritchie, with J C Sutton (clerk).:</p>
        <p>SCHOOLS COMMITTEE. THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD INSPECTOR</p>
        <p>AND THE TEACHING SrAFF.</p>
        <p>The Padgate Schools Committee recommended that the Matron be allowed special leave of absence on convalescence. A report had been received from Mr Mac rsom and was submitted for the Board's perusal. The CHAIRDIAN moved the confirmation of the minutes and referred with gratification to the fact that the Matron was very much better after the very serious operation she had undergone, and was progressing very satisfactorily. (flear, bear.) , Mr. ASIICROFT seconded the adoption of the minutes, and the proposition was Lusa niinouily agreed to. At a later stage of the meeting the Rev J a RIDDETTE called attention to Mr Moor- som's report and thought the Board lost something in not having such reports read. Se would like to ask the Ciniiirman of the Schools Committee what steps they proposed to take with regard to the recommendation of the Inspector to increase the teach- ing staff ? _</p>
        <p>The CHLIRMAN : We don't propose to take any steps. The Roy J H RIDD7TTE: ssid ho would like to know how many children there were in the schools ? The CHAIRMAN ; There are three teachers for 150 children.</p>
        <p>The Rev T II RIDDETTE asked en what grounds the com- mittee justified its inaction because it seemed to him that they were inadequately fulfilling their respon- sibility to the children. The least they could do was to give them the best educational equipment. He noticed in Mr Moorsom's report the tollowing sentence "I attenied a meeting of the Sehcols Committee and brought to their notice the fact reported by Mr. Mczley that the teaching staff is insufficient and does not com- ply with the requirements of the Board of Education in National Schools." Ile hoped the importance of the subject would be considered sufficient justification for alluding to it and it would give the chairman an opportunity of justifying the action of the Committee. Tele Cimmuss : I dare3ay the committee will look , after themselves. (Laughter.) Continuing, the Chair- man said the Committee were endeavourieg to give the best possitle education. At the time ot Mr. Mozley's visit the number of children in the schools was slightly above the number which would be sanctioned in an ordinary elementary school for three teachers. Since then the number of scholars had decreased, and there was no reason to increase *he staff. If from any reason the number increased, the whole circumstances would have to be considei ed. The Committee were quite unanimous in their decision to make no change. lie wauld like to add that a very important question would have to be faced before keg. That was the question of aendiog younger children to Padgate. At the present time, the B rard did not Send children from the Workhouse to the schools until they were five years of age. If they commenced smding, the children to the ischools when they ware three years of age—and there was a good deal to lot said is favour of that policy—it would involve the erection of addi- tional buildings at Padgate. Toe committee • did not feel themselves justified in taking definite action at present. The matter was being kept in view, and be was sure the members were desirous of doing the best they could for the children placed uader their control. The Rey JEI RIDDETTE hoped it would not be pre- sum d that he raised the question in an oinfriendly spirit.</p>
        <p>The CR/trim/or said it was quite le:2lth:cults for any member to raise a question affecung the Board. THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD AND THE</p>
        <p>WORKHOUSE MASTER.</p>
        <p>The House Committee's minutes included the follow- ing :--rhe Local Government Board having by letter dated 11th o.etober, requested to be furnished with the report which the Guardians undertook to make upon the conduct of the Master of the Workhouse it is recom- mended that the Clerk be instructed to reply that a satisfactory report may be made thereon, such report however is deterred owing to the serious illness of Me Master, the present state ot whose health is such as to make a short postponement Of the action to ne taken by the Guardians desirable, also, that the Clerk be inttructel to inform the Local Government Board that the present state of the Work— house management is satisfactory, and tha Guardians have not deemed it necessary to appoint a person temporarily to unisortaka the duties of Workhouge Master. The claim of William Finch for one week's wages for alleged dismissal withoul notice, having been entered in'ths County Court, the cmeumetarlees appear to be such as to call for resistance of the claim. It is therefore ordered that the necessary witnesses attend the hearing of the c!aim, and that a solicitor usually actising in the Court be employed in that behalf.</p>
      </div>
