      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>IN ALL THEIR BRANCHES.</head>
        <p>IN ALL THEIR BRANCHES.</p>
        <p>JOHN WALKER 'iS C°.</p>
        <p>EXAMINER OFFICE, BEWSEY-STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Judging from the .infor- Foreign Competition mation conveyed in a let' in Locomotives. ter from LORD GEORG° HAMILTON to MR. BAIRD, the member of Barb's' ment for Central Glasgow, the British factories</p>
        <p>entrusted with the making of locomotives mo 4 be in a very unsatisfactory condition. Although they have been flooded with orders for some con'</p>
        <p>siderable time, they seem to have made no eflort towards expansion in the means of productive, with the result that, when asked recently to °fief for locomotives for the Indian railways, they asked 2.5 per cent. more than makers abroad, and sought double the time for delivery. Amid the general outcry that is made about British trade decrear ing, one sometimes hears a complaint that engin° for British colonies are bought abroad. Such o' complaint was made about South African rad' ways not long ago, aud it is probably in order to anticipate a similar grumble that the Socre- tary for India has now volunteered the information referred to. MR. BAIRD, to whons he addresses his letter, is a member of the great Gartsherrie firm of iron and steel manu" facturers, and therefore in a position to weigh the facts and suggestions laid down by Lord GEoria• which are of a rather important nature. It seeree that the placing of some South African orderg with British firms had filled up their producing a pacity, with the result that the Indian GevB,rll;:. ment for some time have had to postpone We': orders until those in hand were cleared out. •°1- similar story was told at a meeting of the South- Eastern and Chatham Railway about a year ag°l</p>
        <p>and it was given as a reason for the deficiency a rolling stock and consequent delays for whicl' that line has becomo notorious. The Indian rail- ways, however, were not content to sit down under an admitted deficiency, and refused to wait irr definitely for the execution of their pressing wants. The Assam-Bengal Railway, therefore, icy vited tenders for ten locomotives, and the BO Indian Railway for forty, other countries being vited to offer along with British makers. The 1.010 was that the orders were placed with a Hanover fir° at 25 per cent, less money than that asked by ad British firm, with an undertaking for delivery in about half the time. As Lord GEORGE HAMiI"</p>
        <p>TON remarks, this difference in price and can only be accounted for by the fact that th: British firms were so fall of work that they Coul'</p>
        <p>only tender for more at prohibitive rate-s.</p>
        <p>the result is not only the loss of the present ordo to the country, but the important and disagree; able fact that a trade connection has been forraee with a foreign firm which is bound in future t° act to the detriment of British manufacturers the considering of subsequent tenders. The India° Secretary says that the demand for locomotive° for India has greatly increased, and will continue</p>
        <p>to increase. In view of that fact it might be siir posed that British makers, had they been as alil°</p>
        <p>_ to their interests as, say, Americans would hal</p>
        <p>been, would have tendered for the work at reasow able figures and a moderate time limit, and once addressed themsslves to the laying down new plant and expanding their means of produe- tion to meet the increased demand. But CO petition, the main incentive to such enterprise' as exhibited in other industries, apPeaffj to be wanting in the locomotive ins°e- facture. Each railway company works itol on limited lines at its repairing 51109, building an occasional engine when there is nothinb else to do. So the work is kept going on fashioned lines, while there is not sufficient 'al for outside makers to induce them to risk roue. capital outlay on a large scale. This is where ' American, with his huge combinations and lIPPle' cation of modern machinery, .has the advantag? and is coming in as a serious competitor to the British manufacturer. There is evidently t°°lll for a complete revolution in the British mant'fa: tura ; and now that colonial dereand is likelY to become healthy there is a good dpening for fittaa with the requisite knowledge, enterprise tb° capital to undertake the work. Lord 0•043'°0 HAMILTON points out, with regard to a comPlaillo from the makers that there is no attempt to °recto a standard of quality which would be a guidere.. makers in offering tenders, that he is quite P- pared to move in the direction of obtaining fr°o, the Indian Gnernment some form of standaripars; tion, and also a forecast of the requirem3uts for a year in advance. With this valuable assistau rders towards keeping in this country the railwaY ° _ay for other parts of the Empire, nvinufacturersiP. surely lae prepared to bestir themselves, and ten crease the means of production on the bee. t-1°1" ter°s" principles, in oraer to retain, in their own 1° hat is a as well as the interest of the country, w most valuable industry.</p>
        <p>-</p>
      </div>
