      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>The press are unanimou Views irement.of Buller's in their sympathy for St Ret REDVERS BULLER RS the Men idol, the unfortunate military man who has ttlived his usefulness, and closed ingloriously a the opening chapters of which promised itter things. With such qualifications of regret- -11 sympathy it is not worth while to quarrel, but, 'he, critics of the General try to argue that his retirement justifies all that they have said tetrimental to his character and military abilities is allowable to say that they are acting without at, and adopting as a proved result what was .iothing more than a foregone conclusion. The memorandum from the War Office did not in the least recall or impugn the confidence of his super- iors which the gallant Gmeral's appointment to the First Army Corps implied. It reads rather as if action had been taken on the pretext of a purely technical offence against military discipline in order to pacify the outcry that had been raised against him in certain military circles and among a section of the press. In this view there is an element of weakness visible which it is to be heped is not to form a part of the new regime. The wobbling disposition of the Govern- , anent has become characteristic in other depart- ments of State; if it is to enter into the business , of military promotion and appointment a new danger will arise. It is a fortunate circumstance , in connection with the present case that both Generals FRENCH and HILDYARD have come to the front in their profession by brilliant and meritorious action in the field, without social or other extrinsic considerations. But it is not diffi- cult to imagine a case in which less worthy successors might claim positions to which they had not been appointed; and if a clamour in the press is to be sufficient for the removal of those who stand in their way a serious step will be taken to dangerous methods which have had results only too mischiev- ous in the R3public across the Channel. Many journals write as if justice claimed the sacrifice of General BULLER, and was now satisfied; but this method of throwing a man to the wolves is not English in its conception, and, if repeated too Often, will be apt to lead to evils which will far outweigh any temporary satisfaction with the action just taken by the heads of the War Dapar t- tient. The Memorandum does not state that tho authorities are now satisfied that they made a mistake in appointing General BULLER. If it had they would have stood self-condemned, and Mr. Baceenicit's resignation might well have followed the removal of the General. But they stand equally condemned if, still convinced of his capac- ity and worthiness, they have sacrified him to ' gratify his enemies. Yet it is difficult to find any Other construction to place upon their action. They do not say they have dismissed the General because he suggested to General WHITE that he might have to surrender Ladysmith. They have relieved him of his command in consequence 01 the speech he made, and after taking into consideration all the circumstances and explanations which he furnished. It will be, Observed that the GENERAL has not been asked to answer for his conduct to a court-martial; he has Volunteered some statements—not, perhaps, so clear as they would have been if he had read the message to which he referred, but which he con- sidered sufficient to justify him against virulent one-sided Lattacks which were appearing in the newspapers. Without further ado he is asked to</p>
        <p>career</p>
        <p>forced</p>
        <p>Ivarram</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>send in his resignation, and when he declines he iS. at once placed upon half-pay. It may be that h.'s acknowledgment that in certain unfortunate ctircurnstances he counselled the surrender of a ritish fortress called for such drastic treatment ; but if that were the true and only reason, why have the Government so long delayed this Measure ? They must have known all about the Circumstance long ago, and the whole episode •seems.to point to a hasty, shifty, cowardly policy, Which, however much it has been in evidence and has been deplored in other departments of public administration, is most of all unfortunate as a Part of the new policy of military administration.</p>
        <p>It is not surprising to learn that a Bill for the exclusion of alien Anarchists from the soil of the tnited States has been prepared, and that "hav- ing been carefully examined by prominent mem- bers of the Administration and Congress no fault is found with it." It is clearly in the public in- terest that such a measure should be passed, as stringent in its terms as possible, and that the question of its efficiency in operation should be left for the future to decide. It will doubtless, therefore, be enacted with the full force of the Legislation that "no alit). Anarchist shall here- after be permitted to land at any part of the United States ;" and if this regulation could be carried out to the letter no doubt Anarchism would cease to be a danger, seeing that America, which produces so many notable things, has hitherto neon dependent on the "effete monarchies" of Europe for its supply of Anarchists. However, what distinguishing mark is there by which the Anarchist may be recog- nised ? It is expressly laid down that political refugees should not be excluded as such,and politi- cal refugees are generally persons with views which are admittedly advanced, and in most cases distinctly disquieting to comfortable middle-class Opinion. The revolutionary used to be known by his lon g cloak and soft felt hat much the worse for wear; but Czolgosz was not ill-dressed, nor Was there anything noticeably criminal in his aPpearance and bearing. The usefulness of the law will be found in the result to depend greatly ou the activity of the secret service agents of the government, but it would be ridiculous to sup- Pose that they could be in touch with all the Coutinent, and be able to notify Washington of the sailing of every insignificant hanger-on of the Movement.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>The " tipping system" is one of the abomina- tions which the present method of local govern- ent mistakenly permit!. There could scarcely be any more serious menace to the health of a c.onununity than to allow the filling up of dei.res- slons in the ground by the tipping of ashpit refuse, especially when that refuse is afterwards 11„sed as a foundation for dwelling-houses. Yet Inis sort of thing is permitted over and over again. t is so easy and convenient to dump the rubbish 4.nto a hollow. Perhaps it smells at first, but that 1.4 forgotten when the lands gets built upon ! '3llOl are the specious arguments which weigh authorities careless as to the health the people under their charge. The "kedical Officer of Health for Bradford lays the 14:1101e of the blame for a scarlet fever outbreak there at the door of the" tip system," an ad- ditio nal and not less serious oojection to which ii round in the subsidence of this unstable founda- °ll) causing the breaking oi pipes and con- esCillent inefficient drainage. A ring—or rather o„ -Ing—example of this C )US and danger- SYStes is to be found at Stockton Heath, ~Ithere one of the low-lying fields along Fairfield- .. 3a 71'4 forisome time been the scene of persistent ref nraPing," the reftne being certainly ash-pit use but incontestably not composed solely of ash" the Loud complaints have at last resulted in "PPing being put a stop to, but not before eios-Y tOna of foul rubbish have been piled up in 'OE4 Pr"ilnity to dwellings. Probably it will, "g, be built upon, but if the Stockton Heath</p>
        <p>oing • ,</p>
        <p>!:drain•</p>
        <p>stril</p>
        <p>bnoxii</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>Parochial Committee had been alive to its duties such a disgraceful condition of things would never have been allowed to exist. Even now the Com- mittee would only be making some amends by insisting on the removal of the rubbish to the des- tructors at Warrington. Instead the probability is that the land will be let as an "elig,ible building site and building operations sanctioned on the identical spot. We shall note events.</p>
        <p>Last week witnessed the celebration of the Jubilee of Wycliffe Chapel—a place of worship which stood on the site of the present Wycliffe Church—but which was found too small for the accommodation of tho congregation after an exist- ence of twenty years. The celebration was made the more notable by the interesting ceremony of placing the memorial stones of the new building which it is hoped will increase the usefulness of the church and its numerous organizations by providing them with suitable rooms for 'social and religious purposes. A robust Nonconformity has always been associated with Wycliffe and it has taken a prominent part in the religious life of the town. There is still plenty of room in Warrington for aggressive Christian work and when the new building is completed, Wyclifle should be • better equipped to embark upon new movements and to increase its influ- ence for good.</p>
        <p>The Warrington tobacconists have shown them- selves alive to the difficulties which retailers will have to face in consequence of the new phase of trading known as the 'combine' principle. In order to meet the new situation it is becoming more and more evident that tobacconists will have to com- bine both the productive and the distributive ele- ments of the trade. This has been realised by the United Kingdom Tobacco Dealers Alliance, an organization of tobacconists with members in every part of the British Isles. The Alliance has received an offer from a leading firm in the trade, which has successfully com- bined production and distribution in the tobacco business, to manufacture for mem- bers of the Alliance at a charge of thrse per cent, above the cost of manufacture and to divide the profits at the year's end amongst the members. By this scheme every retailer will become his own manufacturer, and will have a share in the manufacturing profits. Tho firm in question offer to manufacture any tobaccos asked for and it is suggested that the tobacconist should cultivate lines of his own and thus raise himself from the unenviable position of being the pur- veyor of other people's brands to that of a dealer in his own special goods. The scheme was laid before a meeting of tobac :onists in Warrington last Thursday and approval was expressed of the scheme. By this means, the tobacconists hope to successfully combat the designs of the foreign tobacco combines upon their trade.</p>
        <p>1 Beg,. 13t1/11, C. oas ttre, • ref., 'oink "se k ashes vs_ I'm 'lt, tip's' ae, bt, tn. ,3f_ tons t nig, be bui t t</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT. A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Taken in connection with the Temperance subjects discussed at the annual meeting of the General Council of the United Kingdom Alliance this week,the official returns of the London Asylums for the past year, which have just been published, serve to emphasize the evils of the drink traffic and to show the cause of our increasing insanity. The returns give the following as the probable causes of insanity among the patients of the asylums referred to: Intemperance in drink, 584 ; domestic troubles, 198; adverse circumstances, including business anxieties, 191 ; mental anxiety, worry, and over- work, 166, love affairs, 55; religious excitement,34. It is not easy to deduce inferences with any degree of certainty, perhaps, from the tabular form giving the callings of the patients, since these include labourers, clerks, tailors, printers, painters, carpen- ters, shoemakers, butchers, seamen, sailors, &amp;c; bu it is worthy of note that 86 men and 477 women werb of no occupation. Reference to the districts from which the patients came bear out much of what has been said in ',these columns about the housing problem. The returns show that the largest number of lunatics came from those districts in which overcrowding and poverty most prevail. But alcohol is unquestionably, as Dr Stanfield,the medi- cal officer of Bexley Heath Asylum says, a leading factor in the causation of insanity, although not the only factor, and there is manifestly ample work for social reformers in removing the causes which make for lunacy.</p>
        <p>Very tardily, and not before the cry had gone up from all parts of the provinces as to the manner in which the Imperial Yeomanry were treated by the War Office on their return from the war had be- come a public scandal, have new regulations been issued with a view to meet the case of those who have not received their arrears of pay after weeks of waiting in penury. Thb delay in paying the claims of the men has been a disgrace to the nation, and it is for many reasons regrettable that the whole country should have rung with the wails of disappointment and suffering soldiers be- fore any attempt was made to do justice to the men The latter volunteered for service at a critical juncture, and many of them threw up good appointments in order to serve their country. The public had been led to understand that whatever other fault could be found in the Army organisa- tion, that of the obdervation of regulations was not one of them. But here it was found that even the usual red-tape which might have spared the Yeo- manry much distress, and in many instances abso- lute poverty, broke down. Even in time of war it might be expected that when whole companies are discharged some one would be in a position tO issue pay certificates. But the result of this neglect has been that the War Office could not identify the claims of the Yeomanry, and every- body by this time knows bow difficult it is to approach that Department without all the neces- sary, and many unnecessary, documents. The War Office is not blamed for taking ample precautions to guard against frauds, but it might reasonably have been expected that something would be done to relieve the men's anxiety and immediate wants. It is not yet known whether the new regulations will prove satisfactory, but if they do it is a pity they were not put into operation before the hard- ships of the Yeomen had become so widespread a scandal.</p>
        <p>In the west of England especially, the announce- ment of the supersessin of General Buller has been received with general astonishment and regret. Whatever may have been the unwisdom of his recent speech, the country as a whole will not for- get that the man who landed in South Africa "a General without an army," as he himself expressed it, had allotted to him a gigantic and stupendous task, that he did not shrink from it, that he went to Natal of his own choice, and undertook a task that was well nigh hopeless and that he was able to retain the loyalty and affection of his troops in a most:wonderful way. Hence, without criticising the step which Lord Roberts has thought it right to take, it is but just to say that Sir Redvers Buller takes with him into retirement the sympathy of his fellow-countrymen everywhere, and that he has won their gratitude by more than forty years of brave and conscientious service. The important statement issued from the War Office late on Tuesday night was that after carefully con- sidering the statements of Sir Redvers Buller it had been decided to relieve him of his command at Aldershot, ani he is accordingly placed on half- pay, while General Freuch, when he can be spared from South Africa, will take over the Aldershot command. The public will now be as anxious as ' General Buller was to know who is the distin- guished writer of the letters in the Times, signed 1" A Reformer."</p>
        <p>An indication of the coming " war" in the Tobacco Trade is making its appearance in the shop windows of some of the larger towns of the pro- vinces. It takes the form of a small bill bearing 1 the headline. "A burning question," and after quoting from trade journals as to the probable course the struggle would take between the Eng- lish " combine" and the American trust. declares that the latter aimed at making " Englisn smokers veritable slaves of the ring," smoking only that which the trust permitted to be smoked, buy- ing only as the Americans wished, and paying what these trans-Atlantic aggressors liked to charge. Then follows an appeal to British smokers not to be led by 'these Yankee rings and trusts," but to support English industries. This is the kind of warning that may already be seen in many of the tobacco shops in Liverpool.</p>
        <p>Many of the boroughs where the housing ques- tion presses have been anxious to increase the in- terest taken by the local authorities in the condi- tion of the people living in slum dwellings, and now they have the opportunity of giving voice and point to their desire. The chances of doing ireally good work in this direction are at present very plentiful, and should not be allowed to pass unim- proved. Where there are contests anent the Ist of November elections especially, they will be en- abled to suggest the kind of policy which they would have the Town Councils to duly formulate. It is for the electors to propose and the elected to' thus dispose of the matter, local circumstances per- mitting, and it has to be borne in mind that such an opportunity for moving in the matter comes, like Christmas, but once a year, and that if rate- payers are silent now they must for twelve months at least hold their tongues if they would retain credit for personal and general consistency.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY OCTOBER 25 1901.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY OCTOBER 25 1901.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.I</p>
        <p>One of the ord world poetic customs which is fast dying out in this matter-of-fact prosaic century was observed on Saturday at Halton with all the historical quaintness and picturesqueness. The Halton Court Leet is one of the oldest ceremonials existing in this part of the c3untry and should be prized by the inhabitants as well ai by local anti- quarians as a survival from the misty ages of the past. In its day the Court Leet performed an honourable and useful function. Its ephors took the place of our County Court, its burleymen, bad charge of the roads, its ale-tasters kept up the quality of the liquors at local taverns, its jury in- quired into the sudden death of any inhabitant ; its high steward was virtually the justice of the peace. it can thus be easily seen tacit in the days when might was right this local authority played no small part in the well government of the dis- trict under its control. And the importance in feudal times of the Halton Leet is evinced by its range over the townships from Widnes to the con- fines of Northwich.</p>
        <p>Over and over again in these columns has the necd for a cottage hospital for Runcorn been amply demonstrated. The only thing which has hitherto prevented its erection bad been lack of funds. Owing , however, to the heroic exertions of the Runcorn Cycle Club which has nobly pioneered the way the permanent possession of a hospital is only a matter of time. For some five years nom it has steadfastly Can- centrated its efforts- in this direction and having been backed up by many influential gentlemen who have generously opened their purees there is now a fair sum in hand. This will doubt- less be greatly augmented on Saturday when the annual Runcorn Cycle Pa-ade and horse show will be held. Let everyone do his best and a permanent cottage hospital is assured.</p>
        <p>In the truest sense of the word the annual distri- bution of prizes at the Technical Institute on Wednesday was a success. There was a crowded meeting of scholars and adults; all were enthusl— astic ; the speeches were, on the whole, very good; nod everyone struck a harmonious note on the importance of educating the rising generation. The prize list was a fairly lotig one and while it showed that some were only at the bottom of the ladder of educa- tional fame, it also showed that there were or.e or two who were treading the highest rungs. They have won their position only by industry and un- tiring perseverance and they justly deserve all the encomiums passed upon them by Sir John Brunner the head ma-ter, and the o'her speakers. One fact should be brought out clearly before the minds of the ratepayers. The schools are theirs. They are to equip their children with mental weapons to fight life's battles in the coming years. Runcorn, and Great Britain for that matter, will be behind or 1 in advance of other towns 0! countries proportion- ately as she neglects or pays attention to education There is now a glorious chance given to the inhabi- tants, which could not be given to the youths of ten years ago, and it is for them to decide whether they will support an institution capable of doing so much good to them and theirs or ostracise them- selves from the education fount from which flows truth and power.</p>
        <p>The princely generosity of the Highfield Tannery Company has again manifested itselt. Speaking at the close of the meeting at the Institute, the managing director, Mr Posnett, astonished and delighted his hearers by announling that his firm were prepared to give £5O towards a new dynamo for the Technical Institute. That was not the first exhibition of generosity and the people have need to be grateful that they possess individuals and firms wh3 are fiD open handed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE. Distribution of Prizes.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN TECHNICAL INSTITUTE. Distribution of Prizes.</p>
        <p>Speech by Sir John Brunner.</p>
        <p>• - The annual distribution of prizes in connection with the day and evening classes was held at the Technical Institute on Wednesday evening. There was a very large attendance of scholars and adults, Mr R II Posnett, J P. occupied the chair and he was supported by Sir John Brunner, Messrs A R Nor- man, F J Norman, JPritchard, S Taylor, J Wilson, W Handley, W J Lightburn, J M Frith, F Hutton- E Masshall and Wilding. The Chairman said he felt great pleasure in address ing them as fellow townsmen because the Institute belonged to the town and was free from all sectarian differences. It was free:from any bitterness of any sort and Was worthy of the support of everybody in the town. He was delighted to see so many of them to witness one of the most interesting eyeats of the whole year in connection with the technical schools and to welcome such an educationalist as Sir John Brunner,—(applause), who took such a deep interest in the welfare of young people. Oae of the things they should consider was he question whether they were getting the very best that might be got from their schools. He believed as years went by that the Institute would became the most important building in the town. He felt so strongly as a com- mercial man, of the absoluo necessity of their doing everything they could in the town to support the 1 , Institute because he believed not on y the wel- fare of the town, but the welfare of their , country depended on the higher and more 1 accurate and scientific training of their youths. Thorn ought to be better scholarships. Not only should ; a workingman's intelligent lad be sont to their school, , but they wanted to enable him to be sent to the school ; without loss, If they were t) take is foremoit place it I would be by training to the highest point the intelli- s,sance of the boys and girls in their midst. Every one of them should be so trained in all the great aspects of their national life that they ; might form an aecurate lodgment for themselves. So he asked them to weigh and consider everything read that they might form an accurate judgment and become ; =rat illy stronger as they grew older. (Applause). i Mr. Lawrenson, head master then submitted his re- I port. Hs said that two years ago when Sir John Brun- I leer gave the prizes away in that building he expressed the hope that some day they would have some one who would go from that school to the university college, or some place of higher education than , that school and so carry on the work already I begun there. This year, he was glad to Say, i they had succeeded in doing that. One of their boys, Joseph Stubbs, had been successful in getting a County scholarship which would take him to the Liverpool University. He intended to devote himself to chemistry and to take the honours B. Sc. course sod be hoped in ithree or four years that he would have developed into an intelligent and experienced chemist, and do some useful work. There were several Cambridge local successes. Three bad passed the Cambridge junior local and. 13 the preliminary suc- cessful in gaining a Ipoocsaitt.ionOneinhadthbeeen Civil service being placed twelvth out of 13 successful candidates. Three had succeeded in gaining advanced certificate for mathematics, Joseph Stubbs, Joseph Nickson and Harry Hough. The success of Stubbs after three years holding of a County S.oholar- ship lin Class I showed the value of the scholarship system and he would continue holdinz a County Scholarship of a different class. He hoped others would follow in his footsteps. In addition to the County scholars in the classes they had scholars holding Brunner Scholarships given by the kindnes3 of Sir John to enable a youth who had been two years in the school to centinue a third year. Two years was hardly long enough and in a school of that sort it was neces- sary to continue their course three years. With regard to the general work of the schools it WAS giving more satisfaction than at any time since it bad been Opened four years ago. The number of scholars was not as large as it should be. If there were more it could be worked more economically. Hotter value could be got out of a large than a small number. (Applause.) Mr MarEhall read the general report and said that as good education was being given in that school as any Liverpool one. Is was doing a grand work for the young people and should be supported by the towns- people with enthusiasm. (Applause.) Sir John, who was received with applause, said he had been previously thinking what was the broad effect upon the lives of the people of Runcorn of an institution like the Technical School. One of the good things was that it gave them a different basis from which to con- sider each other. They were far too much in the habit of paying consideration to the individual possessed of wealth. Now the idea of distinguishing between the rich and the:: poor in Mr Lawrenson's hands absolutely dis- appeared, and the youth generally got to the top by force of brain and character. It was the boy and girl of true sound character as well as the boy and girl of ability that got inevitably to the top and had considera- tion paid to him or her by their fellows. The subjects taught would tend to diminish among them that vulgar worship of wealth. The aim of amassing wealth rapidly was more and more possessing the peo- ple. Education was a very powerful weapon against this evil which they all deplored. The Technical In- stitute produced the feeling of equality amongst parents as well as the children. there could be no real and true society in the beat sense without the feel- ing of equality. When Joseph Stubbs went to the Liverpool University and got to the top</p>
        <p>of his career people would not ask what was his father. They would honour him for the work he had done and the example he had set. He had no doubt he ;mild take his degree and hoped he would then find himself in a situation where he could earn a salary equivalent to hie ability. He hoped the day would come when the managers of works and commercial business would themselves sufficiently appreciate edu- cation in others to put such qualified men into the honourable dignified and well paid position as was their due. He bad recently heard a very distinguished statesman describe what Germany had been doing during the last generation and to him it had been absolutely terrifying. In England the commercial organizations did not employ scientific help as they ought to do. It was a many years since the London and North-Western railway with a capital of £120,000,000 spent £3OO a year in scientific assistance. He told one of their directors when be heard this that there was not a little bit of a Swiss railway that did not spend more. He counselled rho director t 3 advise his colleague to multi- ply tnat £3OO a year by 10 and in a few years more by 20. It would be an economical expenditure for them. Brunner, Mond, and Company did not carry their busi- ness on in that fashion. They would not be there if they did. He thought 60/113 provision might be made for the teaching of navigation in Runcern for it wou'd be of great assistance to those who wished for a seafar- ing life. Referring to German Sir John said that in brewing the Germans copied the English methods and worked them out scientifically training their young men in colleges and afterwards placing them in god positions. In the manufacture of explosives the Germans hal found out a better explosive which would multiply the life of a gun by nearly three, and as the guns were extremely expensive and their lives comparatively short, they would see how impor- tant a matter for r:113 national strength and welfare the study of explosives was. The Germans never spent a penny save in the hope of getting tw,opence back. He was in terror of being beaten by them and be hated to be beaten. The Technical Institute taught the study of science and the stady of literature as well, and this would bring them into touch with the best minds of the world. (Ipplause.) Re wished the Institution every success.</p>
        <p>-------- Sir John then diStribnted the pri2 SS to the following scholars : Ernest Andrews. 'geometry, 2 elem ; William M Arm- strong, applied Mechanics, 2 elem ; 1 steam adv ; Ada Aspay French, 2 mega ; Martha A Aston, 1. dressmaking( ; James H Austin, 2 geometrical drawing; Joseph H Banks, I 2 model and 2 L and ii.; 1 geometrical draw- log; James Banner, shorthand 60 words; John F Beck L and S; Emily Id Blundell, 2 dressmaking; Clara Bradshaw, 1 Cookery ; Beatrice Britiand, 2 dress- making ; James P Carter. 2 freehand and 1 model ; Minnie Carter, 1 dressmaking ; Thomas Clare, 2 elem French ; T J Clarke, shorthand, 60 words; Elizabeth A Cooke, 2 cookery': Arthur Cropper, building construction 2 elem , Joseph S Cunnah maohinn construction, 2 elem ; Thomas Curzon. Naval architecture, 2 ads', and mathematics 2 stage 1 ; Harry L Dale, machine construction the elem . • Beatrice Eyre, dressmaking 2 ; Beatrice Fleet. dressmaking 1 ; Gowen Fryer ,maohine construction 1 elem . Stephen Gale, theo chemistry 2 Oen:: ; Ralph Gibbs. shorthand 90 words ; Thomas Green, machine construction, 2 adv ; Rowland Hadfield and James H Hamilton building construction. 2 elem ; Alice 31 Handley cookery with distinction ; Florence B Handley. 1, cookery s Bessie Harrop, 1, cookery ; Florence 11 Hill, dressmaking, 2 ; Lilly Hill. Freehand, 2 ; John S Hitchmough, shorthand. 60 words; Walter Howard, art teachers' certificate ' • Elizabeth Johnson, dressmaking. 2; Emily 0 Johnson, French elem, 1 ; Frederick Killcross. geometers', 2 elem; Mabel Leathwood. dressmaking, 1 T Lightfoot. mathematics, 2, stage 1; G A Mack. Theo chemistry 1 elem, practical 1 elern L A Meredith, French, 2 eiemen ; Sidney Millington shorthand 6) words ; Hannah Moores, dressmaking 1; Thomas Mort, building construction 2 ads', geometry 2 elem ; George Pennington, carpentry and joinery 2, ordinary G ; George H Powell, machine construction 2 adv. ream 2 ads', applied mechanics 1 elem ; geometry 1 &amp;ern ; L J Prince machine construction 1 elem; T Prince. 2 elem, michine construction; William Ray, machine construction. 1 elem ; Walter Reynolds, magnetism and electricity, 2 elementary; W J dandbach, building con struction, 1 eieni ; Annie Sefton, dressmaking 1 ; Frank Sherlock, geometrical drawing, 2 elem ; perspeetive, 2 elem: Laura Treacivrell, 2 cookery ; J 'Ft Tweerieli, machine construction, 2 ads'; le E Ward. light and shade 2 ; Elizabeth Wells. dressmaking 2; F W Wilkinson. shorthand 60 words ; John Williams, steam. 2 ads'; Ruda Williams, dressmaking, 2; Dora E Wilson. dressmaking, 2 ;TN Withington, mathematics, 2 stage 1; J W Wiswall, naval architecture 2 ads' ; H A Woodier machine construction 1 elem.</p>
        <p>At the close t-h-e- -usual votes of thanks were placed;</p>
        <p>HIGHFIELD TANNERY COMPANY'S GENEROSITY. Mr Posnett in reply said it would be a good idea if every one of the books could be stamped with the Arms of the town. He thought the chairman should offer a reward and he would leave, as a precedent, something in the hands of Mr Lawrenson for him to use at his dis- cretion. He had heard that the Institute wanted a gas engine and dynamo which would cost about £l5O. On behalf of his firm hs was prepared to give the first £5O. (Applause.) The msetiog concluded with the singing of the Nati )aal Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUMRDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUMRDIANS.</p>
        <p>rhe fortnightly meeting of this authority was held at Dutton Worinouse oa Tuesday aftsrnoon. Prerent Messrs J Stokes (in the chair), A R Norman (vice- chairman), Rev Dr Gastaldi, Messrs S Taylir, T Lea, T Wood, J Douglas, the members of the Rural District Council with Messrs G F Ashton (cierk),and WE Hough (assistant clerk).</p>
        <p>TELEPH7NIC COMMUNIC&amp;TION_ FROM DUTTON WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>The secretary et the G.P.O. London wrote as tollow's-: —With reference to your letter of the 7th August I am dircc'ed to state that he isiprepared to provide and main- tain and renew when necessary a double wire telephone circuit between Dutton Workhouse and Runcorn with loops to the Medical Officers Home, Clerks office and Police Station, Runcorn, for an annual rental of £52 151 under an agreement for five year. The charge for a I double wire telephone from Dutton Workhouse to the Police Station, Runcorn, with loops to the Medina' Officers home and the Clerk's office woul I be £4l 153 per annum under an agreement for 5 year,. The Clerk said that under the preseut arnngetnrnts they could not get into communication with any other place except those which were within the Runcorn area. The General Post Office Engineer had assured him that the National Telephone Company coul4 not get com- munication with the workhouse onithe ground of arrarge ment between the Post Office and themselves.lt seemed a veryletrange thing that the Government should tako over a portion of the telephone and then not be in a position to get into communication with the other portion of the system.</p>
        <p>-Mr. Wo9(iward moved that the question be deferred. Mr. Moseley Williams seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>DESERTIOTI BY A MOTHER. Colonel J El HammerBlw, Chief Constable of Cheshire wrote uader date 15th Octsb.r that he would direct inquiries to be made to ascertain the whereabouts of the mother who had deserted her child at Kinzsley by leaving the sune at the house of Mrs. Parker. . _</p>
        <p>The Clerk said nothin further had since transpired. MAINTENANCE OF PAUPER LUNATICS</p>
        <p>Mr. A. [Tor/thy, clerk to the committee, County Asylum, Chester, wrote to the elZect that the mainten- ance of pauper lunatics in the agrthirn for the quarter ending Decembor was fixed at 7s 71 per head per week. The Clerk said that the cost was 7s 10i1 last quarter. VACCINATION FEES.</p>
        <p>M; Woodward said tiat the chatees for vaccination far the quarter ending October were SR foliows :—Dr McDougall, £27 Is ; Dr Bu:ton, £lB 41 Id ; Dr Car- ruthers '£2 10s. For the corresponding period last year DrMeDDugall wart paid £3O 104 and Dr Burton £l6 10s. He moved the statement be adopted. Mr Wright seconded and the motion was passed. PERAMBULATORS FOR BABIES. The Ladies' Visiting Committee reported that they had visited the wards and found everything in a eatis- fartny condition. They suzgested it v.ould be greatly to the advantage and well-being of the babies if they were taken out, and thought perambulators should be bought for the purpose. Mr. Moseley Williams said they wanted some good strong ones. There were quite five babies in arms now and two were merely toddling. He proposed that four I be purchased.—Passed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>.....•1...1..........0</head>
        <p>.....•1...1..........0</p>
        <p>Sr. PETER'S CHURCH BELLS NEWTON. Affairs in Connection with St. Peter's Church, at Newton. are in a rather mixed state at present. After agood deal of labour, and through the generosity of well-wishers of the:Church, an excel- lent peal of bells has been provided and fixed in the tower. A team of ringers have practiced assi- duously for some time under a capable instructor and they have made themselves efficient in so much that they have for a Sunday or two been allowed to ring the bells for morning and evening service. During last week, however, the Vicar told the rinzers that the bells must not be rung again but only chimed. The ringers refused to chime them, and the Vicar was put to the neces- sity of engaging outside help, with the result that the bells were only chimed on Sunday. The stop- page has been brought about by a resident who, it is said has secured the co-operation of the Bishop on the ground that the noise created by the ring- ing of the bells is a nuisance and detrimental to the health of himself and family. Consequently the bells, about which SO much fuss was made a short time back, are practically of no use. There is con- siderable indignation on the part of the congrega- tion, and it remains to be seen whether the action of one person will prevail against the united voice of the congregation. The congregation are being asked to subscribe towards paying for one of the bells, but they state they will not subscribe to an article Which has become of no use. At present the Vicar has prevailed, but if be stands out against the wishes of the congregatioc,he is in for a very lively time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>_NELSON-STREET MISSION,</p>
        <p>On Thursday evening a tea party and concert were held in connection with the mission, at which there was a large attendance. Mr. Edward Boden, an old worker, or...cupied the chair, and delivered an encouraging address. A good programme was sub- mine&amp; Tand at the close fruit and sweets were dis- tributed. RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>A public meeting in support of the Progressive Candidates will be held in Brunswick school room. on Monday evening next at 8 o'clock. Mr F J Norman will occupy the chair. The Rev 3 llirst Hollowell, a well-known authority on education will address the meeting and the candidates will also be present. SUDDEN DEATH OF A RUNCORN CAPTA/N.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning Mr Ralph Atherton, a well- known Runcornian, died very suddenly on board the river flat Florence, of which he was captain and j-iint owner. He had been in indifferent health for sometime, and had been under the treatment of Dr Robinson. The deceased, who was about GO years of age,was highly respected in the town. His wife predeceased him a short time ago. An inquest has been deemed unnecessary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RUNCORN HARRIERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>I This club commenced the season on Saturday by taking a run from their new headquarters, the Park Pavilion. It was an ideal day for a cross- country run, but there was only a moderate turn out. The trail was laid across the C3mmon in the direction of Rocksavage, and thence by Sutton Thrnbridge. Capital country was found from this place to Stockham. The pace was made fast con- sidering that this was the initial run. The first man to land home was Hopkins, followed by Ley- land and Povey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN FREE LIBRARY.</p>
        <p>Mrs Jones, the librarian, reports that during the month of September, when the library was open 21 days, the number of issues was as follows : Theology 30, philosophy 4. science 49, history, &amp;c., 79 ; poetry 9, fiction 1,333, miscellaneous works 137, French literature 3, reference works 48, music 14 ; total 1,706—an increase of 39 in the reference and a decrease of 162 in the lending departments, as compared with the corresponding period of last year. RUNCORN CHURCH MISSION'</p>
        <p>The mission commenced on Sunday. The pulpits at the Parish Church, Holy Trinity, St. Michael's Mersey Mission, and the Weston and Weston Point churches were occupied by special preachers, and In the afternoon service 3 for men were held. The attendance at each place of worship was most gratifying to those who have been instrumental in organizing the mission. Services were held at all the churches each evening in the week. EXEMPLARY SENTENCE ON A RUNCORN WOMAN. At the Runcorn Police Curt on Friday before Mr John Ockleston (in the chair', and 31r E Imison, Sarah Helsby, Runcorn, was charged with frequent- ing Regent-street with intent to commit a felony. —Sergeant Gregory stated that she saw prisoner trying to reach down a lady's hand bag from Kirkham's shop. When questioned by him she said she had nothing to eat and what could she do. Prisoner was commi ted to gaol for three months hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>DEATH OF AN OLD RUNCORN TRADRSWOMAN.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF AN OLD RUNCORN TRADRSWOMAN.</p>
        <p>On Monday there was committed to the grave at Warburton Parish Church the mortal remains of Mrs Jane Broadhurst, widow of the late Mr William Broadhurst, of High-street, Runcorn, whose death occurred on Thursday aftsr a very brief illness. The deceased lady who was 68 years of age, had conducted the business of a grocer and provision dealer for many years and was highly respected. The service was conducted by the Rev J Warbur- ton, vicar of Warburton. A large number of floral offerings were made by relatives and frieniq.</p>
        <p>STEVENSON, THE CHAMPION BILLIARD PLAYER;</p>
        <p>AT RuNconx._</p>
        <p>Mr 11 W Stevenson, the champion billiardist of the United Kingdom,played two games of 800 up at the Liberal Club on Wednesday, his opponent being Mr P Quinn, Widnes, who was the recipient of 400 points, The match in the afternoon, Stevenson won with the greatest ease by 144 points, his biggest break being 121, Quinn showing very poor indeed not doing himself justice. In the evening there was a crowded audience and the spectators were treated to a sensational finish. Quinn got to within two of game when he broke down at a red winner. Stevenson picked up his cue and ran out with a splendidly executed 46 break. Quinn made breaks of 51, 41, 36,and 35, and Stevensen breaks of 199, 95, 95, 83, 69, 61, and 2of 51. The game was admirably marked by He Elarry Wright. THE RUNCORN COTTAGE HOSPITAL,</p>
        <p>As will be seen from our advertisement column the annual cycle and horse parade will be held on Saturday, the proceeds of which will go to swell the coffers of the R.C.R.F. Already the Runcorn Cycle Club has been instrumental in collect- ing £414 163 ai towards the noble ob- ject in visw and with the subscriptions of local ladies and gentleman a grand total of £1,209 15s Oct has been raised. More money, how- ever, is sti I required, and on Saturday the collec- tors will expect every loyal Runcarnian to unbutton his purse to help on the work. All par- ticulars may be obtained from Mr Booth, secretary, who will be only too pleased to get everyone into harness. For further information see advertise- ment.</p>
        <p>_ At Keighley on Tuesday, war female inmates of the workhouse were sent to gaol for periods varying from a fortnight to a month for overstaying leave granted to enable them to go to the Roman Catholic Chapel—they spent their time in drinking instead—or for leaving the house without leave after returning. AN EXTRAORDINARY SIIICIDE.-9 shocking, case of suicide occurred at Leamington about noon yesterday, the unhappy man being D inlet Self°. an engineer and fitter,who lived with his wife in Lansdowne-itreet. SElfe had been depressed for a considerable time past, and was known to have made an attempt on his life some years again.On Wednesday whilst alone in his workshop he blew his brains out,using a niece of piping, which he must have charged and then ignited it having placed it against his bead. He was about 67 years of age.</p>
        <p>CRAW FORD'S CREAM CRACKERS ,</p>
        <p>CRISP AND CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Council was held at Dutton Workhouse on Tuesday afternoon. Present, Mr J Knowles (chairman), Mr 8 Woodward (vice-chairman) Admiral Jones-Parry, Messrs J Mclntyre, J Stokes. T Darlington, T Wright, J B Chadwick, T Baxter, C E Linaker, J Moseley IN illiams,T Lewis, Charles Roylance, P Gleave, H Ddawdsley, G Piling, J Bolshaw, E Clark., W Turner, J Whitlow, W El Bradford, J Mountfield, R Billington, G Ashton (clerk). W E Hough (assistant clerk), W Swinton and W Dig,gle (surveyors), J Far- rington (inspector) and Dr. Adams (Medical Officer cf Health).</p>
        <p>THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD AND THE THELWALL NUISANCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Noel T. Kershaw assistant !secretary to the Local Government Board wrote under date 17th October, 1901 Su' : lam directed by the Local Government Board to advert to your letter of the 25% ult relative to the nuisance from Messrs Long's Tannery at Thelwall in the Runporn Rural District. It appears to the Board to be clear that there exists from time to time at Thelwall very serious nuisance and that although in its more aggravated form it is intermittent, it is of frequent re- currence. The Board arc of opinion that in view of the complaints which have been persistently and widely made the Rural District Council should take the neces- sary steps to deal effectively with the matter. The Board request to be informed of furtb(r action which :nay be taken with this object by the Rural District Council.</p>
        <p>The Clerk geld he had notified the Board as to the action the Conceit were taking. So far as the sewage was concerned they were takicg care to purify it before it went into the brook. They had instructed the Ship Canal Company to clean out the culvert and they say they are only responsible for what goes ouZl of the canal and not for the catch pit at the top end of the culvert. They were quite willing it should be filled up. He could not find out whether it had been made before the canal or not.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : It will be a job to father it on the canal company. The Clerk : Unless there is evidence to show it was made by the Canal Company or that they have prr- viously cleaned that portion of it you cannot compel them to clean that portion of the culvert.</p>
        <p>The Clerk then read the following letter from the Ship Canal Company under date 12M Octob.r, through Mr refiloCl3 Wiswall :—Mr Ashton. Dear Sir. Referring to our interview I now beg to confirm what I said verbally that we were prepared to clear out the culvert under the canal to our boundary, and that the portion of culvert under the road and the well or catch pit was: not our liability, and we had no 'objection to the catch pit being altered in any way desirable. I at once gave iastructioss to our inspector to have the cul- vert under the canal cleaned out so far as oar portion was conc-rned.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : They will only clew out to their boundary,</p>
        <p>. - Admiral Jones-Parry said they bad never been told by the medical officer or the, inspector where the nuis- ance existed. They had it from Dr Penny that the tannery was the cause of the nuisance. Ele moved that Messrs Long be ordered to do away with the process in which yeast is used. Mr Woodward said they had no power to interfere with any man's procese. They could only ask him to do away with a nuisance to the public health. Admiral Jonel-Parry said that Dr Penny bad said it was a nuisance. They could not get behind that. Mr Wood ward submitted that Dr Penny's repart bad been refuted by Dr Adams who had been entirely sup- ported by the reports of the committee. Mr Linaker contended that the committee were net capable of saying whether there was a nuisance being , given off in the course cif 'preparation. The Local Government Board bad recognized the value of Dr Penny's report. The Council were in a most discredit- able position. It had been going on for 15 mo atlas and things load reached such a pitch that MrNaylor had actually eht up hi 3 house and gone away. The Chairman : I should liko the Local Government Board to send down an Inspector.</p>
        <p>rilr Linliter : lam confident wear() going to get into troublc.</p>
        <p>Mr Stakes, as an outsider, bad been to see the place for himself and was surprised to find that so much had been made of the sewage matters. Mr Naylor had Pointed out the tannery and they ought to have the advice of some chemist to see whether there were any injurious vapours arising. Mr Linaker asked why toe ditch could not be filled up as suggested in the Medical Officer's report ? The Medical Officer: Because Messrs Lang refuse to Lave it doae.</p>
        <p>Mr Roylance said be had inspected the place and had found out that for along' time reta4e from the tannery had been employed to fill up pits There were hundreds of tons in one of the pits. rid examined one of thorn and foond it was literally alivo and that fermentation was going on. On the surface was a fungus growth similar to that of s mushroom. He overturned a par-1 tion and there was an abominable stench. He did not I won Er at it making people ill. One of the tioings to be attended to at once was to pipe the ditch and be moved a proposition to that effect. Mr Baxt or seconded. Mr.Linaker proposed as an amendment that the recom- mendation of Dr Penny and Dr Adams bo carried our. . . . . .</p>
        <p>After further discuasion it R 7,19 ultimately decided that the clerk be instructed to see Messrs Long with the letter of the Local G wernrnent Board and endeavour to arrange for the brook to be piped in accordance with the suggestion of the medical otfic.r.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>HATTON SEWAGE.</head>
        <p>HATTON SEWAGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. John White, Eatate 01Bee, Warrington, wrr,t. under date 7,:b October, 1901, re Flatton Sewage :—" I return plan herein. I think that £1 a ynar siouid b 3 paid for the way leave acrosa Sir Gilbert' land. The tenant will require compmsation no doubt but it cannot amount to winch. If you are unable t 3 settle with him</p>
        <p>I iarealy Mr T S Buriesi will do so if you will leave the matter in his:bands." Writing on the 9:h October, Mr. White acid " If you think 20;. a year la too much we will fir the amount at 5. twr annum. Will this do." . . _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Smith Harms wrote on the 10% October : " I have seen the tenants of the land (exers of Mr, John Blackshaw). They will require compensation for dam- age but are unable to sav what it will be until the work is finished. They are quite willing to leave it to me to as2e.zs the damage when the work is complete." The terms were agreed to and it was left with the sewage agent to arrange with Mr. Stnita Burge,s for compensation for damage done.</p>
        <p>LIGHT RAILWAYS.</p>
        <p>The following letter dated 02tober 86 was received from Mr R H Scotter, consulting engineer :—Dear Sir. " Lieht Railway throueti Great Buthvortb."—At Lbe request of Mr Piers Egerton Warburton we have planned a deviation from the main road which will if agreed upon, cut Cock-lane about 300 yards from the water I cook and the road leading from Great Budworth pump .ncl Comberbach at the top of the hill. We shall there- for( leave the main read at Gib Hill and return to it opposite the footpath run down from Buisvorth not far iron, the boundary brook. Kindly let me know if Cock- lane and the Curiberbach-road are in your care. The Clerk said he had already supplied the necessary informa.ion.</p>
        <p>This was all the business of public interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ST PETER'S CHURCII BIBLE CLASS, NEWTON. A social gathering to inaugurate the opening of the winter session of this class took place on Saturday evening in St Peter's Schools, Newton There were about 60 present, including the Rev J Ryder, vicar, who briefly referred to the approach- ing bazaar in connection with the church. Vocal items were given by Misses Wilkinson and Bsybut, Messrs Perry, and Bradshaw, and recitations by Mr JII Lane. A number of dances were indulged in during the evening. Refreshments were provided.</p>
        <p>- ALLEGED THEFT OF BOOTS AT LARLE-STOWN. At Warrington Occasional Court. on Saturday, before Mr F A Frost, a man named John Gray, of no fixed residence, was charged with having stolen a pair of boots, valued at 5s lid, the property of the Public Benefit Company at Earlestown, on October. 18tb.From the evidence of the police it appeared that prisoner was observed by two police-officers to be handling the boots outside the shop in Market- street. They watched him for about ten minutes and then he cut the string of a pair of boots and when walking away one of the boots fell to the ground. A little farther on the other boot fell, and he was then taken into custody. Prisoner, who denied the offence, was remanded to the Newton Sessions to be held on the 31st inst. ODDFELLOWS' ANNIVERSARY AT COLLINS GREEN. The sixty-ninth anniversary of the Loyal Farmers Refuge Lodge, St Helens district, was celebrated in an enjoyable manner on Saturday last at the Pear Tree Inn, Collins Green, when the members cat down to an admirably served knife-and-fork tea in the Lodge room, to which ample justice was done. After tea the tables wer cleared and PPGM Fox, ot St Helens, was elected chairman, who proposed the usual loyal toast, after which he proposed suc- cess to the Farmers' Refuge Lodge, which was Suit- ably responded to by the Secretary P i,W Sephton, The toast of " The host and hostess" was given with musical honours, to which Mr Maddison re- plied. Songs were given by Messrs Cope and Bainbridge, of St Helens. and recitations by Mr R Fairclough. Mr F Cunliffe, of St Helens,officiated at the piano. A very enjoyable evening was spent and brought to a close at 11 'o'clock pm. TO CURB A. COLD IN ONH DAY. Take Laxative Brom° Quinine Tablets. All Druggists refund the money If it tails to Cure. is. lid. The genuine is stamped L.H.Q.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By "pyalist."</p>
        <p>Warrington's latest performance has once again raised them to the highest estimation of their followers. By beating Oldham they have accomplished a feat which has been beyond the capabilities of any other club this season, and by scoring 15 points to 5 they have aggregated more points against the champions than all the other clubs they have yet met put together. Furthermore the licking is about the severest Oldham have sustained for a long time. And finally the score fairly represents the measure of Warrington's superior- ity. They were a full ten points better than the Oldhamers, who were beaten fore and aft and ia between from start to finish.</p>
        <p>It was a splendid tame, full of incident, and con- tinued at a very fast pace throughout. The opening was sensational enough to work up the blood of a marble statue, for Warrington got a try in the very first minute. Such things as a score from the opening movement-are not very uncommon in " Socker " but in the Rugby code the event is very rare indeed. And it happened so quickly too, on Saturday. Morrison returned the kick-off, and A Lees mulling the catch allowed the ball to go behind him. The Warrington forwards were the first to reach it, and in the twinkling of an eye they took it over the line and Jolley scored. There was a shout—a startled, surprised shout, but nevertheless a shout of joy from the home crowd. Fish missed the goal; but went so near with the kick that many thought he had succeeded.</p>
        <p>Further points were not long in coming, however. The sudden success had fired Warrington's blood and they borepown on the Oldham line like an avalanche. Fine passing by the backs saw Fishlin possession near the touch-line with a possible chance of scoring. That possibility was soon a certainty for ha rounded fluzzey at a startling speed, bluffed the full-back and finished up behind the posts. Isherwood placed a goal and Warrington were now feeling pretty secure, a feeling which was strengthened a few minutes later when Fisl. placed an easy penalty goal.</p>
        <p>Oldham roused up at this and gave the first real glimpse of their usual form. The backs led the way to the home half and after Huzzey had been well-stopped on the right, Oldham passed across beautifully and Williams darted over at the corner. No goal followed and Warrington immediately drove their opponents to the other end where Hallam was deliberately obstructed and Fish kicked another goal from the penalty, making the half time score 3 goals 2 tries (12 points) to Oldham 1 try (3 points).</p>
        <p>Both sets of committee men administered advice during the interval, but that of the home officials was evidently best for the locals immediately took up the aggressive. Oldham defended well and eventually got a bit the best of matters. Another passing bout was under weigh when Fish nipped irk on his own goal line- and taking the pass meant for Huzhey he darted through the Oldham backs like greased lightning and soon had the whole team behind him. Huzzey made a great effort to overhaul the Warrington idol but after the first few yards he never gained an inch and Fish crossed the line after a magnificent effort quite a yard ahead of his pursuer. The cheering which greeted this sensational score was tremendous and long- ' sustained, even the Oldham contingent applauding the ' hero of the day. The goal-kick was muffed but the game was as good as over. Warrington easily kept the visitors under and all they got was a penalty goal from the foot of Dickson, the final score being 3 goals 3 tries (15 points) to Oldhain 1 goal 1 try (5 points.)</p>
        <p>Even strangers to the Rugby game enjoyed Satur- day's match, it was so full of incident and clever play by both teams. Oldham were a long way from being at their best, and the absence of Thomas and S Lees is not by any means a sufficient excuse for the poor show they made. Warrington bottled their opponents up from the start and never gave them any quarter. Though the first point scored was lucky the two other tries by Fish were the outcome of superior play, while the goal score should have been even greater. Nobody could controvert the statement that the visitors in sus- taining the first loss of the season were fairly beaten by ' a better team. Warrington's success went a long way towards consoling their supporters for the unhappy result of the Swinton match.</p>
        <p>Saturday was Fish's day out, and though he did not look in the best of health he was certainly in the best of football form. I rather fancy that the Welsh Inter- national, Vivian Huzzey was more than a little sur- prised by the way the two tries were scored on his wing. The first time Fish completely beat him and the second time outpaced him, which fairly opened the eyes of the Oldham folk who have no small idea of I Huzzey's capability and speed. Fish quite outclassed him and it is a question whether there is a better wing threequarter playing to-day.</p>
        <p>Though Dickenson was off Ratcliffe got through the work at centre with infinite credit. He seems better here than on the wing. Isherwood also played a fino game, while the only fault that could be found with Harris was that he put a little too much energy into his tackling. The halves were better than thepldhamn. pair, both Allen and Hockenhall showing greatly improved form. They look like making a highly suc- cessful couple at the base of the scrum. Hallam out- classed his vis-a-vis who was too slow for first-class football, and in the forward argument Warrington quite held their own. Fell, Jolley and Morrison were always to the fore. S Williams was the best man on the being side, Civil and Joe Lawton corning next in the claim for honours.</p>
        <p>Hunslet's victory at Leigh was the most surprising or the other League results, Goldthorpe's men giving the Mather-laners quite a wrinkle or two in class football. This bodes ill for Warrington, who visit Hunslet to-day minus Fish, and will have to play at the top of their form to avoid defeat. Broughton got another lucky two points at the expense of Salford, the margin being once more the leastpossible.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows :</p>
        <p>..., . For Against P WL D ... G T YTS ... G T PTs Broughton Ran7,ers 6 6 0 0 —ll 16-70 ... 4 3-17 —l2 Oldham Hull Huraslet Runcorn *Warrington Leigh 7 3 4 0 ...I0 8-44 ... 5 9-37 ... 6 *Swinton 4 3 1 0 ... 5 5-15 ... 3 2-12 ... 4 *Salford Batley Bradford Halifax 5 1 4 0 ... 4 6-23 —lO 10-53 ... 2 Hudderafiell Brighouse Rangers *Salford. Swinton, and Warrington have each had two, points deducted for brsech ot the rules.</p>
