      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>There are no special agents for this Cocoa.</head>
        <p>There are no special agents for this Cocoa.</p>
        <p>It can be obtained from every Grocer.</p>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>DEFRAUDING THE RAILWAY COMPARY.—LYMM WATCHMAKER HEAVILY FINED. At the Altrincbarn Petty Sessions on Monday, before Mr Bulkeley Allen (in the chair) and otlaer jestices, man named John Brearley. describ,d as a watchmaker, of Lymin, was nummoned for having travelled on thel railway between Oxford-toad, Manchester,and Dunham Massey, on August 14th, without having paid lais fare, and with intent to avoid payment of it. He was also charged with having been previously convicted of a similar dience.—Defendant pleaded guilty.—Mr Fence, Selicitor, who prosecuted on behalf of tbe London and North-Western Railway Company. said that on August 14th defendant arrived at the Heatley and Warburton Station by the 2.45 p.m. train. The etationmaster,whn knew defendant, was collecting tickets. Defendant said —"What is the fare from Dunham Massey ? " Be was told that the fare was lid. and he paid the fare. The stationmaster tben said, "Did yen speak to the guard or the stationmaster at Dunham Massey ?" Defendant, in I answer to that paid, No. I had not the time.' The! Stationmaster thought this was rather unsc.tisfactnry, and be told defendant so. Subsequently. defendant acknowledged to Detective Howells, of the London and North-Western Railway police, that he had travailed on the occasion in question from Manchester. D..fendant, as they had heard was convicted in October. 1899, when , be was summoned for travelling on three separate occasione on the railway with intent to avoid payment of his fare. This man had given them &amp;instant trouble-, and since his last conviction bad boasted that he would have it out of the Railway Company. Be knew the :On very well, and bad been ery clever at avoiding pay- ment of fares.—The Chairman said this was C very gross Case, and the magistraten thought the circumstances could only be met by fining def.ndant £lO and costs. In default of payment defendant woald have to undergo two months' imprisonment. The fine and costs amcuntcd in all to £ll 45.</p>
      </div>
