        <p>ALLEGED D•MIGE AND ASSAULT.—A boy named James Haziehurst, Heaton•street, was surnmonded for committing wilful damage to a wall the property of John Hill.—Mr Steel appeared Use* complainant and Mr Crampton represented the defendant —Complaiasnt's cAse was that defendant who lived next door to crmplainant, knocked holes through the wall, doing damage to the amount of 25.—llefendant alleged that the bole was made by complainant's daughter and Hazlehmet was pigging it with a piece of wood. Hill was then charged with assaulting Hazlehurst —The case arose out of the last one. it keing stated that on the follow- ing day Hill saw the toy and hit him in the face. knocking him to tte ground.—The assault was admitted, defendants stating that complainant used impudence to him and pro- voked the assault. —Both cases were dismissed, Hill having to pay the casts In tke last case.</p>
        <p>bATURDAY.—Before Messrs Chanelley (in the chair), BirettalL, R T Fare lough and J W Hayward.</p>
        <p>Ais 'ERRAND BuY'a THETT.—A bey named William Row- lands. (13). living In Gorsey-lane, was charged with having sometime between the 16th and 18th inst. stolen 33 pen knives, of the vatic of 59s 2d, the property of Mr H N Houghton, ironmonger,c( Market-gate.—The Chief Constable stated that the prisoner had been an errand boy for two months in the prosecutor's services, and last Wednesday Mr Stanley, the manager, pl4ced a number of knives in a certain portion of the shop and left tbem there. He subsequently missed them, end suspicion falling upon the prisoner be charged him with the theft. The lad at first denied having stolen the knives, but. being strongly pressed. he admitted that be was the culprit. Some of the knives bad been sold for trifling sums, and others given to Ids companions. Some of the knives bad been recovered.—The Bench ordered the tad to receive twelve strokes of the birch rod, and to be kept In the bridewelt until six o'clock in the evening. Before Mr. F. Monks fin the chair), Aldermen Heaton and. Hutchinson. Councillor Smethurst, and Messrs. birchml, Hayward. Thornton, and Wright.</p>
        <p>FIRING laurs TuE HIGRWAY.—dohn Syers, forgeman. 34. Watkln-etreet, was summoned for firing a gun on the publio highway at Arpley en Sunday week.—P.C. Clough said he saw the defenoant Ire the gun front the highway. He produced a lieence, and said he did not knew he was doing wrong —The Chief Constabos said there were many complaint. about this sort of thing en Jund4y.—Detendant was fit.ed 28 6d. SUNDAY GAMBLING—George Higham (17). 4, Bay Horse- 'card ; Walter Deloozo (17). 19. Winwlek-road ; JoSeph platten (18) Turner4treet ; Joseph Williams (17), 15, Turner-street : James Potts (17), 18, Turner-street; John Parker (WI), 7, Houghton-strut ; John button (19), 1, Rodney-street ; Thomas Ireland (19). 23, Turner-street ; John Baldwin (15), 7, Haydeek-street, were summoned for playing bin ker for money in Turner street on October 13th: PC. Turner proved the cast., and Sutton. ho had been pre• vioty4y convicted, was fined 106, and the rest ss.—John Feernley (14). 30, Watkin-street ; Peter Leonard :13), 5. Melville-street : Thomas Hayes (10), 4, Welkin-street, were fined 26 6d each for tossing for money in Melville-street on he same day. UEB73sY.—Before Mr T Biraball (In the chair), Aldermen Shaw-Hreen and Hutchinson, Dr Fox, and Mr J Rymer Young. A VIoLENT PRISONER —Frank Mills was charged with assaulting Police-constable Howard the precious night, and also wish being drunk and disorderly In Winwici street. The prisoner was being taken into custody by Police-constable Heap, and Police-constable Howard Was trying to keep baek the crowd,when the prisoner slipped away and kieked Police- constable Howard in the stomach. Tbe officers had to pursue him tcr same distance before he was re-arrested. A fine of fa and costa was imposed for drunkenness, 106 and costs for the assault, or in default twenty-one days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>WEDNESVILY.—Before Mclennan Sutton (in the chair), Alder-</p>
        <p>- man Monks, and Mr. BirelBll. NEGLECT or CHILDREN.--Jamee Marsh. 49, Atkin-street, was charged with having neglected his six children, Annie, James, damuel. Mary, Mabel, and Bilen.—Mr A. Browne appeared on behalf of the N.5.P.0.C.. and stated that on Saturday evening the priSoner turned his wife and three of the children out and they were obliged to stay in the streets all nlght.—The Chief Constable said this wag prisoner's sevcnth appearatum The previous offences were chiefly assaults upon his wife. Prisoner was sent to gaol for a monde.</p>
