      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>UTE EXAMINER FRIDAY, OCTOBER 25 1901</head>
        <p>UTE EXAMINER FRIDAY, OCTOBER 25 1901</p>
        <p>THE THOMPSON MEMORIAL FOUNTAIN.</p>
        <p>Turning on the Water.</p>
        <p>An interesting ceremony took place at Winwick on Saturday alternoon,when Captain Withingtoa turned on the water at the fountain which has been erected by public subscriptions in mcmory of the late Mr J Thomp- son, Surveyor to the Warrington Rural District Council. The fountain is of light York stone, and has two water bowls and taps, with enamelled drinking cups. There is also a trough for horses, underneath which is a small one for dogs. The inscription is cut into the stone,and reads as follows :—" Erected by councillors, friends,and employes of the late Mr John Thompson, A.M.1.C.E., surveyor to the Warrington Rural District Highway Authority from 1866 to 1900, in commemoration of his good work on the highways of the district, and as a token of esteem." A large crowd assembled near the fountain,- and amongst those present were Captain Withington, Canon and Mrs Penrhyn, Mt Joshua Ball and members of the Warrington Rural District Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOSHIIik BALL said he had a very pleasing duty to perform, and that was to ask Captaid iNithington ta torn on the water to the fountain. He was sure the committee could not have made a better selection, and '..ho family and relatives were doubtless obliged to him for many reasons. In the fir,t place big acquaintance I with Mr. Thompson extended over 40 years, and again it was he who proposed Mr. Thompson for the post of surveyor. In conclusion, he said he hoped that the fountain would be used and not abused—as m.,ny were , —by the public. Captain WITHINGTON said it was he who first proposed the late Mr. Thompson for the post of surveyor to the Highway Board. They had 35 applicants for the post, and their late friend was the successful candidate. He was then only twenty years of age. He re-echoed the words of the chairman, and hoped that that fountain— a memorial to one they all knes —would suffer no abuse. (Loud applaese.)Captain Withingtoa then turned the water on, and had the first drink.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lemma THOMPSON, in reply, wished to thank the subscribers most heartily on behali of himself and the whole of the family. The present was the proudest moment of his life, and although good work was not always appreciated, in this case it was clearly shown to have ben. He must also thank the Parish and Rural District Counoils for so readily consenting to the rais- ing of the memorial. The spot on which it was erected was the most important in the district, and it was very dear to them because of family connections. His thanks were also due to the Rev. Canon Penitiyn for so kindly remeviog any difficulty there might !Ave been with the Ecclesiastical Commiesioners. He wished to im- press upon the subscribers how grateful to them the family were. He was a highway official himself, and they could understand what a proud moment this was to him. He also thanked Captain Withington for his pre- sence that afternoon. Captain Withington supported his father the whole of the time, and they weed him a debt of gratitude for his assistance. He moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Within7,ton and to Mr. Ball. The Rev. CANON PENSITYN seconded, and spoke in eulogistic terms of the difference in the state of the roads during the time Mr Thompson acted as surveyor. Speaking as chairman of the Parish Council, he was dc- lighted to have the monument in that part of tho parish. He thought it would be an improvement to have a light on the top to guide people along the right row/.</p>
        <p>Captain WITUINGTON and Mr. Brat returned thanks, and the alsemtly dispereed,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>MR. JOHN PEMBERTON AND THE WARRINGTON CORPORATION. --- Action Dismissed with Costs.</head>
        <p>MR. JOHN PEMBERTON AND THE WARRINGTON CORPORATION. --- Action Dismissed with Costs.</p>
        <p>The action commenced by Mr John Pemberton against the Warrington Corporation in the High Courts of Juice has been dismissed with costs. It will be remembered that Mr Pemberton issued a writ on the 13th of February lest, claiming the right as a burgess to inspect the minute books of the Corporation. Since thea there have been three interrogatory appeals in the law courts en each of which the plaintiff was defeated. On the Sth October last the Court ordered that the action should stand dismissed unless notice should be entered for appeal before the 22nd inst. As no notice of appeal was entered by that date the order was dismissed with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>BOILER EXPLOSION AT RUNCORN</head>
        <p>BOILER EXPLOSION AT RUNCORN</p>
        <p>Board of Trade Inquiry. A Difference in Expert Opinion.</p>
        <p>A 131.trd of Trade inquiry into the cause of the ex- el,sion of a water-tube boiler at tee works of the Castner-Kellner Alkali Company, Limited, West sa Point, near Runcorn, was concluded on Saturday. The Commissioners for the Board of Trade were Mr. Fioward Smith, barrister, and Mr. Alexander Gray, consulting engineer. Mr. Gough, one of the Board of Trade solicitors, conducted the case, and Mr. Collingwood Hope, K.C., appeared on behalf of certain officials of the Company to whom the exploded boiler belonged. ' The boiler which formed the subject of the Investiga- tion was of the Babcock and Wilcox type. It ceneisted of 160 tubes, each about 41n. outside diametei. and 18ft. long. The tubes are of solid drawn steel, -15 el an Inch in thickness. The boiler was set to work in the year 1898. Several tubee were renewed from time e to time, aid on the 17th of July last, (m of the tubes, which had only been in use 17 or 13 dayi, rupturad,ar.d a fireman was severely scalded. Tao boiler was insured with the National Boiler and General Insurance Com- pany, Limited. It was worked night arid day, without intermission, at a very l'igh preaseire. 180Ib. to 200 b. per square inch, which it was supposed to be quite equal to. That the explosion was dote to overheating was admitted without quezticn, but how the overheat- ing occurred was the Raj, ct of a remarkable difference of opinion in the expert witnesses. It was proved that in spite of filters of cloth and sawdust there was a trace of oil in the feed water, and to the presence of this oil the expleeirn, in the judgment of Mr E G Hiller, chief engineer to the National Boiler Insurance Company, two ' of his assistants, and one of the Company's inspectors, was due. Their theory was that the oil, mixing with the water and forming all emulsion, or mingling with the scale, prevknted the water from coming in eon- tact with the metal at one particular point where the flame of the fire was hnttest, and so brought abou the rupture of the tube. Oa the other hand, Mr. W. Harm, senior engineer-survoy sr to the Board of 'Trade at LiverpiPol, and Mr George Gray, engineer sur- veyor to tie Board of Trade, were of opinion that the p7eserice of oil had nothing to do with the explosion. Their belief was that, owing Co too heavy firing and the want of a better diffusion of the flame, steam pockets had been form' d, and that which is technically known as " water-hemmer" had been set up. In this way the water,:in their judgment, had been prevented from having full contact with the iron. Mr Stromeyer, chief engineer of the Manchester Stearn Veers' Association, inclined ta the same opinion.:Under these circumstances the Court declined to decide what was the cause of the overhea' ing, which was, of course, the imusediate cause of the explosion. They had, bowever,formed the opin- ion in their own minds that the overheating was due to oily deposit on the iron. A negative answer was therefore riven to the question put to them by the Board of Trade, as to whether the explosion had been caused by the negloce, of any of the Castner.liellner Alkali Company's officiate. Th4t being so, the Com- missioners did not hold the Castner-Sellner Alkali Com- pany responsible for the explosion, and made no order as to caste.</p>
        <p>"The Eye in man ana Animals" This was the subject of the second at the series of sight lectures promoted by the Combe trustees on "The senses : sight, bearing and touch," and delivered in the Cu-operative Hall, Cairo-street on Thesday evening, Professor W Stirling, M.D., of the Owens College was the lecturer. The lecturer first explained the structure of the eyeball, and described the various parts and their uses, illu- etrating his remarks by a series of models. Late on he showed a number of beautiful lantern slides and treated the minutest details of the subject, describing the cornea and sclerotic, the choroid coat and retina, the iris, the pupil and the optic nerve. In speakinz of the colour of the eye, the lecturer showed that the various degrees of colour which were seen such as blue, brown and grey were all derived from black pigment and this black when seen through the semi-transparent iris gave the appearanc6 of the blue, brown or other colours in different individuals. In an interesting way the lecturer showed that the cornea was without blood vessels and stated that there were excessively few In the retina. The absence of blood gave the eye its transparency. There was, however, plenty of blood in their is and one of its most important functions was to keep the retina warm and of an equable temperament. The eyes of mammals, fidies and birds were fully described and the evolution of the eye from the lower animals was demonstrated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>MOST NUTRITIOUS.</head>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
        <p>E PPS'S</p>
        <p>GFIATEFUL-COMFORTING.</p>
        <p>COCOA</p>
        <p>3REAKFAST--SUPPER</p>
        <p>rnlll4 - • C</p>
        <p>.$ 1 In' a</p>
        <p>I WILL REPAY. POWERFUL NEW STORY STARTS TO-DAY. `, Heaven help all whom it may concern. Jude Bryce has returned."</p>
        <p>CHAPTER I . The Homecoming.</p>
        <p>It was four o'clock in the afternoon of a fine day in late October, and the townlet of Stoke Bassett was in a high state of excitement. The little town was only a hour-and-a-half distance by rail from London, and the whole place had turned out to-day to celebrate the home-ccrming of Miles Deering, the son and heir of the lord of the manor, and his pretty and much-envied bride.</p>
        <p>"There she is! There they are " came at last from the little throng gathered in front of the principal shop in the High Street. Several carriages and a few cabs had already passed and had had their occupants duly remarked and discussed; but now there came along an open landau which aroused so feverish an interest as showed that its arrival had been looked for as the great event of the afternoon. A very fine landau it was, drawn by a pair of roan horses, and in it there sat three persons—a man and two ladies.</p>
        <p>The man was the newly-married Miles Deering, the only son and heir of Sir Archibald Deering of Portman Square, London and Quex Woods, Stoke Bassett, bead of the firm of Archibald Peering &amp; Company, Shipowners and General Merchants of Graoechurcb. Street, City. Opposite to Miles Derring in the carriage sat his bride of three weeks, Eve Deering, with her fair face alternately flashing and paling as she noticed the interest taken in her. A very charming bride, indeed, was she, and so the Stoke Bassett people thought and said to each other, as they duly inspected and Minted her. But their eyes did not test long upon • her, for there was one at her side who was better known to them than she, and was dearer to them, and was also in their opinion far better worth looking at. Better worth looking at? Yes, indeed ! Miles Deering's hride was like a violet in her girlish shy- ness and purity and modest sweetness. But the girl who sat beside her—Lena Deering, the bridegroom's sister—was lerely with the rich loveliness of a cdowing and glorious English rose. _ _</p>
        <p>" She takes the shine- out oflklrs. Mitee, doesn't she 7 " cried the old bookseller, NvN) owned the shop aforesaid, uncompromisingly. "Well, the nian that gets her will be a lucky Man, and no mistake. But where's the man who loves her best? Why isn't he here with her ? " "Who's that ? " asked a voice. "Who's that? Why, John Stirling, of course. I'll beck him to win her in spite of all your Captain Teirises, though it may take him another ten years." With which sentiments the majority of the crowd agreed, for the domestic affairs of Quex Woods had lately been the chief topic of convensation in Stoke Bassett. And while John Stirling was a general , favourite, Stoke Bassett did not take at all kindly to Captain Terriss, said to be from Texas, the latest suitor for Miss Daering's hand.</p>
        <p>The carriages had all passed now, and the crowd in the High Street was dispersing, men; women, and children alike going homeward, when suddenly a shoek arrested them ; arrested them one and all instantly, as if at a word of command. The noise of a terrific -crash, muffled by distance, yet still fear- ful, had reached them. •</p>
        <p>"There was an interval of paralysis, when none either spoke or moved. Then some of the Men started to run Eastward towards the station. But the crowd as a body waited; waited in consternation and terror, Holding its breath. And presently some of. those that had gone in search of news came back to make the startling announcement : " The train which was due here ten minutes after the special has met with an accident a quarter af a mile out from the station. Another train crashed into the back part of it. • There are about five they anr, and half-a-dozen injured. They're saying that John Stirling is among-the injured and that he's not expected to live."</p>
        <p>John. Stirling fatally injured—Join Stirling, the brother of the bride who had just gone by, and the favourite of her new father-in-law, Sir Archibald Deering 1 The shadow of a heavy gloom settled on the fading of the afternoon light. They did not go home now, but waited in the streets, talking in low voices of the disaster and expecting they knew not soliat. They had awaited bare* a hail- hour when voicoff, among them were heard saying: . .</p>
        <p>''.They are bringinol John Stirling along to Quo Woods, because it is nearer than the hospital." So they bore John Stirling to Quex Woods, the home of the woman whom be secretly loved, and on the evening fixed for the ball in celebration of the return of his lately, wedded sister after hex honeymoon. "it looks as if the Lord had taken away hi: chance of • winning Lena Deering," said the old bookseller in a low voice to the group immediately about him.</p>
        <p>She started back with a quick, low cry. Then she stooped over him again, even more ten- derly than before. "o,—no I " she said, hurriedly. "You,• are making a mistake. You must be making a mistake. You are feeling, no doubt, as if your life was crushed and broken; but it is not so—it cannot be so."</p>
        <p>John Stirling smiled faintly. His hand clung to hers again. "God bless you for saying this—for trying to comfort me. To know that you care—that you feel for me—would help me to live if anything could, but nothing can. When I recovered my senses just now I a-stked the doctor—the old one, Dr. Brunker —,if I were going to die. He would not tell me at first, but I insisted; and at last he told me the truth, very unwillingly. He told me that my injuries are so bad that I cannot recover. So I wanted to see you, to say—to tell :you—while yet there is time. Presently my strength will go, and I may not be able to speak. That would be best for you, perhaps but not for me. I want to die knowing that I have bared my heart to you—that yon know and will not forget, even though your life be always full of light and joy, as it is now."</p>
        <p>He tried to raise himself, 'but the effort was vain, and a moan broke from'his ashen lips. "it is only the knowledge that I am dying that gives me- courage to tell you what I am going to tell you now, he panted. t"And . that is that I love you—l love you—l lave yon ! " His strong hand—such a strong band it had been a few short hours before! clung tighter yet to her small, delicate one. "1 lore you with all the strength Itohat is in me," he whisiwred, hoarsely, daring to say all now thiit his tongue had once been unloosed. " Lena ! Lena !- I have loved you and wanted you for years!" _</p>
        <p>• • " Mr. Stirling ! John ! " She slily a shiver shake him. " It is true ! It is true !;' his eyes told her even more surely than did his voice. "I should never have told you but for this death-Wow which has struck me. I should never have dared to speak. What right could I have bed to hope for you?' I had no great name to give you, no fortune, I am the son of one of your father's dearest friends, it is true, and if I had livA I should soon have become a junior partner in the firm of Archibald Deering &amp; Co. ; but how much would these things coma with . youd For yttars my, heart has longed and ached to tell you, but I would. not. I could not. It would have seemed like offering you an insult." "A!), no! Ah, no I The love of a good man is never an insult," Lena Deering denied; softly, yet proudly. "it does honour to any woman yes. (Ayer ti ;'ii she be a queen." "Vim 4.verc a queen to me," he whispered, ardently. "And von cannot know with what</p>
        <p>alnusy and pain I have seen. that Terries was winning you. Can you think how it has hurt me to see this rich T exan interloper watching you and following you hour by hour, and playing the part of lover to you—the part which I longed to play but might not? Can you think how I have suffered? Heaven knows, and it is perhaps in pity that Heaven sends me this death. Ni hen I am gone and you are Terriss's wife, you will for- get these wild words of a suffering man. And just now, when I herd that I could not live, I said to myself that, at least, one blessing would come to me with death which life could not have given me—the blessing of being able to tell you at last that my heart beats for you. Lena I- - Lena "</p>
        <p>_ He broke off, his voice momentarily failing him. He closed his eyes for an instant. When he opened them again be was conscious that the vision in white was no longer standing, but was kneeling baside his bed. "John I" she whispered, tremulously "John 1" A second time John Stirling tried to raise him- self, and a second time he sank back helplessly, with a moan. But a light had come into his eyes which no mere bodily pain could banish. " John 1" she whispered again, and in the lamp- light he saw a quick blush dye her tear-wet cheeks. "I love you, too. I never knew it until to-night but I know it now. lam yours in heart, John, and I shall be yours until—until—" "Until the end comes ?" be whispered. "Yes. Until the end." "Now I can afford to die," he murmured. "Even if it be to-night. I can afford to die." His brave eyes feasted on her face, and a strain of the music of the dance reached them from be- low.</p>
        <p>Ancl in a few minutes he confronted his father's and his own head clerk—for he was his father's partner in the great Deering business. " Good-moiming, Mr. Pinkerton. Sorry to keep you waiting. What's "Sir Archibald Deering has sent me Sir, to see you and return with you by the next train." "Go back with you to London"' "Sir Archibald wishes it, Sir. Something very serious has happened and he says he cannot move in the matter until you come. The auditors have discovered a sgood deal wrong with the books. There are defalcations to the extent of close upon twenty thousand pounds." ' "Defalcations!" Miles Deering repeated the ominous word in a tone that cleverly indicated the sheerest amazement. "It is astounding ! it is perfectly incredible I Are you sure that there has been no mistake I " _ _</p>
        <p>The clerk sliook his head. "Unfortunately, Sir, there is no room for any doubt whatever. The books have been examined most carefully and there is no istake possible, and one of the worst features of the ugly business is that the accounts have been cooked most ingeniously cooked. Whoever the embezzler is, he has done his work most cleverly. But we had better not waste time in talking, Sir. I conld give you the details in the train., There is a train at eleven-forty- five which we might catch it you can hurry.", But the young, man hesitated. "I have an important letter or two to write," he said hastily. "Let me see—when is the next train after that I Twelve-thirty? That will do. We will go by that." Miles hurried out of the room, crossed the inner hall, hesitated for a moment at the foot of the stair-ease and then mounted the stairs and walked with a nervous step along the corridor to the room whore John Stirling lay_ broken and racked with suffering. Softly he entered. There was no one in the room except the patient and his day nurse. The sick man himself, lay with closed eyes. In spite of the utgeacy of his business here, Miles Deering stopped to draw a sharp breath at sight of the face ' • it was so drawn, so haggard, so changed from what it had been but yesterday morning.</p>
        <p>" Jogn t" the visitor uttered the name in a startled .voice, "yon are better ? "</p>
        <p>, The sick man smiled and feebly put out his hand. "Only better ii so far as I am nearer a better world, Miles. That is the only way in which I shall ever be better now."</p>
        <p>The other stared at him. "And yet you look happy—radiantly happy I" he cri,Scl, wondering. "You are haggard and pale and altered, of tonne, but the look in your eyes suggests thab you have just come in for a fortune." • "That is not the best thing that could happen to tae, or to any other man," rejoined Joh,</p>
        <p>Stirling, gravely, yet brightly, "there are better things inalife than coming in for a fortune, Miles. You, as a newly-married, man ought to know this. Yes, there are many better things—and the best. of them all has come to me, although only for a few short hours. But this is my h'eart's secret.'- But you are looking terribly ill, Miles. Whtt is the matter I Has anything gone wrong with you!" His friend and brother-in-law nodded: "Yes, everything has gone wrong :with me. I am in a terrible difficult; Johl—in difficulty and even danger—and I want you to help me .—to help me for Eve's sake more even than for mine." He glanced aside apprehensively at the nurse. The sick man read his meaning and said faintly: "Nurse I Nurse ! will you leave me alone for a few minutes with Mr. Deering? '' The pleasant-faced woman in her hospital uniform smiled her assent and softly went away. John Stirling set his lips tightly Stia turned his head upon his hot pillow. Hifi brother-in-law bent over him. "John!"— he whispered; "John,? listen to me. I've an awful story to tell you. It concerns you nearly as closely as it does me, because I've married your sister, and if ria shown up she will be ruined, too."</p>
        <p>" Shown up ? Ruined!" Miles Deenng nodded. "Yes. Half an hour ago Pinkerton came down to say that the auditors have discovered something Wrong iii the books. There have been defalcations amounting to close upon twenty thousand pounds. Did you know anything about it, ,Join? Did you suspect it ? * John Stirling looked up sharply. "Did I know? Did I suspect anything? Certainly not. Should I have -kept silent if I had had any suspicions? Should I have let the criminal Work go on? Surely, Miles, you know me better than that! Even as it is, I feel that there must be some mistake—that it cannot be. There,is not one of all the men who have access to OE books whose honour and honesty I could not vouch for as for my own." " Not so, John. You are wrong. You can't onswer for other tnen. You would have vouched for me' but you would have been wrong. For I did it—l "</p>
        <p>- " Yes, I. I'd got into difficulties over horses and the Stock Exchange, and I needed money and the governor wouldn't give it ;to me, so I took it—fifty at a time at first, then a hundred and then five and six hundred at a time until I'd got close on twenty thousand pounds. I cooked the accounts so carefully that I never thought the deficit would be found out; but they found it out somehow, and I'm in a tight corner."</p>
        <p>"My God " gasped John. Stirling. " You I You! And you have married Eve I " His hands that had been clenched with pain now remained clenched with despair.</p>
        <p>"Miles it's not true. You must be ill !" Miles Deering laughed a short bitter laugh. "It's no good you're deluding yourself, ,rw! I've done the ugly job, and the only (1 ass no now is, how am Ito get out of it." bo "There is only one way in which I can saved, and with me my wife—only one way." ko "What is that way I" The sick man sP° quickly and eagerly. f "It is this, John." Miles took one 0- brother-in-law's clenched hands in his, lin, t gripped it desperately. "It !is this, John--blt you take my guilt upon yourself. No, no;d°l' start I You are a doomed man. I need If mince matters about that, for you Youlse4 have just told .me so. You must, die, and allflo', at once—perhaps this very night—while llnsbi, live to be an old man of eighty. You cannot r punished; even if you were guilty of made; You wulol not now be called upon to • pay If, earthly penalty. , You cannot stiffer at 0 while you can save me—me, your friend, the 0 of the man Who has been a second father to I°ll' and tip husband of your sister." " Miles ! " "Look here, John Do you remember days when we were at college together! Do 3,17 i, remember what chums we were, and how one 63-, you said to a fellow who told some nasty lie abt me thit you were prepared to defend my Iloilo': as firmly as your own? Do you remember thol John r Well, now you've got a chance to Maw my honour. You've only got to scrawl af% words on a scrap of paper and sign your 133112; under them, and the thing will be don.; You'll have saved me and Eve and tui father's name, and my mother's liappiatos end Lena's pride. I believe you love Leo; Think of what it would mean to her—to be happiness and her future—if I were publicly er posed as a thief! Think of it, John!! of "My Goel !" came once more from the lipo - the man who lay racked with suffering. Miles bent lower over him. ph "Will you do it John?" he panted. " you do it &amp;del save me? It won't matter to Y°"" You're going to die. And if God sees these thing:f' as we are told He does, He'll conut this to VA credit in the next world. What do you S" Will you do it and save me!" • And then the answer came faintly and aril' isedly from the helpless man on the bed. 1.</p>
        <p>Miles Deering drew a deep breath of unutb;ro° relief. He felt in his pockets and presently V°. duced a piece of paper and a pencil. "You must write it," he said hoarsely. "Cll you do it? Can you. use your hand to write ? " , r "Yes; only Rut the paper on something liarti;., The guilty man fetched a book and held tn; paper steadily upot) it, while the innocent• vr; strove to write. "After a moment John Stiriolo gave up the attempt.</p>
        <p>"I can't do it," he said despairingly, at last. With an inward oath, Wes took-the pencil. "I'll write it and you can sign it," be sharply. Then after a moment: "No; Paha; I'd bitter call the nurse." And he rang the bell. Arle Promptly the nurse reappeared and —1 motionher to a seat. • t "Mr Stirling desires to make a statemen,* t he said. "Will you sit by, him and write w--a be dictatei?" She seated herself with pen an, paper before, them and then slowly John Stirline spoke these words: "I, John Stirling, do hereby confess that 44. and I alone, am gkulty of the embezzlernent.!!•:, over twenty thousand pounds of money belong7o to the firm of Archiba Detring &amp; Company, of• which firm I hold and have held a position confidence." d• Mi.lNee,7hanyoitilingMlihsitm rntallimageralteo s:gflr. :rus'it: your name. You must IRO it. tWO force yourself to do it. Only two words-- little words—' John Siling —au By the sheer driving force of iron sick man's shaking fingers had traced the pencil the letters of the name Stirling,' and the man whom he had saved Bnstoll the paper back. "Now the nurse will witness it," he said, the unsuspecting woman added her siguati ‘• Ailsa Pen." "All's'well, John." said Miles. But John Stirling had swooned. * * * • * •</p>
        <p>S * *</p>
        <p>And many times that day Miles Deering ' back to the doer of that room and lisi anxiously to the sick man's laboured breatl "If only he would uie to-night t" he mutt " If only he.Wmht die to-night."</p>
        <p>' CHAPTER IV. "Thou shalt not bear false witness."</p>
        <p>Something ,approaching a miracle had-ioniV: at Quex ,Wed. For a fortnight John Stull life had hung by a.thread. Life and Death fought .for him above his bed, and, at lot,. had .conquered. A London specialist • had ; summoned to .see him, and this great declared that he would surely live. And had been -rejoicing at Quex Woods ; rejoicing all 'hearts save the hearts of the master of house himself ad of hi •son Miles. Fol., latter had east honour to the winds, and BD," his father the • confession which he induced 4( Stirling to sign. 1,</p>
        <p>, Up to the present, Sir Archibald had OPIII no. word, to the sick man shoat the grave ' painful subject. But to-day he felt he must 51/eDo To-day it would he safe to speak, for to-del an! Miles and Stirling found themselves aloaol "The time has come. John," said the to of the great firm, "when I must speak.,J you about your confession of guilt with res,g to the lately discovered defalcations in the books. Surely I need not tell you how gr"oof and shaken I was by that confession ; to that you—you whom I loved and honoured-10 the son of my old and trusted friend ell° le , have been guilty of embezzlement ; you, Irb° another few months . would have becereehae partner in the firm ! God knows, the MO smitten me. body and soul." ea "Sir Archibald I " John Stirling lead state oir his couch. 'You are judging me wronglY, ArcliihAd, I am not guilty of this thing." " Not guilty 7 Not guilty?" "No. as Heaven is my witness, I a7° guilty." rieg, A tremor ran through the figure of Miles Dee as he stood at the distant window. •b,ela "Is not this your confession ? " Sir Arcial Deering drew from his pechet a folded pa"pers eoV opened it before Jclut Stirlinds eyes. • this your signature ? The acon,ed man took the document and slowly. Then ben: 'dos ci his set lips to aressve" ottr " The signature is mine—yes. Bat ask 'ill son what the truth is." A word from hi° set the matter right. He will tell you the truth. it " Miles!" cried Sir Archibald in astonisll. Miles Deering had become suddenly liagoleirookA his face was of a greyish pallor; yet in John's eyes and lied coolly. Ole bout t "A word from nee? I know nothing 00 accursed business, except that you dictate sit' confession to me and signed it, and. told Me your own lips that you were guilty." b 6 • * * • • * oavi.e• „41 That , night an unexpected and re;:ar letter Was delivered at Quex 'Woods- 'Pit ki ea Deering: • It's contents were brief. Wrl °thee the front of a half sheet of paper were Words : si%teeetLt " See Exodus, twentieth chapter, ed. verse." At this curious unsigned message Mlles end aloud. But his laugh was mirthless, bead, rose from his chair, and with unatea(rlYsheivesa took down a Bible from one of the libra Yeastofq: Turning the pages with hasty, tillaegua fingers, he found the place he sought,a,ain.4 to, "Thou shalt not bear false teitlass (of neighbour." t of Parlor° He looked again at the half-shee , some sign of its sender. On the froiit.Fle bed; was no sign, but when he looked pr writtio' it he found some more words flurstL3 They were these: "'know the truth. Jude Bryce." 101 _fd StO,Jor [The continuation of this poivu, ~_tut ly pa •e. be found in ANswalts, the great 1.?,1 .ready at all newsagents OLI Nad 441'`` 'October. 2141 Price ld.}</p>
