      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>CHAPTER In the Valley of the Shadow.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER In the Valley of the Shadow.</p>
        <p>The home-coming ball was in progress at Quel Woods, bra an . air of gloom seetned to have settled down upon the gathering. Many of the guests were asking each other whether it would not be kinder towards their hosts to leave the house at once than to stay. The cotillon was beginning and the daughter of the house had some days ego promised to dance it with the man who now , lay upstairs injured and* unconscious—John Stirling. She consented now, at ther mother's urging, to take Captain Terriss for her partner in ins stead, arid he was leading her to her place, when a keen-featured, middle- aged man .who had long been known as the best doctor in or aroand Stoke Bassett, hurriedly pushed his way through the fashionable throng. "has Deering, may I have a word with you?" 4‘ esyes ." She said, eagerly. "Mr. Stirling has asked me to find you and tell you, that he prays you to come and speak with hida for a few moments. Will you do it?" The girl turned to her partner. "Captain Terries, I am so very sorry to disappoint, you but you mutt fo find some other partner r the cotillon. I am going upstairs to see Mr. Stirling." A cloud of mortification darkenedllthe face of the dashing young gentleman from Texas. ' "Can't you wait and go to him afterwards, - Miss Deering? 1 "The wish of a sick man comes before every- thing else," she answered, almost with a touch. of rebuke in her voice. With her heart beating faster she turned and went away with the doctor, unconscious in her anxious eagerness that nearly every eye in the room followed her movements. The doctor entered the sick room first and walked softly to the bedside. "Miss Deering has come to you," the girl heard him say in a low voiee as he bent over the bed, "sit, is here with me now." "Ah the word escaped the sick man's lips as if it broke from them against his will, "that is good of her. And now, will you leave us for a few minutes 7 I want to be almae with her—alone." Lena Deering heard him say these words and then watched Dr. Brunker whisper to the nurse and then lead her from the room. She moved then to the bedside, and stood there looking down at the sick man. He put out one of his hands as if seeking something, and she bent down and took it in hers, speaking then for the first time. "You asked for me?" she said very quietly. "I was glad, because I had suffered very much while waiting to hear that you were conscious. It has been terrible to dance and to smile, knowing that you were here—like this." A fleeting gleam of joy came into the eyes Of the sick man. "You were sorry, then ? " he asked her, quickly. "You grieved for me?" "I do grieve for 'you very deeply." Lena Deering bent her head Power over his hand. "Heaven knows we would have given all we have in the world to ,spare you this fearful calamity. You have all our sympathy, Mr. k Stirling. It has been so terrible for poor Eve, too—to have her home-coming shadowed by a : blow like this I But you will soon get well— you will get well again before very long !' John Stirling strove to shake his head. A stange expression was creeping over his face. "I shall never get well again," he said.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>CHAPTER 111. Saved!</head>
        <p>CHAPTER 111. Saved!</p>
        <p>Miles Deering was breakfasting late and alone the next morning—in fact the hour would have better suited lunch—when the butler brought him a card.</p>
        <p>" Mr. Pinkerton has come, and is asking to see you, Sir," he said. "I've shown him into the • library." - - -</p>
        <p>"M. Pinkerton ! " Miles Deering leaned back in his chair in apparently the moat extreme astonishment. And, oddly enough, the colour was leaving his face. "Mr. Pinkerton!' be repeated slowly. Air. Pinkerton was the head clerk in the office of Messrs Archibald Deering and Co., of Grace- church Street, and a visit from him at Quex Woods was a very remarkable and portentous Sign, especially when Sir Archibald was in toWn an he had gone up by an early train that morn- ing. Very well," said the son and heir of the head of the firm, after a pause, addressing the footman. "You can tell Mr. Pinkerton I am coming at once."</p>
      </div>
