      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES</p>
        <p>" MARIAN' NE FARNIZZGHAM."</p>
        <p>The 4"ree Church Chronkle for November, of • we have received an early copy, contains interesting sketch, with portrait, of Marianne tarningham, The well-known journalist and I/Q'tess. Miss Farningliam is taking an active Part by public spooking in the promotion of the l'ew Free Church Girls' Guild. For over forty- Years she has been a regular contributor to l'ne Christian World. Scarcely a number has appeared without a poem or an article from her 1)etl• The Free Church Chronicle, by permission • the. proprietors of the Christian \World, teprints the poem which Miss FarninghaN con- ttibuted to the first.number of that journal in 1857. The verses are in the well-known style • the authoress—simple, sincere, sweet and 41elpful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>DR. PARKER AND DR. MCLAREN.</head>
        <p>DR. PARKER AND DR. MCLAREN.</p>
        <p>The meetings of the Baptist Union at Ellin- rlrgh, and of the Congregational Union at (anchester this autumn have been , of quite eeptional interest. It was a great disappoint-</p>
        <p>tent to the Baptists that Dr. Parker, by reason ()f his health, was unable t 9 attend the Edinburgh _</p>
        <p>„Itteetings, and to the Congregationalists that McLaren for the same reason was unable to 1)131)ear before them. Dr. Parker's heart has een "murmuring,” and .he has been strictly ;)Innianded to confine his work to the City (=rnple. In the closing passage of his</p>
        <p>peat speech at Manchester, fir. Parker I'll'l that all his outside engagements "ere cancelled. "I retired to think, to serve Ittletly, and soon to go Up." Dr. Parker is &amp;lather fond of talking in this strain, and his Tends need not be very much alarmed thereby. tur. NlcLaren, in the course of a characteristic °te to Dr. Parker, spoke of himself and his</p>
        <p>'lleed as two "almost worn-out hulks, fitted ZdY to be towed out of action." But when Dr. :-arker read these words he shouted out loudly, t` deny it." As far as their friends can judge, bliere are some years of work still before the rave old veterans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>TWO ABERDONIANS.</head>
        <p>TWO ABERDONIANS.</p>
        <p>iTwo Aberdonians have just settled in London distinguished positions—Principal Forsyth tlitekney College, and Dr. John Hunter in King's Weigh House Chapel. About thirty 'l4l's ago, when young men in Aberdeen, these :111111ent Congregationalists became fast friends, 14,11'1 have remained such ever since. When the t 4tlonal Free Church Council met at Liverpool ial'er two years ago, Dr. Forsyth and Dr. Hunter 111°th took part in the proceedings, and spent as „lieh time as they could in each other's cora- -1,"113', recalling the old days in the far north. len Dr. Hunter was exorcising his ministry ork, as the successor of James Parsons, r• 1). L. Moody came to the town, and ta`4,ached in Mr. Hunter's church. The young t 141ster and a friend who discussed the sermon, qi4llle to the conclusion that it was the poorest Iter)tirse they had ever heard by a layman. occasion was Mr. Moody's first visit to this it'Untry. Dr. Hunter's revised opinion of Mr' °°(br's preaching is very different.</p>
        <p>f</p>
        <p>i ,</p>
