      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, OCTOBER 25, 1901.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>WARRINGTON TOBACCONISIS AND THE TRADE CRISIS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TOBACCONISIS AND THE TRADE CRISIS.</p>
        <p>Proposed Combination.</p>
        <p>A meeting in connection with the Warrington and District Tobacconists' Association was held on Thursday last week. Mr. Joseph Beswick presided, and there was a representative attendance. Mr. Thomas Glover, of Chester, was present as a delegate of the United King- dom Tobacco Dealers Alliance, and delivered ar address on the impending crisis in the tobacco trade. kr. Hey, of Halifax, who had been announced to attend, was unable to come owing to pressure of other engagements. —Mr. Newton (hon. secretary and treasurer) read a communication from Mr. Charlton, of Earle stown, stat- ing that he was also unable to be present. Mr. GLOVER, in the course of his address, said they had been called together at this important juncture in the history of the tobacco trade, to take counsel in the matter of further developments. They were all con- versant with the new phase of trading, and the combine principle had come to stay. They therefore wished to make the retailers' position as secure as possible in face of the coming tug of war. The scheme by which it was hoped to prevent the utter collapse of the trade,to make them able to stand against all comers, was the one they were there to discuss, and it was this. They must combine both the productive and the dis- tributive elements of the trade, for the one without the other was powerless. Mr Glover proceeded to explain how this was to be accomplished. Messrs Salmon and Gluckstein, the well-known firm of Glasgow. he said, had offered to ally the firm with the Alliance, to com- mit its future to the hands of the Alliance, and manu- facture for them any tobaccos they asked for equal to sample and provide them with the means of making an honest living. After paying three per cent, of profit to the firm, the rest of the profits would be divided at the year's end amongst the members of the Alliance, pro rata to purchase. (Hear, hear.) By this means every tobacco dealer _would bezonne his own manu- facturer. The salvation of the tobacconist was in the cultivation of his own lines and the raising Of himself from the ruck of a mere purveyor of others' brands to that of a dealer in his own brands. They would not be tied -down to use Messrs. Salmon and.Gluckstein's lines solely, they would be at liberty to ibuy where they liked, when they liked, but if they bought from the firm they would participate in the profits. The firm reserved their proprietary lines for the members of the Alliance and themselves and would charge anyone not a member of the Alliance 10 per cent, more on all goods than members paid. The object of that meeting was to ask them to throw in their lot with the firm, whom hitherto, they had considered their biggest enemy, yet who had come forward and offered them that which their greatest friends had refused. The project was commendable in that it embraced for the first time (to his knowledge) the principle that the distributive side should have some share in the manufacturing profits. He would ask them to examine the project in the light of the treatment received from manufacturers,and after discussing the matter on its broadest lines, to give their opinion without prejudice. (Applause). The CHAIRMAN invited the members to express their opinions freely and an interesting discussion followed. Ultimately it was unanimously agreed to give the Association's approval of and support to the suggested scheme.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. BESIVICK seconded by Mr. PERRIS, a vote of thanhs was passed to Mr. Glover for his address. '</p>
        <p>A similar compliment was accorded Mr. Beswick for presiding and the meeting terminated. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>WARRINGI-ON MARKET. TRANSACTION.</head>
        <p>A WARRINGI-ON MARKET. TRANSACTION.</p>
        <p>Alleged Receiving of Stolen Gloves. At the Manchester Cuy Police Court on Thursday last wuk three men named James Halliwell, and Benjamin Ginsberg, of Bewsey-street, Warrington, and Isaac Altman, of Waterloo-road,Cheetharn,were charged with having stolen and received 65 dczen pairs of leather gloves, value £55, the property of Mr Tom 11. Lambert, a commia4ion agent, of Piccadilly, between September 17ch and 21-t las% Mr. W Cobbett appeared for the prismer Altman, and the other two men were represented by Mr. Leake.</p>
        <p>'The C3S9 for the prosecution was that the gloves were missed from the warehouse of the prosecutor, and that on the 21st nit a market draper named James West bought 40 dozen pairs of the same kind of gloves from the prisoner Bothwell at Warrington, paying 6s 6cl a dez-as pairs. West sold them at the rate of Is a pair or is 81 for two pairs. Though he did not think they were stolen he thought there was something wrong with the gloves, and came to Manchester to seek advice. I The result was that Detective-Inspector Bough visited Warrington on the 9th inst and arrested Halliwell and Ginsborg, both of whom were stall-holders in the market there, They were charged with the offence, and both replied that they had no idea the gloves were stolen. They also stated that the gloves bad been purchased as a job lot from to Jews. Just after midnight last Friday Inspector Hough and Ginsberg. who was on bail, went to Waterloo-road, I where Altman was arrested just as he was about to enter his house. Be told the officer that three weeks , previously be was at the Central Station, when a Jew same up to bins and asked him to buy some gloves. Be replied that he could not do so, but agreed to go with him to Warrington and help to dispose of them. The result was that the gloves were sold for something over I £l2, of which he received 30s. When formally charged Altman said "Pdon't understand it at all." Halliweh's story that he bought the gloves from two Jews wail corroborated by a witness named Alfred Frank Peacock, a whrdesale warehouseman, of Warring-to:1,1 who *as called by Mr Leake. He heard Halli well bar-1 , gaining with them, and subsequently learned from him I Ithat be bad bought the gloves and sold them again at a profit of £4. Witness added ' that he had bought job goods even cheapei than those in question. k Mr Leaks also elicited from other witnesses that there was np concealment on the part of Bothwell, or Ginsberg,both of:whom bore unimpeachable characters according to the evidence of Market Inspector Place. Mr Headlatn discharged Bothwell and Ginsberg and the former was called as a witncss. Halliwell then repeated the statement he made to the police, producing the cheque by which he paid for the goods. The cheque was paid to A man not in custody. Mr. Cobbett, on behalf of Altman, who pleaded not guilty, said that there was not a scrap of evidence to show who stole the gloves, and that the prisoner's ac- 'count was perfectly consistent with the evidence that hid been given. It was clear from the evidence of the last witness that Altman was not the principal. Altman was then committed for trial to the Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>Public-House Trust.</head>
        <p>The Public-House Trust.</p>
        <p>'According to the advance report of the Central Public-house Trust Association, of which Earl Crewe is the president, and the Bishop of Chester and Mr. Joseph Chamberlain, M.P.. are vice-presi- dents. Liverpool, at present, appears to be the only town in Lancashire where a Public-house Trust Company has been actually registered, but organizing committees are at work for establishing them in other large towns, and they will soon be beard of in the manufacturing centres of Lanca- shire. The Liverpool and district company contains an influential list of names of gentlemen who are willing to act on the Council, including the Lord- Lieutenant of the County, the Lord Mayor and the Mayors of Birkenhead, Bootle, St. Helens, South- port, Warrington and Wigan. The authorised capital is £50,000, of . which 20,000 shares of £1 each will be offered for subscription. The maxi- mum dividend is 4Y, per cent. cumulative. At Thornton, near Blackpool, the Fleetwood Bench has on the sppliestion of the United Alkali Company, granted a provisional full licence to a company to be formed to work it. Ths Trust Companies are open to accept the management of licensed houses which are acquired by municipal corporations in the course of public improvement.</p>
      </div>
