      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. Another Meeting of Conservative</head>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS. Another Meeting of Conservative</p>
        <p>Council.</p>
        <p>Lively Proceedings.</p>
        <p>The adjeurned meeting of the council of the Warrington Conservative Association:was held on Monday evening, in the Assembly Room, at the Conservative Club, under the presidency of Mr John White (chairman of the association). Amongst those present were—Aldermen Sutton (hon secretary) a3d Hutchinson, Councillors Davies, Baird, Greening Hulse. Smethurst, Sampson, Forshaw, Seillcock, and Greening, Edwin Woods, C S Bur- gess, Dr Braids,. Thomas Mason, George Pilling, John Pemberton, W 3 Seaw, George Jolley. William Dooley, T El Ashcroft, James Bolton, W L Williams, H Isher- wood, R Chadwick, George Watkins (manager of the club), William Holbrook (registration agent), and Her- bert Hayes (manager of the Junior Conservative Club). CANDIDATES APPROVED. The CHAIRMAN, in opening the meeting, caid the business was very simple ; it was to confirm or other- wise what had been done at the different ward meetings. A meeting had been held in every ward. The Chairman then read the results of the different meetings, and it was decided to approve of the selection of Mr. Forshaw for the Town Hall Ward, Mr. John . Baird for the St. John's Ward, and Mr. S. Davis for the Howley Ward. WHITECRtiSS WARD. The CHAIRMAN having invited an expression of opin- ion, Mr. W. W. WESTWELL briefly alluded to Whitecross Ward, where it had been decided that there should be no contest in opposition to Mr. Brough. He said that no one had e greater respect for Mr. Brough than he had, but it was out of place that he should be allowed a walk-over. The present was about the best time to oppose. Mr. EDWIN WOODS suggested that the matter should be left open, and the diecussion ceased. ORFORD WARD. COUNCILLOR WILKINSON'S CANDIDATURE. ATTACK ON COUNCILLOR GREENING. Mr PETER WNLLS thought it would not be wise to allow the present retiring Liberal representative of the ward, Councillor Wilkinson, to be elected again. The ward was as strongly Conservative as any in Warringtoa He Was astonished at the ward meeting on Wednesday night to hear the utterances of the Chairman(Councillor Greening) who could speak of nothing else but the Radical candidate. It looked as if there was a compact between Councillor Wilkinson and Councillor Greening to assist eacn other. He believed Mr Wilkinson was the only retiring candidate who had answered the circular which had been sent to the candidate. He said he had Councillor Greening on his side and he could fight as he liked. Mr W J Srlaw, said he was pleased to hear Mr Wells seek as he had done about Conservatism v Radicalism. For the life of him he could not understand whn the town had been disfranchised, as the ratepayers had a perfect right to send representatives to the Council. Allusion was then made to a speech delivered by Coun- cillor Greening at the Horse and Jockey Hotel, Winwick- road, in connection with the distribution of the bowling prizes. Councillor Greening was inv,ted by the Presi lent of the Bowling Club (Councillor Wilkinson) to attend, and he (Mr Shawl read Councillor Greening's I speech with some surprise. He should like to know if it was a Tory speech. (Applause.) Mr Shaw then read the following extracte from the speech .—" Now he wished to speak about a matter which would I concern the President on the Ist November. As they knew his term of office in the Council would expire on that day, but he sincerely hoped that if there was a con- test in the Orford Ward he would be returned by a large majority. (Applause ) When he (the speaker) contested the Ward he received the treat support of Liberals and Conservatives, and he had not forgotten the kindness then shown to tira. He should take his stand In the side of the President at the coming election. There was only one aide amongst them. They did not fight politically, but for good old Warrington. (Applause). S 3 far as he was concerned he would do all that lay iii his power to secure Mr. Wilkinson's return, and he hoped the members of the club would di the same. He was the right man in the right place." Mr. Shaw commented on the speech, and observed that Lao alen knew Mr. Wilkinson to be a " decent sort of chap." He was also sure that they were all for" good ell Warrington." but some worked it one way and some another, and if Councillor Greening was standing on the side of Liberalism he was in the wrong position. (Applause.) Mr. ASHCROFT said it WAS evidently the feeling of the room that there should he a fight all round. He was sorry to hear of Couccillor Greening being on the Radi- cal side, but they had some weak-kneed Conservatives amongst them. He was, therefore, pleased to see cer- tain gentlemen speaking their minds. (Applause.) Councillor GREENING explained that a meeting' Was duly called im the Orford Ward. The Cheirenen of the ward was not present and he (Mr. Greening) was put in the chair. For fully five minutes be eat there and asked for a resolution ard there was none. Mr. Peter Wells woe present. He (Mr. Greening) waited fully live minutes for a proposition and he, as chairman for the evening, proposed that Councillor Wilkmson be not opposed. He was undoubtedly a good councillor and a good representative of the ward. He should support him. When he (Councillor Greening) fought Orford Ward he distinc;ly stated that he fought It not as a Conservative but as an inhabitant of War- rington who cared more for the prosperity of Warring- ton than for mere party. He repeated that assertion that evening. Mr Shaw had alluded to his speech at the Horse and Jockey, and he (the speaker), repeated again that Councillor Wilkinson was an admirable mem- ber of the Town Council and fairly represented the feel- ings of the ward. Mr SHAW: No one has said that Councillor Wilkinson is not an admirable councillor,but he does not belong to the Conservative side, and we wish to strengthen our hands in the Town Council knowing that our majority is so week. (Hear, hear.) Councillor GREENING: All I have to say is that I did not fight Orford Ward, and was not elected as a Tory representative but to represent the ward, and as long as I am in the Council I will represent the ward fairly and squarely, Liberals and Conservatives alike. I don't care the toss of a button what is said to-night. I have influence in that ward. The ward rises above the petty factions of a municipal election, and I say that Coun- cillor John Wilkinson had no opposition and was carried unanimously at the ward meeting. I shall not say any- thing more than that. Mr WELLS I can only take it that Councillor Greening is defying the whole of the Conservative machinery. (Applause). Mr SHAW : Can we make a proposition with respect to the ward, The CHAIRMAN replied in the affirmative. Mr SHAW : Then I propose that Mr Edwin Woods be the candidate. (Loud cheers). Mr PETER WELLS seconded the motion, and said the proposition had " lifted him about two feet higher." (Laughter), he wished they ceuld get Mr Woods to stand for the ward. Be (the speaker) was only a little I chap but he could pledge himself to do a lot more than some of the big onee. (Laughter). Mr. EDWIN Woons was much obliged to Mr. Rhavo and to Mr. Wells, but he had stated ia that room that he did not seek office and did not intend to do so. He would keep his word, however anxious they were to send him to the Council. He Ma nked them very much but be could not accept the position under any cirenre- stances. Mr. SHAW thought the ward should arrange for a Com- mittee to find a candidate, and let the name be submit- ted at a meeting to be beld on the following day. tie proposed that a committee be formed to select a candidate to oppose Mr. Wilkinson and that the Ward Committee be called together again. The CHAIRMAN thought it was rather futile to call a meeting again so soon, but if the meeting wished it, it could be called. Mr. C. S. BURGESS asked if the meeting in the ward was properly called, and to Ole Mr. Holbrook replied in the affirmative, stating that the usual circulars were sent cut. The CHAiRMAN added that there were 16 present, and that on the motion of Mr GREENING seconded by Mr Robinson, it was decided to allow Mr Wilkinson to have a walk-over. There was no amendment. Mr. SELAW 'S motion was carried, and it was decided to call a further meeting of the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FAIRFIELD WARD.</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr A J THORPE, as secretary of the Ward Committee, explained what bad been done in this ward. Mr Williams was mentioned as a candidate to oppose Mr Duncan, but he declined to stand and no candidate was selectee. Alderman EIIITCHINSON, as &amp;airman of the ward, stated that be was present at the meetio,g in question. Different gentlemen had been spoken to asking them to contest the ward, but they cou!il not get a candidate. It was ultimately suggested tbat, as a mark of respect to Mr Parr, it would bo as well not to seek a contest this year. The action of the committee was approved. ST. AUSTIN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>Mr W W WESTWELL said there was not a large meet- ing. Mr Ashcroft was present, and took some excep- tion to the proceedings, but he thought be might now say that he fell in with the resolution, not to contest the ward. (Clear, hear.) The arrangtment was approved. LATCHFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>MR. PEMBERTON SELECTED AS A CANDIDATE. The CrrattaraN atated that at the ward meeting, it was decided to recommend Mr. John Pemberton as the can- didate. Mr. W. J. WILSON said that if he got the support of the association Mr. Pemberton would be "run in." They did not want one half to fight and the other half to act in the other way. If all put their shoulders to the wheel there was no doubt they would oust Mr. Ellison, and have a Conservative for the ward once roore. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. PILLING observed that Mr. Pemberton received ten votes out of 15. (Applause.) Mr. Thomas KNIGHT asked if the heads of the party would go aga;nst the ac'ion of the committee. He con- tended that the borough had been disfranchised long enough. It was said that those who were in the Council were afraid of losing their seats. There seemed to be a great deal cf lukewarmnes3 amongst the members of the party. It was time the compromise was done away with, or their machinery would become rusty and re- quire oiling. There was no compromise at the general election. It was becoming a by-word that the Conser- vatives dare not fight. The Liberal candidate in the Latchford Ward was the only man who had snubbed Mr. Parr. (Applause.) Mr. JOHN PE3IBERTON : All I require is the working element of the party. I will pay all my own expenses. (Cheers). lam quite certain that if I have the help of the workers that I shall be returned for Latchford. Mr. W. J. BLOOMFIELD said they could run Mr. Pem- berton in. He could get 50 Orangemen to work for nothing on the day of the election. Mr. PEMBERTON'S nemination was then unanimously agreed to. NO COMPROMISE IN EXISTENCE.</p>
        <p>The vacancy in Wnitecross Ward was again alluded to. In answer to Mr. Pemberton, Tins CHAIVIAN said he stated distinctly at the last meeting that there was no compromise in existence, and that there had been no negotiations with regard to the coming elections with the Liberal party in any shape or form. He said that distinctly, and he repeated it on this occasion ; also that if the wards met and candidates were found that they would have to pay their own expenses, but that if they came out as Conservatives they would have the whole of the machinery of the association placed at their service. Why all these insinuations were made again to-night he did not know. Be said now, after careful thought, that he thought it was very unjust to corns here and repeat the Same thing after what he distinctly stated at the last meeting. (Hear, hear.) • Councillor GREENING : It was made clear at the last meeting that any independent candidate would have the whole of the Conservative Association at his disposal provided he paid his own (xpenses. Mr. EDWIN WODDS How will yon act if a candidate cornea against Mr. Wilkinson and Mr. Greening does not help? Councillor GREENING : .If anyone does oppose Coun- cillor Wilkinson I shall fight for him (Councillor Wilkin- son.) I have influence in the ward and I can put workers in. If they oppose Mr Wilkinson they will have to reckon with me. (Sensation.) Mr. WELLS : After these observations I for one shall look upon Councillor Greening as a" down-right" Radical in the future. (Applause). Councillor GREENING : Thank Ton. On the motion of Mr. BURGESS, Seconded by Council— lor GREENING, it was decided that the decision of the Ward Committee with respect to Whitecross Ward be confirmed. ALDERMAN SUTTON AND THE lACE OF C kNDIDATES.</p>
        <p>Alderman et TTON stated that at times the associafien had been put in a corner as they could get no candidates. Tney wanted men of position to represent them in the Council, men who paid large rates and who had great interests at stake. They were the people who should come forward. It had been impossible for tbe associa- tion to do other than it had done in reference to elec- tions which had taken place during the oast few years. They heard a good deal about being disfranchised, but it had been because they had no candidates, He had gone about the town almost on his hands and knees asking people who ought to represent them to come for- ward and take a part in the walk of the town. They had in consequence been forced into the compact. :Mr EDWIN WOODS thought the meeting ought to ac- cept the explanation. He urged the meeting to prepare six months beforehand for the elections twelve months hence. Some discussion took place with reference to allowing Councillor Williams Roberts a walk over last year,and it was contended by one or two speakers that if he hod been opnosed he would have been defeated. Mr THOMAS Kmicare asked who was at the head of the clique. He could guess the second time and miss for the first time on Purpose. (Laiaghter)• THE CHAIRMAN DEFENDS .THE ACTION OF THE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. L. WILLIAMS said Conservatism was synony- mous with progress and they ought to strive to their utmost to maintain their majority in th.ir own local Parliament as well as in the House of Commons. If they did not do so they would probably become prc-Raers or something of the sort. Looking round the room he could see no lack of good men who would make good candidates, such for instance as Messrs. Burgess,Voisey, and the Chairman. (Applause.) Ho had much pleasure in proposing a vote of thanks to the Chairman for pre- siding over the meeting. Councillor SIIILLCOCIC seconded, and said he should have been better pleased if there had been no contest in any of the wards this year cut of respect to Mr. Parr. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Themotion was heartily agreed to. The CHAIRMAN, in reoly, said he had never attempted 'to stifle discussion whether it was pleasant to him or unpleasant. Although he did not agree with all that had ibemn said that evening he should endeavour loyally to carry out he wishes of the meeting to the best of his ability. Although it was not pleasant to have these bickerings, perhaps what had been said would arouse the spirit of the general body of the association. They were apt to become apathetic when they went on year after year without a fight. He agreed with what Alderman Sutton had said about the difficulty of getting candidates. It was useless to blame the Council or the committees because they had not been able to find can- didates. They had begged and prayed people to stand. For the future, as it seemed to go so much against the grain, they would endeavour to have no compact, but all present must help to find candidates. They could not fight a municipal battle without a candidate. NO CANDIDATE FORTHCOMING FOR ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>A meetinz of the Orford Ward Committee was held at the Junior Conservative Club, Orford-lane, on Tues- day evening in aecordanco with the resolution adopted at the meeting of the cmancil of the a9soelation. There was a large attendance but no candidate could be f ound. LIST OF CaNDIDaTES. .</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the candidates niminated:— Town Hall Ward Coun W J Forshaw (C) Whitecross Ward „ Walter ftronel (L) Harry Singleton (I) Bewsey Ward James Payton (I) Samuel Gough (I) Orford Ward Coon John Wilkingon (f.) St John's Ward Samuel Gough (I) Fairfield Ward „ David Sedg wick Duncan (L) Howley Ward St Austin.. Ward „ Thomas Burton (L) Latchford Ward John Pemberton (C)</p>
      </div>
