      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RATEPAYERS' MEETING. Mr. Norbury Wiliiams at • Warrington. Trading With the Corporation. Consolidaton of the Borough Debt.</head>
        <p>RATEPAYERS' MEETING. Mr. Norbury Wiliiams at • Warrington. Trading With the Corporation. Consolidaton of the Borough Debt.</p>
        <p>The Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, Warring- ton, was tilled to overflowing on Wednesday even- ing when a meeting which had been convened by Messrs. James Dodds, E. L, Fleming, John Pember- ton and John Upson was held, to which any rateplyer was invited who was in favour of :—(1) Consolidating the Borough debt. (2) Passing a resolution against any and all municipal trading, whether direct or indirect. (3) Electing a committee for the purpose of taking such steps as may be considered advisable for the efficient carrying out of the objects men- tioned. The announcement that Mr Norbury Manchester,would address the meeting, was no doubt responsible for a great deal of the interest manifested in the proceedings and some time before the hour advertised for the meeting to commence a large crowd had assembled outside the ball. Only those persons who had secured tickets were at first admitted, but when they had been accommo- dated the general public were allowed to occupy the remaining space. Among those present were Aldermen Shaw Green and Hutchinson and Coun- cillors Evans and Peacock. The chair was occu- pied by Mr James Dodds, and supporting him on the platform were :—Mr Norbury Williams, Mr E L Fleming and Mr John Pemberton. SPEECH BY MR. NORBUP.Y WILLIAMS.</p>
        <p>Mr. NOR—B-1712Y WILLIAM% who was received with loud and prolonged applause, said he always felt it to be a serious responsibility to address hi, fellow countrymen ' on municipal eubjects, for the reason that his words seemed to be so closely watched and carefully taken down that one had to weigh every nee of them, but he rejoiced to be in Warrington that night. He was asked that day when it got to be known that he was coming to Warrington—for he was down for a meeting in an hour and a half at Manchester—whether be was going to that wretched Warrington. (Laughter.) " Yes "he said t' I am going to that wretched, Warrington and we are going to-night to endeavour to &amp;tart a new order of things in Warrington." It was with that view he was presents on that platform. (Applause.) He had the distinguished honour and pleasure of bear- ing in Manchoster that afternoon at a non-political meeting, a great statesman who was speaking about another statesman equally great and now deceased. and in referring to the many great points in the character of that great dead man, the. speaker (Mr. John Morley)—(applause)—referred point</p>
        <p>edly to: Mr. Gladstone laying great stress in the early 1 years of his career, fifty years ago, upon the necessity for thrift and vigilance. Throughout his whole life Mr. Gladstone kept these two points in view, and if the burgesses of England and Wales, of whom there were at present over two millions, had kept constantly in view these two great characteristics of thrift and vigilance they would not be in the condition they were to-day in Warrington and in a number of other boroughs that he could name. There was much room for improvement in Warrington. It needed salvation very seriously and the sooner they set about making a start tol</p>
        <p>CLEAR TUTS AI7GEAN STABLE OF WARRINGTON, the better it would be for the Borough, and the better for the credit of every burgess of the town. To tell them the truth be should not care to be a burgess of Warrington to-day. (A. voice : It is a very responsible position). Somebody said, and said with truth, that it was a very responsible position. Yes, it was, but the responsibility of the position did not seem to him to .. have come home to nine men oat of ten in Warrington They seemed to be waiting f4)r something to turn up and for someone to do the work they ought to do. That was the curse of municipal Government. '(I voice : B?gin with the abuse). No, he was not going to talk about the abuse till he had talked about men woo had to deal with that abuse. He was going to talk about men who had got into the Council who had no business to be there and would not have been there if they (the audience) had been properly vigilant and bad a full eye to that economy which he had spoken of. Proceeding he said the Municipal Act of 1882 was their chatter so far as municipalities were concerned. it was the act which originally was passed through the House of Commons in 1335 before Queen Victoria came to the throne, but it was an act which very few muni- cipalities had fully availed themselves of and from the straight lines of which many municipal Councillors and Aldermen had very seriously wandered. (Hear, hear.) He had come particularly to speak on THE MATTER OF MUNICIPAL TRADING?</p>
        <p>He had not come to speak in any mealy-mouthed language. He was a straight up and down man, and he did not care whether a man was a Mayor or a Ceuucillor, if he dil the wrong thing, if he did the thing which the municipality itself ought not to condone or agree to he (Ur Williams) was there to say that man ought to, be put out of the Council. (Hear, hear.) Before he went on to that subject, he wanted very clearly to lay down before the consciences of those present and of those wno would see from the newspaper I reports what he had said, that it was they who had power to deal with this matter. The Chairman had just referred to their being disfranchised by the action of the political parties la the town. Now, he did not know what was the political complexion of their Council, he did not care two straws what its complexion was; all be cared was, were the men Who were la the Council, men ache had a single eye for the good of the town arid who never gave a single thought to making anything for themselves out of their connection with the Council. (Hear, hear.) Warrington is helpless and disfranchised " they told him. Nothing of the sort! If the men of Warrington had no more back bone, no more commonsense and no more determination than to allow the political parties to settle for them quietly in some back parlour,who was going to represeat them on the Town Council, then he said the men of Warrington were the ,men who were responsible, and not these wretched political committees. (Hear, hear.) He be- longed to a political committee, and was as strong a politician as any of them when it came to Imperial politics, but he had NEVER HEARD OF A MORE ARIUDD ARRANGEMENT in Eh., world than to go to a political association in order to decide who was the man that should go in to govern the town. If a.town allewed itself to drift, as Warrington had done, into tho hands of two political parties, who practically were dictating to the town as to which wards a fight should take place in, he said the burgesses had forgotten themselves. He hoped that one result ot that meeting and the projected meeting which would probativ be hell in abeet a week, would be that they were going to have a regular stir-up. (Laugliter and applause.) Leave the political parties out of the question altogether,let them go their way. What he had come to protest agaiest was that IF TaE MEN OF WARRINGION WOULD WAKE UP</p>
        <p>they could next week have a contest in every ward, where the mea were not of the egbt sort in these mat- ters. The men and not only the men but the women of Warrington should rise to the occasion. They had, so far as he had been able to judge, permitted themselves to ha the mere csts-paws of the political leaders, and he hoped that they,would came out of the leading strings ' of these men,and judge for themselves whether the time was ripe for a change of things to take place in the Council. Every trader should be excluded from the Council. He did not care who he was, and whether he traded through a limited company or not did not make a bit of difference. Do not let thorn be splitting straws about the morality of the thing..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRI DAY, OCTOBER</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>CITY FROM WHICH HE CAME</head>
        <p>THE CITY FROM WHICH HE CAME</p>
        <p>and in connection with which he held a responsible office was a city that he WU not ashamed of. It was a city, the Government of which was far from perfect, buc he could tell them that they made great efforts to keep it as near perfect as possible. Toey bad to deal witn these questions of trading on the part of C ouncillors:and Alderman and after a long period of striving, speaking and working, they had arrived at a condition of things such as this—that Manchester Would , never dream to-day of allowing to remain in the, City ' Council any man, however distinguished, of whatever psrty, and however long his services might have ex- tended, who either directly or indirectly, through him- self or through a limited company, was trading with the Council. (Applause). That was the stand Man- chester had taken and he should never be content with the local Government of England, until every borough and city, and every Urban and Rural District Cows cil had adopted the same distinct rule. (Applause). They boasted of their local Government in England and Wales and were pruud of it, but when men forgot the prov:- sions of the Act oi Parliament and the powers which they enjoyed under the law ,when they became lethargic and sleepy as Warrington had become, and allowed the law to become almost a dead letter, they must not be surprised it outsiders spoke of the town as that wretched Warrington. (Laughter.) They wanted to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>REOIOVE TEAT- DISGRACE FROOI WARRINGTO'</head>
        <p>REOIOVE TEAT- DISGRACE FROOI WARRINGTO'</p>
        <p>and to come out with a clean sheet. If thy were men worth a single sixpence morally, if they were men Wh3 had not sunk below the level of a go3d Briih citiz they would see tp it that ev.l7 ward in Waniugton, and there were nine of them, should at any rate have one mu standing on November let for whom the bur- gesses might record their vot,s apart from polities. There must not be a ward in Warrington to go uncon- tested. Trading with the Corporation through a ; limited company was a mere subterfug,e, and it was the most miserable and immoral subterfuge that ever was devised. It was a means of evasion which no honest man ghould allow himself to be a party to whether he was the Mayor or the simplest Councillor. If he did this, th 3 burgesses themselves should be 83 ashamed of him that they would</p>
        <p>EXCLUDE HIM FROM THE MUNCIE. (Applause.) In that great charter, the Municipal Cor- porations Act, it was stated that any person should be disqualified from being a Councillor if he had, directly lor indirectly, by himseif or his partner, any share or I interest in any contract or employment with, by, or on '1 behalf of the Council. The man was worthy of con- ' tempt who sailed round the Act of Parliament and made himself and wife and children, uncles and aunts, partners. (Applause.) That man was worthy of cc- tempt and should be driven out of municipal life. Men did try to get round Acts of Parliament if they found it was to their own interest and would put a little money in their pockets. They had tried it on in Manchester, but they clearly showed their man the rightabout and he was an Alderman. (Laughter.) He was an aldezmae of many years standing and had grown grey in the service, and wanted to trade with the Corporeicrt—a most natural thing,—the easiest way of making money in the woad. (Laughter). After referring at length to the fliggingbottom case, the speaker urged his hearers to put aside political' associatiens. Tell them t they were useless and were created for another object altogether, tell them that the men of Warrington were determined that on the Ist of November the nine men to be elected, whether they were the retiring or new men, should be definitels pledged to the very eyes to put down the trading on the part of the cluncillors, whether it be direct or indirect, by themselves or through a limited compane. If he heard in twenty-four hours that they had resolved to fight and be men and not elaves, then he would c 3me again next week. (applause). If he heard that they had " tucked" and had bowed down slavishly before two wretched political narties, then he would treat them as they de- served to be treated, with the contempt that was their portioa. (Loud applause.) Mr. Now:malt teen left to address a meeting at Manchester, amid loud cheeTs.</p>
        <p>----___, LOCAL GWERNMENT BOARD INQKIRY ASKED FOR.</p>
        <p>rg Mr. Gocroa then moved the i3lloWing resolution : " That notwithstanding the serious cha es made through the newspapers against several prominent members of the Corporation with regard to their having personally benefited, either directly or indirectly in coa- -1 nee;lon with contracts with:the Warrington Corporation, such charges have never been refuted or any proper inviry held by the Corporation to investigate such charges and this meeting now resolves that an application be made to Vie Local Government Board for a thorough inveitigation by way of a public!inquiry into such charges and into all Corporation contracts that the ratepayers may know who are the guilty parties and that they be dealt with .as they deserve."</p>
        <p>Mr. FLEMING seconded. The molution was carried, only Alderman Hutchin- son bolding up his hand against it.</p>
        <p>LETTER FROM MR. KENYON..</p>
        <p>Mr S KENYON wrote that though he might n3t he at the meeting tie considered that Mr Norbury Williams had rendered incalculable service to the cities and borcirh3 of this country. Unfortunately men often took part in public business tor selfish purposes and the gratification of their ambition, he thought i undesirable for members of the Corporation to be greatly interested either directly or indirectly in the contracts of that Corporation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>TEIE REPLIES_ OF CANDIDATES.</head>
        <p>TEIE REPLIES_ OF CANDIDATES.</p>
        <p>Mr E L FLEMING stated that the following letter had been sent to the candidates :—" Dear Sr,—Would you if elected be prepared to move or second, and in any case support the following. (1) That immediate steps be taken for the consolidation of the Borough debt, (2) Will you undertake to oppose on the 9th of November next the election of any Alderman who has traded either ' directly or indirectly by contract or sub-3ontract with the Corporation. Your reply by return of post will oblige as your answer is to be laid before a meeting of ratepayers now convened. We are, your obedient ser- vants. James Dodds, E I. Fleming, John l'embertoo and John Upon." _</p>
        <p>The Ifflowing replies had been received : Mr. T. Brough, 129, Weilfi,ld-streer, wrote :—"Father is away in Saltburn, Yorksnire, at present, for his hellth. He is very unwell.'' Couacillor 'Wilkinson replied ; "Your memo dated 17th inst to hand. I beg t 3 say that the 133r:31303 deht has been cons 4idated, brit I am prepared to support any financial scheme thee will result in a saving t 3 the rate- psyers. Also I shall oppose illegal tradiag, either directly or indirectly with the Corporation by any member of the Council."</p>
        <p>Councillor BURTON wrote :—" With reference to your letter of yesterday's date, lam ia favour of the coo- solidation of the Borough debt or any caner system which may be for the financial benefit of the ratepayers of the town. With regard to the second question I may say I am dead against illegal trading of any member of the Corporation, and my voice and vota will be used against any member of the Council who may illegally trade with the Clrporstion.". _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor FORSHAW replied ; 6' In answer to your letter of the 17th inst., I wilt certainly support the con- solidation of the Borough debt, if by so doing it will reduce the expenditure of the Through or lessen the interest paid for such debt. I will also undertake to oppose on the 9th November next the election of any Alderman who has illegally traded, either direct ,v or indirec ly, by contract or sub-3ontract with the Corpora. tion."</p>
        <p>_ Councillor BAIRD wrote: " I am not a member of the Finance Committee hut any scheme which will tend to reduce the rates will have 113 y support. I strongly ob- ject to any illegal trading by any member of the Cor- poration." Councillor ROGERS replied " I beg to acknowledge the receipt of your letter and reply to the questions as fol- low. (I) The Borough debt was consolidated by the Warrington Corporation Act, 1899. (2) I will oppose any member of the Council or Alderman illegally trad- ing with the Corporation and my actions have always been to support the lowest tender and so to save the town and therefore the pockets of the property ownerri" Ni replies were received from Councillors S. Davies, D. S. Duncan and L. Ellison. NO TRADING—DIRECT OR INDIRECT.</p>
        <p>Th 9 CHAIRMAN said it was hardly worth while asking the last-aamed gentleman a question, as he had never taken any steps to oppose this improper trading, but on the other hand had opposed the election of Mr Parr as Mayor. Every candidate said he would oppose illegal tracing. Tney did not want them to do that because everybody could do that What they wanted tha Coon- cillors to do was to oppose trading, direct or indirect— (applause) —and if any were prepared ta do that they would be glad if they would Eay so. (Applause). He suggested that the following resolution be passed : " Th:t steps be taken for the contesting of every seat falling vacant on tnelst November, unless the retiring Councillor will undertake to oppose any trading, either direct or indirect by menabers of the Council whether illegal or not.,' Mr Gouom moved and Mr Joao; PEMBERTON seconded the adoption of the resolution, which was unanimously agreed to.</p>
        <p>RR. FLEMING AND TIIE CONSOLIDATION OF TUE DEBT.</p>
        <p>Mr E L FLEMING stated that when some years ago he was agitating about the assessment of the Borough the Rev John Yonge wrote him that the political parties seemed to let the Beard of Guardians election pass over as quietle as possible. This was the first indicetion to him that the town was disfranchised and that the rate- payers did not get a voice in the matter. An indepen- dent effort was made and Dr McCarthy was proposed as a candidate. Their candidate was beaten, but the agitation led to the reassessment of the borough and as a result the authorities got about £25,000 a year more to play with. (Laughter.) Alderman Shaw Green said in 1894 that they could not have a municipal balance sheet. Nevertheless in 1897 he produced one. (Laughter.) Ia 1894, Alderman Green said the town was properly assessed. Nevertheless the town was reaseessed and an increaeo of 50 per cent tnaere, the rateable value of the town being raised from £150,000 to £238,000. Quite recently Alderman Green in answer to a question said they could not havel a profit and loss account. The speaker proceeded to show how it was possible for a profit and lose account to be produced and drew one up roughly. Proceeding to refer to the consolidation of the debt, he stated that Warringtun was paying £30,000 a year in interest on loans. Three per cent. was the amount paid by other towns who had consolidated their debts and issued stock. Nottingham three per cent. was 102, Wigan stock 93 and Widnes, a town half the size of Warring- ton, was at 97. He conteradel that a seeing of £4,000 per annum in interest alone could be effected by the issue of stock and a etill further saving could be made through the sinking fund. When they had consolidated their loans they could apply to Parliament for as long a term of years as possible to repay the loam. Mr Fleming showed how this had been accom- plished by numerous municipalities. He quoted the case of Reading where the Corporation applied to Perlia- meat for power te pay back her existing loans and Parliament gave them 70 years to do so. He had been told that they could not do this in Warringten. His c -In- tention was that if Parliament had granted power to Reading to pay off her loans in 70 years, Parliament could not logically refuse Warrington the same. (Hear, hear.) Reading wee a small place compered with Warrington. Bradford recently obtained 103 years in which to repay the amount borrowed for the water 1 works. Here in Warrington they were repaying the loans in 35 years. They were repaeing the loan for the water works extensions at Winwick and Appleton, the street widenings and other improvements in 35 years. During the last few years the rates had increased from Sc 8d to 61 6d in the £ and he understood that a movement was on foot in regard to school accommoda- tion which would probably put another twopence+ on the rates. They would soon have the rates 73 in the £ and what had they to show far it ? Abselutely nothing. They had not even a tramway in the town. He had pleasure in ErlYiring the fellewine, resolut]ou :—" That in the cninioe of this ineetine it i 3 advisable for the Finance Committee to take steps for the early =sonde- tion of the Borough Debt." Mr NIGHTINGALE seconded and the resolution was carried unanimously. PVIBERTON'S CANDIDATURE.</p>
        <p>The Crtareqe; id they had one can-Carta who was going for their ticket respecting trading be members of the Corporation and that candidate was Mr Pemberton. If they put him into the Council he would watch what was going on and would be as good as any watch dog, and would not be afraid to speak. (Laughter.) M. JOHN PEMBERTON addressed the meeting at c n- siderable length. He stated that he was a Confisrvatove in politics. He was in favour of the coasolidationi of the debt because he believed the ratepayers of the pre- sent generation were being called upon to bear more than their fair share. Why should the ratepayers be called upon to pay for the new police court within 35 years, when in the ordinary course of things the build- ings would last 200 years ? The same applied to the technical schools and the hospitals. By consolidating the debt they would get a much longer period within which to repay the debt. He sag irefavour of no con- tracting either directly or indirectly by members of the Council, as most of them knew. (Laughter.) How any man Could serve two masters he did not know. Mr Pemberton proceeded to explain the steps he took in his endeavour ,to examine the orders of payment and the vouchers of the Warrington Corporation. He was allowed only one half hour to examine the vouchers, and a hewas h Afterwards he asked permission to see the minutes of the Committee, but in place of the minutes he was resolutionten ptorlodbitbhiattintr4 the front F ont irn ining the vouchers. inanne Committee had passed shown a printed epitome of the minutes of the Town Council. There were actually Town Councillors who attended the Council meeting and passed minutes they had never seen and never heard of. It was a fact. (Laughter.) Councillor PEACOCK It would be as well to give narnes.and not to class all alike. Mr PEMBERTON then referred to the standing orders of the Council which enabie the Criairman of each com- mittee to exclude from the printed minutes any resolu- tion he thought fit. Could they conceive for a moment a worse state of things than that. B 7 this means the Ant of Parliament authorising any member to see the minutes, was evaded. It showed the necessity of hay- ing town council/ors who would not be a party to any under-handed business. Mr Pemberton procoedel to remark that &amp;lost° was stifled in the Town Council. All The unpleasant things were now said at the meeting of the General Purposes Committee. This committee now consisted of all the members of the Town Council and although the law gave the nublic the right to ho present at the discussion in the Town Council, the General Purposes Committee was practically made into the Town Council. Any member who had a grievance thrashed it out at the melting of the General Purposes Committee when no reporters were present. At the Local Government Board inquiry the Mayor charged him (Mr Pemberton) with making wholesale charges of corruption against the whole Corporation from top to bottom, That was untrue and unfair and was trying to I put every official and member of the Corporation against him personally. (Hear, hear.) His attack was against those who were doing wrong. It was for the members of the Council who did not participate in con- tracting or slit-contracting with the Corporation to tackle the question. His opponent (Councillor Ellison) was a very decent man. He had not a word to say against him but if he could not raise his voice against this trading, he ought to be the very first to corns out. (Hear, hear.) Referring to the report of the committee of irwesti es- ton into the charges against certain Aldeamen, the</p>
        <p>speaker contended that the committee did not find a verdict. The committee simply laid the facts before the public. The Council Chamber received tbe explanation. What did they do then? They passed on to the next business. (Laughter.) It was for the ratepayers to say whether they were going to allow this to go on. The CHAIRMAN said he would like those who were willing to form a committee to carry out the resolution which had been passed to stay behind and give' them their assistance. The nominations must be in by five o'clock the following day. Alderman HUTCHINSON said he would be very pleased if they would allow Alderman Shaw Green, who was the chairman of the Finance Committee to refer to the subject of the consolidation of the borough debt, A VOICE : Lot him do it in the Town Council. Another VOICE : Be fair. Th CHAIRMAN : We wish to be fair. We have called 1 this meeting as a meting of those who are favourable to certain obj.iicts. If Alderman Shaw Green is in favour of the consolidation of the Borough debt we shall be pleseed to hear him. We wilt answer any question he may like o pat. If he wants a closeting to take up the question he must call one of his own. We are not going to have our meeting spoiled by speeches in opposition, when we have stated that we are here as those who are favour. able to the consolidation of the debt. We have not the least objection to 'Alderman Green asking a question. Alder MITCHINSON rose to speak, but</p>
        <p>To CII&amp;IIVILN said he C9Uld not address the meeting as he had voted against the resolutiod with reference to illegal trading. _</p>
        <p>Mr. HUTCH-MON : All I want to say is that I hope that in the tortncomiug election both you and Mr. Fleming wilt come Oct as candidates.</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr Mrsh the Caanuta.N said they were willing Co answer any questions Bent up from the meet- ing. As no questions were asked, the meeting closed with an acknowledgement of the Chairman's services,on the proposition of Mr FLEMING, seconded by Mr NIGHTING- GALE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>HALTON COURT LEET.</head>
        <p>HALTON COURT LEET.</p>
        <p>At the Castle Hotel, Halton, on Saturday afternoon, this ancient and honourable court was held amid much ceremonious pomp and display. There was a full atten- dance of representatives from all over the district and there seems to be as much enthusiasm in the old custom as ever there was. Every township sent its member, but unfortunately, as was the case last year the High Steward of the Manor,Mr R Davies,Warrington was un- able to be present. His place was, however, creditably filled by his partner Mr Kirkconnel. After the customary pronouncement had been made by Mr Joseph Walker, the high bailiff, Mr Kirkconnell said he was pleased to see them toget- her though the pleasure would have been considerably enhanced had Mr Davies been present. He regretted to say that his illness was more serious than anticipated and was causing anxiety even now to his relations and , friends, none of whom especially the latter, would like him to become better more so than the gentlemen who attend Halton Court. Be had frequently attended that Court, as they were aware and with grace and felicity had contributed in no small measure to the happiness of those who had met with him on such occasions. He had a choice fund of anecdotes and happy reminiscences and his powers of entertaining were wonderful. He had always felt that he had been generously supported by those who attended Halton Court Leet. Ele,the speaker, felt he was only echoing their wishes when he expressed the hope that he would be with them on the next occa- sion. At the same time they ought not to be tad expect- ing, remembering that Mr. Davies was 75 years of age. He would have been very pleased to have been with them, but as with other members the spirit was willing but the flesh was weak. If everything passed off favourably it would cause him a great deal of pleasure. He would like to refer to another matter before proceed- ing with the affairs of the Court. That was the first Court which had taken place since the death of Her illustrious Majesty Queen Victoria. It was their mourn- ful duty to pass some slight contribution of respect to her memory. By her example she contributed in no small measure, coupled with her influence, to the peace and prosperityof the country. He had but imperfectly expressed himself, but they must take the will for the deed.</p>
        <p>- The High Bailiff then announced r—All manner of persons who hold suit and service to this Court Lest and Court Baron of His Most Gracious Majssty King Edward VII as Duke of Lancaster, Baron of Halton and Paramount Lord of this Manor draw near and give us attendance and answer to your names." The following gentlemen gave notice of their presence :—Messre J E Bate, J Edison, S Stokes, S Kinsey, J Clayton, E Pick- stock, T Wright, J S Jeacock, J Manning, W Ashley, T Potter, J Newport. C Percival, T Horton, E Slater, J H Horton, T J Weir, J Wright, H H Wright, W A Allanson, Joseph Walker (Hall Wood). These gentlemen constituted the jury, and together with the foram an, Mr T Wright, re-elected on the pro- position of Messrs Jeacock and Bate, were solemnly sworn in.</p>
        <p>_ Oa the proposition of Mr Ellison, Messrs J Whitlow and T J Weir were elected Burlevnian„ The constables Messrs John Newport and T Potter were re-elected en the proposition of Messrs Wright and Jeacock.</p>
        <p>- The Court was then addressed be Mr Kirkeonnel. In a most interesting speech he said that the Halton Court was one of very groat antiquity tne first record of it being in the year 1347 upwards of 550 years ago which was in the 21st year of the reign of Edward 111. One of the gentlemen of the press asked him at their last meeting whether he was not mistaken in saying that the first record was in 1347, Mr Davies on a previous occasion having given an earlier date. He had found out that be (the speaker) was really correct but there 'was presumptive evidence that the Court probably existed a few years earlier. The Rolls of the Manor, each of which consisted of about a dozen sheets of parchment written on both sides, and rolled, not folded up—whence °abed "Rolls "—were formerly kept in the oak chest in that room and had been only recently re- move Ito the Record office, London. The rolls bad been examined and reported ou in the year 1608 by Sir John Savage Knight. His report was said to be in the Duchy office. In it Sir John certified that the honour of Halton with its dependent manors of Runcorn, Moore, Over, Whitley, Hegshall and Widnes were holden of his Majesty by copy of Court Rolls. In the year 1385 Jaen del Wade was the prepositas or president, and William de Rornby Recorder under John of Gaunt. In former days a seneschal was appointed whose duties would probably have been the dispensation of justice and superintendence of feasts and ceremonies. It might be of interest t explain how the title of the Duke of Lancaster became merged in the larger title of King. Edward 111 had four sous, the Black Prince William, who died in infancy, Lionel who was created first Duke of Clarence and John of Gaunt. The Black Prince was the hero of the well known battles of Crecy and Poictiera and died comparatively young having suffered great privations through being frequently engaged in war. He was interred at Canterbury Cathedral and he, the speaker, had seen his helmet, thield. gauntlets and sum- coat embroidered with the arms of England and France hanging above his tomb: The Duke of Clarence. the title of the third son of Edward 111. bad always been a favourite royal title but was at the present time extinct the eldest son of the present King, who bore the title, dying some ten years ago. The fourth son of Edward was John of Gaunt, Duke of Lancaster, and in that capacity, as they had every reason to believe, WAS the first Lord Paramount of that Manor. His name was not so well known in history as that of his brother.the Black Prince As a nation, however, they were indebted to him for one thing in particular. In a great measure it was dun to his influence thst the bible,one of the:founda- tions of success of the English speaking race, was trans- latechinto the English language, It was not the first translation nor the authorized one made in the reign of James I. Tne facts of the translation in John of Gaunt'a rime were as follews : John Wycliffe, a priest, trans- lated the bible into English but it was disapproved by the Bishops, a bill being brought into the House of Lords to suppress it. It was defeated yrincipally by John of Gaunt who concluded an oration by saying. "We will not be the dregs of allseeing that'other nations have the law of GA, which is the law of faith, written in their: own language." John of IGaunt'a scu, Henry Relinbreke, ultimately ascended the throne and was Duke of Lancaster through his father which accounts for the title of King, and Duke of Lancaster being vested in one and the same person causing the lesser title of Duke to be merged into the larger one of King. The company afterwards partook of an excellent , repast nerved up in capital style by Mr Joseph Walker, ' landlord of the hotel.</p>
        <p>The Chairman proposed the health of the king, which was loyally respond° to.</p>
        <p>ma Chairman also proposed the Bishop and Clergy of all denominations in the parish. They were always anxious for their welfare in both temporal and spiritual matters. He coupled with the toast the name of Mr• Jeacock.</p>
        <p>Mr. Jeacock said be did not know why his name vras coupled with the toast, unless it was owing to the fact that he was a schoolmaster. It was said that the school- master was the right hand man of the clergyman, and he had little doubt that that was quite true. Th 3 Vice-Chairman proposed in a neat and apt speech the heolth of the Deputy High Steward, and the toast received. musical honours.</p>
        <p>The Chairman, in reply, declared his interest in the Court and its supporters, observing that he made more friends each year he attended. Mr. John Ellison gave the health of the Foreman, and expressed the hope that while he lived no other gentleman would occupy the position. (Applause). The toast was enthusiastically honoured, and Mr. Wright made an appropriate acknowledgment. Song,' and pianoforte selections were rendercd hy the Misses Walker, Master Walker, Mr J W Wright (Dares - bury) and others. The GLEN-SPEY DisTmEnv, Situate in the finest Whiskv-producing District of Scotland, is the property of W. &amp;A. Gllhey. This Whisky is made enarely from home-grown Barley and is kept absolutely unblended In his Majesty's Bonded Warehouses to mature, and sold by W. &amp; A. Gilbers agents in _every town at 3s. 6d per bottle,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The South Lancashires.</p>
        <p>The following was included in tte casualty list issued on Monday :—At Pinetown, Octotm 20,h, Lt South Lancathire Regirnett.-5537 Private F Fallon, danget- ously ill, cause not stated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Presentation to the 3rd South Lancashire Regiment.</head>
        <p>Presentation to the 3rd South Lancashire Regiment.</p>
        <p>Speech by Colonel Ireland Blackburne.</p>
        <p>Warrington's appreciation of the loyalty of the members of the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire(Wa rington Militia) Regiment, who recently returned home from South Africa after serving their country at the seat of war for over twelve months, found adequate expression on Wednes- day afternoon, when Colonel Ireland Black- burn° was the recipient on behalf of the battalion, of a handsome silver loving cup sub- scribed for by the Mayor and townspeople. The presentation was made by the Mayor (Alderman Roberts), the ceremony taking place in the Council Chamber at the Town Hall. The cup is of handsome design and bears the following inscrip- tion :—" To Colonel R Ireland Blackburne, and the officers, non-commissioned offizers, and men of the 3rd Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, from the Mayor and townspeople of Warrington, in warm appreciation of their valour and patriotism in volunteering for and serving their country in the war in South Africa, 1899-1901, H Roberts,Mavor." The cup is supplied by the Goldsmiths' and Silver- smiths' Company, London. At the meeting on Wednesday Colonel Ireland Blackburne was accom- panied by Captain N. if. Eckersley, Captain .3 L Vaughan, Captain R A Grey. and Lieutenant J Morrell,and the following members of the Warring- ton Town Council were present :—The Mayor, Alderman Hutchinson, Councillors, Smethurst, Davies, W Roberts, Rogers, Duncan, Malkin, James Evans, and Sampson.</p>
        <p>In making the presentation, the Mayor,who wore his chain of office, said it was with feelinge of very sincere pleasure that he asked Colonel Ireland Blackburne's and the officers' acceptance, on behalf of the people of War- riegtoo, of that slight memento of the present war. Ha had had the opportunity on several previous occasions of imperfectly voicing the sentiments of the people of Warrington in regard to the action of the battalion in coming forward at a time in the history of the Empire of such great necessity, and volunteering for active service, and he could assure them that the feelings he had given expres- sion to on previous occasions still remained. All that had passed had gone to prove that although there bad only been two occasions on which the battalion had been called upon, they all felt this, that if—which God forbid—there was ever to be another occasion when the interests of the country required the assistance of its gallant men, that amongst the very first to come forward to give that assistance would be the members of the old 4th Royal Lancashire Militia. • Warrington people felt they should like to have done a peat deal mere for the battalion,but they hoped the recipients would not—in fact they knew they would not—accept tbp gift merely on account of its intrinsic value but only and entirely as an expression of the feel- ing of the town in regard to their action. They hoped the Colonel and his fellow officers would for many years have the opportunity of using the cup on occasions of pleasure and so forth, and that when they did so they would not forget the good feeling which existed between them aud the people of the old town with which the battalion was associated. (Applause.) 1,41 n accepting the gift, Colonel Ireland-Blackberne, said he could assure them that the cup would always be placed on the mess table of the 'battalion. so that it would show to generations to come that the Warring- ton people did appreciate their own regiment when they volunteered to go on active service In this war. He said their own regiment because it was the old 4th Royal Lancashire Militia which was formed in 1853 by his (the speaker's) father long before the barracks were built, and was really the old Warrington regiment and would always be associated with the town. Shortly after it was formed the Crimean War and Indian Mutiny took place, and the regiment was the first during the Crimean War to volun- teer for active service. They held amongst their records the thanks of both Houses of Parliament for doing so. He should also like to take the opportunity of thanking all those people who so kindly subscribed to send out comforts to the mega. He could assure them that the gifts were much apiriciated and when the boxes arrived —some he regretted to say did nct arrive, but the majority did, they were distributed as fairly as possible throughout the bottalion. In conclusion, he thanked them on behalf of the regiment for that magnificent present. (Applause). The 'proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-10-25_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WARRINGTON;</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON;</p>
        <p>Personal.</p>
        <p>Miss Gertrude Picka-v-tin-c-e-,--Who was a student at the Warrington School of Art, recently applied for admission to the Royal College of Art, London. Under the new arrangements admission is only granted to those who submit drawings,and if satis- factory, the candidate is allowed to take the exam- inations, which last six days, in subjects of drawing figure, plant form and design, architecture, and modelling, Miss Pickavance was successful in gaining her admissiontand of all the candidates she was the only lady art student admitted by this examination. _ _</p>
        <p>-On Saturday afternoon Captain 0 R Crosfield, Warrington, inspected the members of the Chester centre of the St John Ambulance Aeeociation.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOE IMMIX TICE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STRELIT.—Advt. The Infirmary Concert.</p>
        <p>The effort; p-ut forth by the members of the Workingmen's Committee to substantially augment the funds of one of Warrington's most deserving Institutions, the Infirmary and Dispensary, by , means of an annual concert, generally meet with the ungrudging support of the public, and this year's concert,which took place in the Parr Hall on Saturday evening, was no exception to the rule in that respect. The spacious hall was completely filled in every part, in fact' some of the seats at the rear of the platform had to be utilized by members of the audience who could not be accommodated elsewhere. Apart from the laudable objects of the concert, the excellent, pro- gramme submitted was an inducement for lovers of music to attend, and the proceedinks from a musi- cal as well as financial standpoint were attended with a full measure of success. The services of Mr T J Lown's orchestra had again been secured, and never have these instrumentalists been , heard to better advantaga. Their opening contribution was a spirited march " Lorraine ' which was rendered in a highly creditable I manner. The audiences were not slow in signify- ing their approval by hearty applause. The other items were given with equally good effect and brought out the full resources of the band. They included a descriptive military fantasia " The emperor's review" whieh was a capital wind up to the programme. Mr T J Down was at his accus- tomed post of conductor, and to him much of the success of the concert is due. The vocalists were Miss Minnie Grimes (soprano), Miss Annie F Sutton, L LC Ist (contralto), Mr Arthur Weber (bass) and the Orpheus glee party. Miss Grimes possesses a very sweet vice and interpreted " The swallows " and " Poor wandering one ' with taste and impression and Miss Sutton gained unstinted applause by her sympathetic rendition of " Kathleen Mavourneen." Mr Arthur Weber is an old favourite with Warring- ton audiences and his , powerful bass voice had ample scope in "The diver," and " The old soldier," Both items were well received especially the latter. and despite the request on the programme that encores should not be demanded, the audience would not be satisfied without the re-appearance of the artiste. In reponse Mr Weber feel- ingly sang "Rocked in the cradle of the deep." The Orpheus Glee party, as usual, acquitted themselves remarkably well, their voices blending splendidly in "Tom, Tom, the piper's son," which 90 delighted their hearers that an encore was demanded. The lighter vein was furnished by Mr Walter Wade, whose songs and patter were ex- tremely good. The audience were kept in convul- sions of laughter, and for both his items Mr Wade was vociferously recalled. Praise is due to Mr W H Paytcn, Al us Bac, who played the accompani- ments with his customary taste and skill.</p>
        <p>At a masa meeting of students of St Andrews University, a letter was read from Mr Andrew Carnegie, accepting the unanimous invitation of the students to allow himself to bo nominated for the Lord Rectorship of the University.</p>
