        <p>ST BARN ABAS'S -.SALE OF WORK. The Rector and the School Accommodation Crisis. The annual sale of wort in connection with St Barnabas' Church Wag held in the Assembly room at the Conservative Club on Wednesday and Thursday last eel:. As the day schools are at present much in need of nancial support, it had been decided to devete the pro- ceeds of the sale to the Resistance of these institutions. No pains had been spared to make the room as bright and attractive as possible. The stalls were effectively arranged, most of them being heavily laden with a variety of useful and fancy articles. At another stall an abundance or refreshments was temptingly displayed, whilst doss by was a flower stall where one could obtain choice blooms. The following is a list of the stall holders ;—Refresh- meet stall, Mesdames Copsage. R. Proud, T. Proud, Taylor, and Atkinson, Misses Johnson, and Kellett ; work stall No. 1, Mesdames Trevett and Bracogirdlo.and Miss Whitehouse ; work stall No 2, Mesdames Looker. Howard and Jones, and Miss E Whitehouse ; doll stall, MiSSPA Fry and J Bail ; Chinese and Japanese art stall, Mrs Iddon, Misses F MWs and E Snelson ; pound stall, Misses Gerrard and A Snel,on ; day school teachers' stall, Misses Lansdale, Tcwning, F Pickering, and Parker; infants' stall, Misses Weathlburo, S Jones, A Jones, M Evans, and Mrs Airey ; fruit tall, Mesdames W Proud and R T Smith, and Miss S White- house. , . The VICAR said that by means 0 the male of work they</p>
        <p>A GOOD FAMILY MEDICINE CHEST vv-IrH a prudent 'use, has saved many a life, and yet we think the idea might be improved upon and reduced to a more simple foria Take some good Compound such lc COCKLeJmS ANTIBILIOUS PILLS, and we find that the desired end may be obtained without scales and weights, or little mysterious compartments, and enchanted bottles with crystal stoppers. Others might be used, but COCKLE'S PILLS, • as tested by many thousands of persons, and found to answer their purposes so well, may be set down as the best. —Observer. t 0 1 C . LE s FREE c'ROM MERCURY , ANT/B/Li0,11.,7.:;„ OF PURL VEGETABLE INGREDIENTS. PILLS. For BILE, LIVER, ACIDITY, INDIGESTION, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>wanted ta raise £lOO for various purposes connected with the schools. In proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs Lyon for opening the sale, the Rector remarked that the present was a very critical time indeed for Church schools. He could not say bow deeply and intensely he himself felt in the matter. Ho knew the secular instruction in Board schools was of the bcst, and he should be quite willing to band over the schools to the Board if it were not for the religious side of the queetion He felt the immense importance of preserving their schools if possible, on account of the religions instru- tion Riven there on five days in the week. It was not simply what might be called religious instruction that he valued so much—that was to say an open Bible— because many who would teach an open Bible would not teach as the churchman taught. At church schools they taught the children who were old enough to under- stand, the value and the blessing of Holy Baptism. They also taught, as no Nonconformist of course would teach, the value and importance of confirmation. That was not controversial doctrine,it was the Bible teaching No Nonconformist could teach that as a Churchman ould teach it because the Churchmen knew and under- toed his Bible. On Thursday the sale was formally opened by the Vicar: The proceeds for the two days amounted to £BB 3s; Bd. The sum realized by the sale of tickets is not included in the above amount. WARRiNGTON. The Sunday School Teachers' Association. The quarterly meeting of the Sunday School Teachers' Association was held in St Barnabas' Schoolroom, on Monday evening. Tea was served at seven o'clock, about fifty sitting-down., Even- song was afterwards held,in the church, whe'n there was a good congregation. The Vicar (Rev J H G Hollingworth) delivered an address on " The work of the Sunday School teacher." He spoke of the self-denial and patience shown by those who looked after the children inltheir Sunday schools, and urged his hearers to be earnest about their work. He prayed that God might fit them for their work of teaching the young.</p>
